window.HRKB_INDEX=[{"t": "0.1 How each chapter is organised", "c": "Start Here", "u": "book/00-start-here.html#s-0-1", "x": "In short Sections are self-contained, so you can study in any order. The side panel summarises each section and lists the terms to recall. Recall Side panel: In short + Recall Correction and India notes Interview questions at the end Notes: cue column + fold line Every chapter opens with a statement of what it covers and why it matters in interviews. Each numbered section then follows the same pattern: a side panel with a short summary and the key terms to recall, followed by the explanation (definition, underlying principle, how it works, examples, and limitations or best practice). Tables are used for comparisons, formulas and worked examples. Two kinds of note appear in the text. Correction marks a point where the compendium is wrong or outdated. India gives Indian law, practice or data. Each chapter ends with interview questions answered in full sentences. They are model reasoning, not scripts: answer in your own words. Using the Notes. Each section of a Notes chapter has the section title and recall terms in the left column and condensed notes on the right. Cover the right side and try to explain each term; fold the page at the dashed line beneath a question to test yourself before reading the answer."}, {"t": "0.2 Chapters and priorities", "c": "Start Here", "u": "book/00-start-here.html#s-0-2", "x": "In short Priorities reflect how often topics come up in HR interviews at Indian campuses. Recall Highest: 2, 3, 5, 10, 11, 12 Your depth areas: 2, 5, 6 Chapter Priority Why 2 Compensation and Benefits (three parts) Highest Pay structure, CTC, the new wage definition and job evaluation are asked by almost every employer 3 Performance Management Highest Bell curve, KPIs and OKRs, calibration and rating design are panel favourites 5 Engagement, Well-being, EX and EVP Highest Attrition, surveys and EVP scenarios 10 Industrial Relations and the IR Code Highest for manufacturing roles Unions, discipline, strikes, retrenchment 11 Labour Codes II Highest The Codes have applied since November 2025; panels test whether you have kept up 12 Group Discussions Highest for GD rounds Fifteen topics with facts and balanced positions 1 Talent Acquisition High Selection methods, offer drop-outs, AI in hiring 6 Org Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning High Structures, spans, job architecture, manpower planning 4 Learning and Development High Training needs, evaluation, ROI 8 Change Management High Models with practical scenarios, including unionised plants 9 HR Analytics, HR Tech and AI High Attrition analytics, AI risks, the DPDP Act 7 Organisational Behaviour and Culture Supporting Theories that strengthen answers in every other chapter"}, {"t": "0.3 How HR interviews test you", "c": "Start Here", "u": "book/00-start-here.html#s-0-3", "x": "In short Prepare four types of question for each topic and use a small set of answer structures. Recall Concept, compare, apply, opinion Define → why → example → limitation Clarify → diagnose → act → measure Position → reasons → counter → middle path Business language Question type Example What is being tested How to answer Concept “What is compa-ratio?” Accurate fundamentals Define it, give the formula or principle, a numerical example and its use Comparison “OKR versus KPI?” “Lay-off versus retrenchment?” Ability to distinguish related ideas Compare on three or four dimensions: purpose, time frame, who decides, example Application “Attrition in your plant is 30%. What would you do?” Judgement and structure State an assumption, diagnose with data, act on the main causes, measure the result Opinion “Should India legislate a right to disconnect?” Awareness, balance and maturity Take a position, give two reasons with evidence, acknowledge the strongest counter-argument, end with a practical middle path Three structures cover most questions. For concepts: definition, why it matters, example, limitation ; the limitation is what distinguishes an understood answer from a memorised one. For scenarios: clarify, diagnose, act, measure . For opinions: position, reasons, counter-argument, balanced recommendation . Use business language. Connect HR answers to cost, productivity, risk, customers or growth. “This would reduce regretted attrition among process engineers, each of whom costs several months of salary to replace” is stronger than “this would improve morale”."}, {"t": "0.4 India HR in 2025–26: what panels expect you to know", "c": "Start Here", "u": "book/00-start-here.html#s-0-4", "x": "In short Ten developments recur across chapters. Know them precisely. Recall Labour Codes in force; central rules Wage definition, 50% rule FTE gratuity 300-worker threshold; reskilling fund Strike notice Gig workers; e-Shram EPF ceiling ₹25,000 DPDP Rules Women at night AI restructuring The four Labour Codes (Wages 2019; Industrial Relations, Social Security, and Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions 2020) came into force on 21 November 2025 , replacing 29 central laws. Final central rules were notified on 8 May 2026 ; states are notifying their own rules at different speeds (Chapters 10 and 11). “Wages” now has one definition: basic pay, dearness allowance and retaining allowance. If excluded allowances exceed 50% of total remuneration , the excess counts as wages for PF, gratuity and bonus, which led many companies to restructure salaries (Chapter 2). Fixed-term employees receive the same pay and benefits as permanent staff and become eligible for gratuity after one year (Chapters 2, 10, 11). Government permission for lay-off, retrenchment and closure is now required only at 300 or more workers (previously 100), standing orders apply at 300, and a reskilling fund of 15 days’ wages is paid for each retrenched worker (Chapter 10). Strike and lockout notice rules now apply to all industrial establishments, and mass casual leave by half or more of the workers counts as a strike (Chapter 10). Gig and platform workers are recognised for social security for the first time, funded by aggregator contributions; aggregators must register workers on the e-Shram portal, and benefits require 90 days with one aggregator or 120 days across several (Chapter 11). The EPF wage ceiling rose from ₹15,000 to ₹25,000 a month from 17 September 2026 , the first change since 2014"}, {"t": "0.6 Main corrections to the compendium", "c": "Start Here", "u": "book/00-start-here.html#s-0-6", "x": "In short The most important errors and outdated points, with the chapter where each is corrected. Topic Compendium Correct position Chapter Labour Codes status Described as of early 2026 In force since 21 November 2025; final central rules 8 May 2026; EPF ceiling raised September 2026 11 DPDP and employee data Consent required for employee data; no breach deadline Employment purposes are a legitimate use without consent; detailed breach report within 72 hours 9 “Industry” Charitable organisations may qualify The IR Code excludes charitable, social and philanthropic institutions, sovereign functions and domestic service 10 Compa-ratio Salary versus market average Salary versus the midpoint of the employee’s pay range 2 Unilever hiring case 7% faster; $1 million saved About four months reduced to four weeks; about £1 million a year (company-reported) 1 Working hours under the OSH Code “Maximum 8 hours a day” 8-hour normal day for daily-wage workers; 48-hour week; overtime capped at 144 hours a quarter; states may allow longer days 11 9-box grid origin Developed by McKinsey for people Adapted from the GE–McKinsey portfolio matrix 3 Maslow Each level must be satisfied before the next No evidence for a strict sequence 7 70-20-10 A measured breakdown A heuristic from executives’ recollections 4 Early attrition formula Leavers ÷ employees at start Leavers ÷ hires in the cohort 3 MBTI Presented without caveats Weak reliability; unsuitable for selection 7"}, {"t": "0.7 A two-week plan", "c": "Start Here", "u": "book/00-start-here.html#s-0-7", "x": "In short Read the book chapter, then revise from the notes and practise aloud. Days Read (Book) Then practise 1–2 2 Compensation and Benefits Explain job evaluation → grades → pay ranges in 90 seconds; calculate gratuity, PF and compa-ratio 3 6 Org Design and Job Architecture Explain how job architecture connects to grading and pay 4–5 11 Labour Codes II, 10 Industrial Relations Self-test from the Notes on thresholds and timelines 6 3 Performance Management Mock: “Should we drop the bell curve?” 7 5 Engagement and EVP Mock: attrition and EVP scenarios 8 1 Talent Acquisition, 4 Learning and Development Metrics and evaluation drills 9 8 Change Management, 9 Analytics and AI Scenario drills 10 7 OB and Culture Apply one theory to each earlier scenario 11–14 12 Group Discussions; Notes for every chapter Two GDs and one mock interview a day; revise from the Notes Principal sources used across the book are listed at the end of each chapter. Statutory points were checked against the Codes and rules, government notifications (PIB, EPFO, MeitY) and law-firm and Big Four summaries of the final central rules. State rules differ and continue to be notified: check the relevant state before advising on any real case."}, {"t": "1.1 Recruitment, selection and talent acquisition", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-1", "x": "In short Recruitment attracts candidates, selection chooses among them, and talent acquisition is the long-term system that plans, attracts, selects and integrates the talent the business needs. Recall Recruitment vs selection vs TA ASA theory (Schneider, 1987) Person–job fit, person–organisation fit “Culture fit” risk Recruitment is the process of identifying and attracting a pool of suitable candidates for vacancies. Selection is the process of assessing candidates and choosing the one most likely to succeed. Talent acquisition (TA) is the broader, longer-term function that connects hiring to workforce planning: it includes employer branding, talent pipelines, sourcing strategy, selection, offer management, onboarding and the data to improve all of these. Recruitment Selection Talent acquisition Question answered How do we get enough suitable applicants? Who is most likely to succeed? How do we ensure the right talent is available when the business needs it? Time horizon Current vacancies Current vacancies One to three years Main activities Advertising, sourcing, referrals, agencies, campus Tests, interviews, assessment centres, background checks Workforce planning, employer brand, pipelines, internal mobility, analytics Output Applicant pool Hiring decision Capability ready when required Fit and the ASA framework Selection looks at two kinds of fit. Person–job fit is the match between a person’s knowledge, skills and abilities and the requirements of the job. Person–organisation fit is the match between a person’s values and preferences and the organisation’s culture. Benjamin Schneider’s attraction–selection–attrition (ASA) framework (1987) explains how organisations become more homogeneous over time. People are attracted to organisations whose values resemble their"}, {"t": "1.2 The hiring process", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-2", "x": "In short Hiring runs from requisition to onboarding. Most problems come from a poor intake discussion at the start, slow decisions in the middle, and neglect between offer and joining. Recall Requisition and intake JD and specification Sourcing → screening → assessment → decision Offer → pre-boarding → onboarding Typical failures at each stage Stage What good practice looks like Common failure Requisition and approval Vacancy approved against the workforce plan and budget Roles opened without budget, then frozen mid-process Intake meeting Recruiter and hiring manager agree the purpose of the role, essential and desirable requirements, compensation range, interview panel, timeline and selection criteria Vague brief, leading to wrong profiles and repeated restarts Job description and specification Based on job analysis (Chapter 6); describes outcomes, not just tasks; lists only genuine requirements Copied JDs with long lists of “requirements” that discourage capable applicants, particularly women, who research suggests apply when they meet more of the listed criteria Sourcing Mix of internal and external channels chosen for the role (Section 1.3) Over-reliance on a single portal; internal candidates ignored Screening Clear knock-out criteria, applicant tracking system (ATS) filters reviewed by people, short screening calls Keyword filters reject good candidates with non-standard CVs Assessment Validated tests, structured interviews, work samples or assessment centres (Section 1.4) Unstructured conversations; each interviewer asks different questions Decision Independent ratings compiled before discussion; decision against agreed criteria Loudest panel member decides; “gut feel” Offer Prompt, competitive, clearly explained offer (fixed, variable, benefits, joining date) Sl"}, {"t": "1.3 Sourcing channels", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-3", "x": "In short Each channel has different cost, speed, quality and diversity effects. Referrals usually give the best quality and retention; internal hiring is the most underused channel. Recall Internal: IJP, mobility, referrals, alumni External: portals, careers site, campus, agencies, search, social, ITIs, staffing Passive candidates Agency fees: ~8.33–20% Channel Strengths Limitations Best for Internal job postings and mobility Known performance; faster ramp-up; signals growth to employees; lower cost Backfill needed; can create internal politics Most roles, especially mid-level Employee referrals Higher quality and retention on average; low cost (referral bonus) Replicates the existing workforce (ASA), reducing diversity; risk of favouritism Professional and technical roles Alumni and rehires (“boomerangs”) Known quality and culture fit Limited pool Experienced roles Job portals (Naukri, LinkedIn, foundit, Indeed; Apna and WorkIndia for frontline roles) Large reach High volume of unsuitable applications Most lateral hiring Company careers site Attracts candidates already interested in the employer Depends on brand strength All roles Campus hiring Pipeline of trainable talent; employer-brand building Long lead time; training investment Entry-level and management trainees Recruitment agencies Speed and reach for hard-to-fill roles Fees, commonly 8.33–12.5% of annual CTC for mid-level lateral roles in India Specialist and urgent roles Executive search Discreet access to senior passive candidates Fees often 20–33% of first-year CTC, usually retained Senior leadership Staffing firms Speed and flexibility for large volumes Contract-labour compliance (Chapter 11) Frontline and temporary volume roles ITIs, polytechnics and apprenticeships Skilled technicians; low-cost pipeline T"}, {"t": "1.4 Selection methods: what predicts performance", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-4", "x": "In short Good selection methods are reliable (consistent) and valid (predict performance). Structured interviews, job-knowledge tests and work samples have the strongest evidence; unstructured interviews and years of experience predict little. Recall Reliability: test–retest, inter-rater Validity: content, criterion, construct Sackett et al. (2022) rankings Structured interview design STAR; behavioural vs situational Assessment centres Two concepts from first principles Reliability is the consistency of a measure. A reliable test gives similar results when repeated ( test–retest reliability ), and a reliable interview produces similar ratings from different interviewers ( inter-rater reliability ). An unreliable method cannot be valid. Validity is the extent to which a method measures what it claims and predicts what matters. Content validity : the method samples the actual content of the job (a typing test for a typist). Criterion validity : scores correlate with a performance measure, either later ( predictive ) or at the same time for current employees ( concurrent ). Construct validity : the method measures the underlying trait it intends to measure, such as conscientiousness. Validity in selection is usually expressed as a correlation coefficient between method scores and job performance. Adverse impact occurs when a method selects members of a group at a substantially lower rate than others. In the United States, the “four-fifths rule” treats a selection rate for one group below 80% of the highest group’s rate as evidence of adverse impact; Indian law has no equivalent test, but the principle is useful for auditing fairness. Evidence on selection methods A 2022 re-analysis by Paul Sackett and colleagues corrected earlier estimates (Schmidt and Hunter, 1998) and pr"}, {"t": "1.5 Fair and lawful selection", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-5", "x": "In short Fair selection reduces bias through structure and audits, and complies with Indian non-discrimination and data-protection law. Recall Interviewer biases Code on Wages: no gender discrimination in recruitment RPwD Act; Transgender Persons Act DPDP Act for candidate data Appointment letters (OSH Code) Common interviewer biases (Chapter 7): first impressions and the halo effect, similarity or affinity bias (same college, region or background), contrast effects between candidates, stereotyping, confirmation bias after the first few minutes, and overconfidence in one’s ability to judge people quickly. Controls are structured interviews, diverse panels, independent scoring, and tracking selection rates by group. Legal requirements in India. Law Requirement for hiring Code on Wages, 2019 No discrimination on the ground of gender, including transgender identity, in recruitment for the same or similar work Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 Non-discrimination; reasonable accommodation, including in the selection process (accessible tests, extra time where appropriate) Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act, 2019 No discrimination in recruitment Maternity provisions Questions about marriage or pregnancy plans invite discrimination claims and should not be asked Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 Candidate data is personal data: collect only what is needed, give notice, obtain consent where required (for example, for background verification), keep it secure, and delete it when no longer needed (Chapter 9) OSH Code, 2020 Appointment letters in the prescribed format are mandatory for every employee"}, {"t": "1.6 Offers and the offer-to-joining gap", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-6", "x": "In short In India, 60–90-day notice periods create a long gap between offer acceptance and joining, during which candidates receive counter-offers. Offer management and pre-boarding are therefore core TA activities. Recall Notice periods 60–90 days Counter-offers, multiple offers, renege Offer-to-join ratio Buyouts; joining bonus with clawback Pre-boarding The problem. Notice periods of 60 to 90 days are normal in Indian corporate employment, and 90 days is common in IT services and consulting. During the notice period, the current employer may make a counter-offer, and other employers may make competing offers. Some candidates accept several offers and join only one. The result is a high offer drop-out or renege rate, which Indian TA teams track as the offer-to-join ratio . What works. Lever How it helps Faster decisions and approvals Reduces the time candidates spend comparing options Accurate, competitive first offer Avoids weak offers that invite counter-offers Clear explanation of the offer Fixed, variable, benefits and expected take-home pay (Chapter 2, Section 2.10) Notice-period buyout Employer pays the candidate’s notice-period dues so they can join earlier, for critical roles Joining bonus with clawback Compensates for forfeited bonus; repayable if the person leaves within 12–24 months Pre-boarding Regular contact from the hiring manager and team, early access to learning content, digital document completion, an assigned buddy Backup candidates Keeping one or two strong finalists warm for critical roles The offer letter and appointment letter. An offer letter states the role, compensation, joining date and conditions (such as background verification). Under the OSH Code, an appointment letter in the prescribed format must be issued to every employee on joining"}, {"t": "1.7 Onboarding", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-7", "x": "In short Onboarding integrates new hires so they become productive and committed. Most organisations handle compliance well and connection poorly. Recall Bauer’s 4 Cs Pre-boarding, induction, integration 30-60-90-day plan Buddy; manager role Early attrition, time to productivity Definition. Onboarding is the process of integrating a new employee into the organisation, its culture and their role, from acceptance of the offer until they are fully productive, often six to twelve months. Talya Bauer’s 4 Cs (SHRM Foundation) describe four levels of onboarding: Level Covers Example Compliance Policies, forms, legal requirements Documentation, POSH and safety training Clarification Understanding of the job and expectations 30-60-90-day goals agreed with the manager Culture Norms and values, formal and informal Leadership sessions; stories of how decisions are made Connection Relationships and networks Buddy, introductions to key stakeholders, team rituals Good practice: pre-boarding before day one; laptop, access and workspace ready on the first day; a manager-led first week; a buddy; a 30-60-90-day plan with early contribution; check-ins at 30, 60 and 90 days; and a short onboarding survey. Measures: early attrition, time to productivity, new-hire engagement and hiring-manager satisfaction."}, {"t": "1.8 Campus and early-career hiring", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-8", "x": "In short Campus hiring builds a pipeline of trainable talent and shapes employer brand. Internships converting to pre-placement offers are the most reliable route. Recall Campus process PPOs from internships Management trainee programmes TCS NQT Fresher onboarding delays (2022–24) Typical Indian campus process: pre-placement talk, online aptitude or domain test, group discussion or case exercise, one or more interviews (often including HR), offer. Many employers rely heavily on summer internships converting into pre-placement offers (PPOs) , because an internship is an extended work sample. Early-career programmes. Management trainee programmes (for example HUL’s Unilever Future Leaders Programme, ITC’s management trainee programme, the Tata Administrative Service) rotate trainees across functions before a first posting. IT services companies hire freshers at scale through national tests such as the TCS National Qualifier Test (NQT) and train them before deployment. India context Between 2022 and 2024 several large IT services firms delayed onboarding freshers who had accepted offers, some for many months, as demand slowed. In 2025, Infosys let go several hundred trainees in Mysuru after they did not clear internal assessments. These episodes are used in discussions on campus offer ethics, workforce planning and the treatment of trainees."}, {"t": "1.9 Volume and frontline hiring", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-9", "x": "In short High-volume frontline hiring is optimised for speed, accessibility and early retention rather than assessment depth. Recall Mobile, vernacular applications Same-day offers Staffing partners 30-day retention Retail, quick commerce, logistics, BPO and delivery roles can require thousands of hires a month with annual attrition above 50%. Effective volume hiring uses mobile-first and local-language applications (including WhatsApp-based chatbots), simple screening on essential criteria, walk-in or same-day interviews and offers, referral drives, and staffing partners for peaks. Key metrics are time-to-hire, show-up rates and retention at 30 and 90 days . Realistic job previews (showing actual shifts, pay and conditions before the offer) reduce early attrition."}, {"t": "1.10 Recruitment metrics", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-10", "x": "In short TA is managed through efficiency metrics (time, cost), effectiveness metrics (quality of hire, retention) and experience metrics (candidate and hiring-manager satisfaction). Recall Time-to-fill vs time-to-hire Cost-per-hire Yield ratios Offer-acceptance, offer-to-join Quality of hire Early attrition Source effectiveness Metric Formula or definition What it shows Time-to-fill Days from requisition approval (or job posting) to offer acceptance Speed of the whole process; for workforce planning. State which start point is used Time-to-hire Days from the candidate entering the process to accepting the offer Recruiter and process efficiency; candidate experience Cost-per-hire (External costs + internal costs) ÷ number of hires, as in the SHRM/ANSI standard Efficiency; compare across channels Selection (yield) ratio Candidates progressing to the next stage ÷ candidates at the current stage Where the funnel leaks Offer-acceptance rate Offers accepted ÷ offers made Competitiveness of offers and employer brand Offer-to-join ratio Candidates who join ÷ offers accepted India-specific drop-out risk Quality of hire Composite of first-year performance, time to productivity, hiring-manager satisfaction and 12-month retention Effectiveness of selection; the most important and least measured metric Early attrition Hires leaving within 90 or 365 days ÷ hires in that cohort Hiring and onboarding quality Source of hire and source quality Share of hires, and their quality and retention, by channel Where to invest Candidate experience Survey at each stage, sometimes as a candidate Net Promoter Score Employer brand and referrals Slate and hire diversity Share of under-represented groups at each stage Where diverse candidates drop out Funnel example (campus HR trainees). 2,000 applica"}, {"t": "1.11 Technology and AI in talent acquisition", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-11", "x": "In short Technology speeds up hiring and improves data, but AI screening can reproduce bias and must be validated, audited and governed. Recall ATS, CRM, assessment platforms Chatbots, GenAI Amazon tool scrapped (2018) HireVue dropped facial analysis (2021) NYC Local Law 144 Human in the loop Technology Function Risk Applicant tracking system (ATS) Manages requisitions, applications, workflow and data Over-rigid keyword filtering Candidate relationship management (CRM) Maintains talent pools and pipelines Data-protection obligations for stored candidates Online assessments Aptitude, coding, situational judgement, game-based tests Validity and accessibility must be checked Chatbots Answer questions, screen basic criteria, schedule interviews (for example, McDonald’s “Olivia” for hourly roles) Impersonal if overused AI screening and matching Ranks CVs or profiles against role requirements Learns historic bias Generative AI Drafts JDs, outreach and interview guides; summarises interviews Accuracy; candidates also use GenAI, reducing the signal from CVs and take-home tasks Cautionary cases. Amazon abandoned an experimental CV-screening tool in 2018 after finding it penalised CVs that included the word “women’s“, because it had learned from a decade of male-dominated hiring data. HireVue stopped using facial analysis in its video assessments in 2021 after criticism. New York City’s Local Law 144 (in force 2023) requires independent bias audits of automated employment decision tools. Governance principles: validate tools against actual job performance; audit outcomes by gender, age, region and language; keep a human responsible for rejection decisions; explain to candidates how they are assessed; and comply with the DPDP Act."}, {"t": "1.12 Case: Unilever’s graduate hiring", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-12", "x": "In short Unilever replaced manual CV screening for graduate roles with online games, AI-scored video interviews and an assessment day, reporting faster hiring, cost savings and more diverse hires. Recall ~250,000 applicants, ~800 roles Pymetrics → HireVue → Discovery Centre ~4 months → ~4 weeks ~£1 million a year Vendor-reported results Problem. Unilever’s Future Leaders Programme received about 250,000 applications a year for about 800 places, and the manual process took four to six months. Approach (from 2016). Candidates applied with their LinkedIn profiles; played Pymetrics neuroscience-based games measuring traits such as risk appetite and attention; completed a HireVue video interview scored with AI; and the shortlisted candidates attended a one-day Discovery Centre assessed by Unilever staff. Screening shifted from experience and university to potential, and every applicant received feedback. Reported results. Time-to-hire fell from about four months to about four weeks; recruiter screening time fell by about 75%; savings of about £1 million a year were reported; the share of hires from a wider range of universities rose; and candidate satisfaction was high. Correction The compendium (p. 13) gives “7% reduction in recruitment time” and “$1 million saved in one year”. The widely reported figures are a reduction from about four months to about four weeks, and savings of about £1 million a year. These figures come largely from Unilever and its vendors, so describe them as “reported”; HireVue’s later removal of facial analysis (2021) is a useful balancing point."}, {"t": "1.13 Policy context in India", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-13", "x": "In short Government schemes subsidise formal hiring and apprenticeships, and verification and fraud risks shape hiring processes. Recall PM Viksit Bharat Rozgar Yojana (from Aug 2025) Apprenticeships (NAPS, NATS) PM Internship Scheme (2024) Background verification Employment-linked incentives. The Pradhan Mantri Viksit Bharat Rozgar Yojana , effective from 1 August 2025, provides an incentive to first-time employees registered with the EPFO and incentives to employers for additional formal hires, with longer support for manufacturing. It lowers the cost of hiring into formal, PF-covered employment. Apprenticeships. The Apprentices Act, 1961 and the National Apprenticeship Promotion and Training Schemes (NAPS and NATS) share stipend costs, making apprenticeships a low-cost pipeline for manufacturing and services. PM Internship Scheme (piloted from 2024) places young people in internships with large companies, with a government-funded stipend. Verification and fraud. Fake experience certificates, proxy interviews and bribery in hiring are known risks. In 2023, TCS dismissed employees and barred staffing vendors after finding irregularities in its contractor hiring. Consent-based background verification and secure, proctored assessments are standard responses."}, {"t": "1.14 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 1 · Talent Acquisition", "u": "book/01-talent-acquisition.html#s-1-14", "x": "In short Show that you know the evidence (structured interviews), the Indian context (notice periods, offer drop-outs) and the metrics. How would you improve the quality of hiring? Start with the selection method, because it has the largest effect: introduce structured interviews based on job analysis, with anchored rating scales, trained interviewers and independent scoring, and add work samples or job-knowledge tests where relevant. Measure quality of hire through first-year performance, time to productivity, hiring-manager satisfaction and 12-month retention, and compare across sources and interviewers to improve the process. How would you reduce offer drop-outs? Analyse where and why candidates drop out: by role, notice period and competitor. Speed up approvals, make accurate first offers and explain them clearly, use notice-period buyouts or joining bonuses with clawback for critical roles, run structured pre-boarding with the hiring manager involved, and keep backup candidates warm. Track the offer-to-join ratio monthly. Referrals are your best source. Is there any downside? Yes. Referrals tend to replicate the existing workforce, which reduces diversity, and can lead to favouritism. Track the diversity of referred candidates, set sourcing targets for under-represented groups through other channels, and apply the same structured assessment to referred and non-referred candidates. Would you use AI to screen 50,000 applications for frontline roles? Yes, for scale, with safeguards: validate the tool against the performance and retention of people actually hired, audit outcomes by gender, region and language, keep human review of borderline rejections, explain the process to candidates, and comply with the DPDP Act. The Amazon case shows what happens when a model lear"}, {"t": "2.1 The two principles behind every pay decision", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-1", "x": "In short Pay decisions must be defensible on two grounds: fairness between jobs inside the company (internal equity) and competitiveness with the outside market (external competitiveness). Each technique in this chapter serves one of the two. Recall Internal equity External competitiveness Individual equity Equity theory (Adams) Determinants: job, market, person, organisation Internal equity means that jobs of similar size and value to the organisation are paid similarly, and larger jobs are paid more than smaller ones. It answers the question: is this job paid fairly relative to other jobs in the same organisation ? It is achieved mainly through job analysis, job evaluation and grading (Sections 2.2 to 2.4). External competitiveness means that pay is set at a deliberate level relative to what other employers pay for comparable jobs. It answers the question: can we attract and retain people for this job at this pay? It is achieved through salary benchmarking and a chosen pay positioning (Section 2.5). A third principle, individual equity , concerns people in the same job: differences in their pay should be explained by legitimate factors such as performance, experience and time in role, rather than by negotiation skill, bias or the timing of hiring. It is managed through pay ranges, compa-ratios and the merit matrix (Sections 2.6 to 2.8). Why these principles matter: the first-principles view. Employees judge pay mainly by comparison. J. Stacy Adams’s equity theory (1965) describes how people compare the ratio of what they contribute to what they receive with the same ratio for others. When they perceive inequity, they act to restore balance: they reduce effort, ask for more, or leave. This is why a pay system’s credibility depends less on how much it pays and more on w"}, {"t": "2.2 Job analysis", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-2", "x": "In short Job analysis collects systematic information about what a job involves. Its outputs, the job description and job specification, are the raw material for job evaluation, benchmarking, recruitment and performance management. Recall Job description vs job specification Methods: interview, questionnaire, observation, SME workshop, work diary, critical incidents Analyse the job, not the jobholder Title rationalisation Definition. Job analysis is the systematic collection and recording of information about a job: its purpose, responsibilities, decisions, relationships, working conditions, and the knowledge, skills and experience required to perform it. Principle: analyse the job, not the person. A pay structure is built around jobs because jobs are relatively stable, whereas jobholders change. If pay is set around individuals, the structure reflects past negotiations rather than the work, and becomes impossible to explain or maintain. Outputs. Output Describes Contents Main uses Job description (JD) The job Job title, purpose, reporting line, key responsibilities and result areas, scope (budget, team size), key relationships Job evaluation, benchmarking, performance goals, onboarding Job specification The person required Qualifications, experience, technical and behavioural competencies, any physical or legal requirements Recruitment and selection, training needs analysis Methods. Most organisations combine several methods, because each has blind spots. Method How it works Best for Limitation Interviews Structured conversations with jobholders and managers Managerial and professional roles Time-consuming; jobholders may inflate their role Structured questionnaires Standard questions answered by many jobholders (e.g., the Position Analysis Questionnaire) Large populat"}, {"t": "2.3 Job evaluation", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-3", "x": "In short Job evaluation ranks jobs by relative size within one organisation. It produces internal equity; it does not evaluate people and does not set pay on its own. Recall Non-analytical: ranking, classification Analytical: factor comparison, point-factor Hay: Know-how, Problem solving, Accountability Mercer IPE: Impact, Communication, Innovation, Knowledge, Risk Evaluates the job, not the jobholder Definition. Job evaluation is a systematic process for determining the relative worth of jobs within an organisation, so that jobs can be placed in a hierarchy and grouped into grades. Why it is needed. Market data alone cannot produce a coherent structure. Many jobs have no reliable market match, survey data fluctuates, and pricing each job separately from the market produces anomalies such as a job being paid more than the job that supervises it. Job evaluation provides a stable internal order to which market data is later attached. Two things job evaluation does not do. It does not assess the performance of the jobholder, and it does not set salaries directly. It produces an order of jobs and, in analytical methods, a measure of the distance between them. Methods Method Type How it works Strengths Weaknesses Typical use Ranking Non-analytical, whole-job Jobs are compared as wholes and placed in order, often by paired comparison Quick, simple Gives order but not the size of gaps; subjective; impractical for many jobs Small organisations (< 30 jobs) Classification / grading Non-analytical, whole-job Grade descriptions are written first; each job is slotted into the best-fitting grade Easy to explain; quick to apply Jobs often fit more than one grade; descriptions become outdated Government and public-sector pay levels Factor comparison Analytical Benchmark jobs are ranked"}, {"t": "2.4 Grades", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-4", "x": "In short A grade groups jobs of similar size so they share one pay range. Grades turn many job evaluation scores into a manageable structure. Recall Grade = band of job-evaluation points Grade ≠ title ≠ level Narrow grades vs broadbanding Typical number: 10–15 for a mid-size company Definition. A grade is a group of jobs of broadly similar size, defined by a band of job-evaluation scores, that share the same pay range and often the same benefits eligibility. How grades are formed. Jobs are listed in order of evaluation scores and grouped where natural breaks occur, or into bands of equal width. For example, all jobs scoring between 340 and 400 points might form grade M1; the shift engineer (380) falls in M1 and the purchase executive (260) two grades lower. Grades, titles and levels are different things. A title describes the work (“Shift Engineer”). A level describes progression within a job family (associate, senior, lead). A grade describes job size for pay purposes. Organisations align the three in a job architecture so that each can be read from the others (Chapter 6). Number and width of grades. Design Characteristics Advantages Disadvantages Suited to Many narrow grades (15–25) Small differences between grades Frequent promotions; tight cost control Bureaucratic; every job change triggers a regrading request Hierarchical, large or public organisations Fewer, wider grades (8–12) Moderate Balance of control and flexibility Requires good range management Most corporates Broadbanding (4–6 bands) Very wide bands, often 100%+ spread Encourages lateral moves and skill growth; fits flat organisations Weak cost control; promotions less visible; managers have more discretion Flat, project-based or knowledge organisations"}, {"t": "2.5 Salary benchmarking and pay positioning", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-5", "x": "In short Benchmarking compares the organisation’s pay with the market for matched jobs. Positioning is the decision about where to stand relative to that market. Recall Survey houses: Aon, Mercer, WTW, Korn Ferry Match on content, not title P25, P50 (median), P75 Lead, match, lag; segmented strategy Age the data to a common date Definition. Salary benchmarking is the comparison of an organisation’s pay for specific jobs with pay for comparable jobs in a defined external market. Data sources Source How the data is produced Reliability Best use Salary surveys (Aon, Mercer, WTW, Korn Ferry) Participating companies submit employee-level pay data matched to the survey’s job catalogue; results are aggregated and anonymised High: verified, job-matched, consistent definitions Setting structures and midpoints Custom / club surveys A group of peer companies share data directly, through a consultant High for the peer group Industry-specific roles Job portals and review sites (Naukri, LinkedIn, Glassdoor, AmbitionBox) Job postings and self-reported salaries Low to moderate: unverified, inconsistent job definitions Spotting trends and emerging skills Recruitment intelligence Candidates’ current pay and expectations, offer declines Moderate: current but anecdotal Signalling when a range has fallen behind Three rules for using survey data correctly 1. Match jobs on content, not title. A job is matched to a survey position when its core responsibilities substantially correspond to the survey’s description. Titles vary too much between companies to be reliable. This is why accurate JDs (Section 2.2) are a precondition for benchmarking. 2. Read the distribution, not one number. Surveys report percentiles. The 25th percentile (P25) is the pay level below which a quarter of the market fall"}, {"t": "2.6 Pay ranges", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-6", "x": "In short Each grade has a pay range with a minimum, midpoint and maximum. The midpoint reflects the market at the chosen position; the width allows for growth in the role. Recall Midpoint = market reference Spread = (max − min) ÷ min Min = mid ÷ (1 + spread/2) Midpoint progression Overlap Definition. A pay range is the band of pay an organisation is willing to pay for jobs in a grade, from a minimum to a maximum, around a midpoint. Why a range rather than a single rate. People in the same job differ in experience and proficiency. A newly promoted person is still learning the role; an experienced person contributes more. The range allows pay to progress with proficiency while keeping a ceiling linked to the size of the job. Point Meaning Who is typically paid here Minimum Lowest pay for the job New entrants to the job, still learning it Midpoint Pay for a fully competent jobholder; set at the market level for the chosen positioning Experienced, fully proficient employees Maximum Highest pay the organisation will pay for the job Long-serving, consistently strong performers; beyond this, higher pay requires a larger job Design parameters Parameter Definition Typical values Effect of getting it wrong Range spread (Maximum − Minimum) ÷ Minimum 30–40% for junior and operational grades; 40–60% for professional and managerial; 60–80% or more for executive Too narrow: experienced people hit the maximum quickly. Too wide: weak cost control Midpoint progression Percentage difference between midpoints of adjacent grades 8–15% at lower grades; 15–25% at senior grades Too small: promotions carry little reward Overlap Extent to which adjacent ranges overlap Moderate overlap is normal Too much: grades lose meaning; experienced staff in a lower grade out-earn new managers above them Wor"}, {"t": "2.7 Positioning individuals in the range", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-7", "x": "In short Compa-ratio and range penetration show where each person’s pay sits. They are used to find under- and over-payment and to target increments. Recall Compa-ratio = salary ÷ midpoint × 100 Range penetration = (salary − min) ÷ (max − min) × 100 Red circle (above max), green circle (below min) Pay compression Compa-ratio Definition. Compa-ratio = employee’s salary ÷ midpoint of the employee’s pay range × 100. Compa-ratio Usual interpretation Typical action Below 80 Below the range minimum or very new Check for green circle; bring to minimum 80–90 New to the role, or underpaid relative to proficiency If performing well, prioritise in increments 90–110 Around target for a competent jobholder Normal increments 110–120 Very experienced, or high relative to the job Smaller fixed increases; consider bonus Above 120 Likely above maximum Check for red circle Group compa-ratio (total actual pay ÷ total midpoints for a team or function) shows whether a whole population is drifting above or below the structure. Correction The compendium (p. 30) defines compa-ratio as salary relative to the market average. The standard definition uses the midpoint of the employee’s own range. The two coincide only when the midpoint equals the market average. Range penetration Definition. Range penetration = (salary − range minimum) ÷ (range maximum − range minimum) × 100. It expresses how far through the range a salary sits, from 0% at the minimum to 100% at the maximum. It is more intuitive than compa-ratio when ranges differ in width. Worked example: three employees in one grade Range: minimum ₹6.72 lakh, midpoint ₹8.40 lakh, maximum ₹10.08 lakh. Employee Situation Salary Compa-ratio Range penetration Diagnosis A Joined 8 months ago ₹7.10 lakh 85 11% Normal for a new jobholder B 4 years in ro"}, {"t": "2.8 Increments and the merit matrix", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-8", "x": "In short The annual increment budget is distributed by a merit matrix that combines performance rating with position in the range, so good performers who are low in the range move up fastest. Recall Increment budget (India 2026: ~9%) Merit matrix: rating × compa-ratio Promotion increase is separate Lump sum for red circles Increment budget. Each year the organisation sets an overall budget for salary increases, as a percentage of the salary bill, based on market movement, inflation, business performance and ability to pay. In India, Aon’s survey reported an average actual increase of 8.9% in 2025 and a projected 9.1% for 2026 , with real estate, infrastructure and non-banking financial companies above the average and technology services below it. The merit matrix. The budget is distributed using a matrix of performance rating and compa-ratio. Illustrative matrix for a budget of about 9%: Rating Compa-ratio below 90 90–110 Above 110 Outstanding 15% 12% 9% Exceeds expectations 12% 10% 7% Meets expectations 9% 7% 4% Below expectations 0–3% 0% 0% The logic. For the same rating, a person low in the range receives a larger increase than a person high in the range. Over several years this moves strong performers towards the midpoint and slows the growth of salaries already above it. In the worked example, employees B and C with the same “exceeds” rating would receive about 10% and 7% respectively. Related practices. Practice Purpose Promotion increase Separate from the merit increase; usually brings the person at least to the new grade’s minimum, often with a fixed percentage on top Lump-sum merit payment One-time payment instead of a fixed increase for red circles or high-range performers, so fixed costs do not grow Market adjustment Off-cycle correction for jobs or individua"}, {"t": "2.9 Interview questions on this part", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-9", "x": "In short Most questions on this part test whether you can connect the steps and justify each one. Answer with the principle first, then the mechanism, then an example. How would you design a salary structure for an organisation that does not have one? Start with job analysis and standard job descriptions, because every later step depends on accurate job data. Evaluate jobs using a point-factor method such as Mercer IPE or Hay to establish internal relativities, and group them into grades. Benchmark each grade against salary surveys, matching on job content, and agree a pay positioning with leadership, usually the median for most roles and higher for scarce skills. Set range midpoints from the market data and range widths according to level. Place employees in the structure, deal with those below the minimum and above the maximum over one or two cycles, and use a merit matrix for future increments. Train managers to explain the structure. What is the difference between job analysis and job evaluation? Job analysis describes what a job involves and produces the job description and specification. Job evaluation uses that information to judge the job’s size relative to other jobs and produces a ranking or points score used for grading. Analysis is descriptive; evaluation is comparative. An employee with a compa-ratio of 85 and an outstanding rating, and another with a compa-ratio of 115 and the same rating: who should get the higher increase, and why? The first. Both performed equally well, but the first is paid well below the target for the job, while the second is already paid above it. A merit matrix gives a higher percentage to the first to move them towards the midpoint, and may give the second a smaller fixed increase plus a one-time bonus so that performance is still"}, {"t": "2.10 CTC, gross pay and net pay", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-10", "x": "In short CTC is the employer’s total annual cost for an employee. Gross pay is salary before employee deductions. Net pay is what reaches the bank account. The gaps between them are statutory contributions, variable pay, insured benefits, taxes and deductions. Recall CTC = gross + employer contributions + variable + other employer costs Net = gross − employee PF − PT − TDS − other deductions Variable pay in CTC is a target, not a guarantee Cost to Company (CTC) is the total amount an employer expects to spend on an employee in a year. It is an employer’s budgeting figure that has become the standard way Indian offers are expressed. Gross salary is the total of the salary components paid to the employee before any employee-side deductions: basic pay, dearness allowance, house rent allowance, special allowance and other allowances. Net salary (take-home or in-hand pay) is gross salary minus employee-side deductions: the employee’s provident fund contribution, ESI contribution where applicable, professional tax, income tax deducted at source (TDS), and any voluntary deductions such as loan repayments. The relationships can be written as: CTC = Gross salary + employer’s statutory contributions (PF, ESI where applicable, gratuity provision) + target variable pay + other employer-paid costs included in the offer (insurance premiums, meal cards, car lease, etc.). Net pay = Gross salary − employee PF − employee ESI (if applicable) − professional tax − TDS − other deductions. Illustrative CTC structure The structure below shows a typical offer of about ₹18 lakh for a management graduate. It is illustrative; actual structures vary by company and are covered by Sections 2.11 and 2.12. Component ₹ per year Explanation Basic 7,50,000 Set at about 50% of fixed pay so that excluded al"}, {"t": "2.11 Salary components and their tax treatment", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-11", "x": "In short Indian salaries are split into components because tax law, labour law and benefits are each calculated on different components. Since the new tax regime became the default, tax-driven structuring matters less, and the wage-code definition of wages matters more. Recall Basic, DA, HRA, special allowance, LTA, reimbursements New regime is the default HRA and LTA exemptions only in the old regime Income-tax Act, 2025 in force from 1 April 2026 Why salaries have components. Different laws and benefits use different bases. PF, gratuity and bonus are calculated on “wages” (mainly basic and DA). Income tax treats some allowances differently from others under the old regime. Leave encashment and some benefits are linked to basic pay. Splitting pay into components lets an employer manage each of these. Component What it is Tax treatment (old regime) Tax treatment (new regime) Basic pay Core fixed pay; the base for PF, gratuity and many benefits Fully taxable Fully taxable Dearness allowance (DA) Compensation for inflation; common in government, PSUs and unionised manufacturing, linked to the consumer price index Fully taxable Fully taxable House rent allowance (HRA) Allowance for rented accommodation Exempt up to the lowest of: actual HRA; rent paid minus 10% of salary; 50% of salary in Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata and Chennai or 40% elsewhere (salary = basic + DA) Fully taxable Leave travel allowance (LTA) Travel costs on leave Exempt for two journeys in a block of four years, travel cost only Fully taxable Special allowance Balancing component Fully taxable Fully taxable Reimbursements (fuel, phone, books) Against bills for official use Exempt to the extent of official use Generally exempt to the extent of official use Meal vouchers Food cards Exempt up to ₹50 per meal Taxab"}, {"t": "2.12 “Wages” under the Code on Wages: the 50% rule", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-12", "x": "In short The Code on Wages defines wages as basic pay, DA and retaining allowance. If excluded allowances exceed 50% of total remuneration, the excess is added back to wages. Wages drive PF, gratuity, bonus and overtime, so low-basic structures now cost more. Recall Wages = basic + DA + retaining allowance Excluded items > 50% → excess added back Effects: higher gratuity, possibly higher PF, lower take-home In force from 21 November 2025 Before the Codes. Each labour law had its own definition of wages. Many employers kept basic pay at 25–40% of gross and paid the rest as allowances, which reduced PF contributions, gratuity and bonus, all calculated on basic and DA. The uniform definition. The Code on Wages, 2019, in force with the other Labour Codes from 21 November 2025 , defines wages as all remuneration expressed in money, including basic pay, dearness allowance and retaining allowance , and then lists exclusions such as HRA, conveyance allowance, statutory bonus, overtime allowance, commission, employer contributions to PF and pension, and gratuity. The 50% rule. If the excluded items together exceed 50% of total remuneration , the amount by which they exceed 50% is added back to wages . The purpose is to stop employers from artificially splitting pay into allowances to reduce statutory dues. Worked example. Monthly remuneration ₹1,00,000: basic ₹30,000, HRA ₹25,000, special allowance ₹45,000. Step Calculation Result Excluded items HRA + special allowance = 25,000 + 45,000 ₹70,000 (70% of total) Permitted exclusion 50% of ₹1,00,000 ₹50,000 Excess added back 70,000 − 50,000 ₹20,000 Wages for statutory purposes 30,000 + 20,000 ₹50,000 PF (where calculated on actual wages), gratuity and bonus are then computed on ₹50,000 rather than ₹30,000. The Labour Ministry’s FAQs"}, {"t": "2.13 Provident fund (EPF, EPS, EDLI)", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-13", "x": "In short The EPF scheme provides retirement savings, a pension and life cover. Employer and employee each contribute 12% of PF wages. From 17 September 2026, coverage is mandatory for PF wages up to ₹25,000 a month. Recall Applies to establishments with 20+ employees Wage ceiling ₹25,000 (was ₹15,000) Employer 12% = 8.33% EPS + 3.67% EPF Max EPS ₹2,083/month EDLI 0.5%; admin 0.5% What it is. The Employees’ Provident Fund scheme, administered by the Employees’ Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO) and now governed by the Code on Social Security, 2020, has three parts: Scheme Purpose Funded by EPF (Employees’ Provident Fund) Retirement savings account with annual interest (8.25% for 2024–25) Employee 12% + part of the employer’s 12% EPS (Employees’ Pension Scheme) Monthly pension after retirement (age 58), and for family on death Employer’s 8.33% share, up to the ceiling; government contribution of 1.16% on wages up to ₹15,000 EDLI (Employees’ Deposit Linked Insurance) Life cover for the family if the member dies in service Employer 0.5% Coverage. The scheme applies to establishments employing 20 or more persons. Membership is mandatory for employees whose PF wages are within the wage ceiling , which was raised from ₹15,000 to ₹25,000 a month with effect from 17 September 2026 (notification S.O. 5109(E)), the first increase since 2014. Employees above the ceiling can be covered voluntarily. Contributions (on PF wages, i.e., basic + DA plus any wage-code add-back). Contributor Rate Notes Employee 12% Deducted from salary; can contribute more voluntarily (VPF) Employer: EPS 8.33% On wages up to ₹25,000, so at most ₹2,083 a month Employer: EPF 3.67% (or the balance of 12%) Balance of the employer’s 12% after EPS Employer: EDLI 0.5% Insurance Employer: administration charges 0.5"}, {"t": "2.14 Employees’ State Insurance (ESI)", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-14", "x": "In short ESI is a contributory health and social-insurance scheme for lower-paid employees, run by ESIC. It covers medical care and cash benefits for sickness, maternity, disablement and death. Recall 10+ employees Gross ≤ ₹21,000/month (₹25,000 for persons with disability) Employee 0.75%, employer 3.25% Calculated on gross, not PF wages What it is. The Employees’ State Insurance scheme, administered by the Employees’ State Insurance Corporation (ESIC) , provides medical care and cash benefits to covered employees and their families. Coverage. Establishments with 10 or more employees in notified areas. Employees with gross wages of up to ₹21,000 a month (₹25,000 for persons with disabilities) are covered. Contributions. Employee 0.75% and employer 3.25% of gross wages. Unlike PF, ESI is calculated on gross wages, so payroll must keep the two bases separate. Benefit What it provides Medical Full medical care for the employee and family through ESI hospitals and dispensaries Sickness Cash benefit of 70% of wages for certified sickness, up to 91 days a year Maternity Cash benefit of full wages during maternity leave (in place of the employer paying maternity benefit) Disablement Cash benefit for temporary or permanent disablement from employment injury Dependants’ benefit Monthly payments to dependants on death from employment injury Unemployment allowance Limited cash support for insured persons who lose employment, under specified conditions When ESI does not apply, employers remain liable under the employee compensation provisions for workplace injury and must fund maternity benefit themselves; this is one reason group medical and personal-accident insurance is standard for employees above the ESI ceiling."}, {"t": "2.15 Gratuity", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-15", "x": "In short Gratuity is a statutory lump sum paid on leaving after qualifying service: 15 days’ wages for every completed year. Fixed-term employees qualify after one year. Recall 15 × last wages × years ÷ 26 5 years (waived on death or disablement) Fixed-term: 1 year, pro rata Ceiling ₹20 lakh Pay within 30 days Provision ≈ 4.81% of basic What it is. Gratuity is a terminal benefit that recognises long service. It is not linked to performance. It was governed by the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972, and is now part of the Code on Social Security, 2020. Applicability and eligibility. Condition Rule Establishments covered Those employing 10 or more persons Events on which payable Superannuation, retirement, resignation, death, disablement due to accident or disease, termination; also expiry of a fixed-term contract Qualifying service 5 years of continuous service; not required on death or disablement Fixed-term employees Eligible after 1 year of service, on a pro-rata basis (Social Security Code) Rounding Service of more than 6 months in the final year counts as a full year Ceiling ₹20 lakh (government may revise) Payment deadline Within 30 days of becoming payable; interest is due on delay Forfeiture Possible, wholly or partly, for termination due to specified misconduct (e.g., wilful damage to employer property, riotous conduct, an offence involving moral turpitude) Formula (for employees paid monthly): Gratuity = 15 × last drawn wages × completed years of service ÷ 26. Here “wages” means basic + DA (now including any wage-code add-back), 15 represents fifteen days’ wages per year, and 26 the working days in a month. Worked example. Last drawn basic + DA ₹60,000 a month; service of 7 years and 8 months, which rounds to 8 years. Gratuity = 15 × 60,000 × 8 ÷ 26 = ₹2,76,923 . Fu"}, {"t": "2.16 Statutory bonus", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-16", "x": "In short The statutory bonus is a minimum profit-related payment to lower-paid employees, between 8.33% and 20% of wages, now governed by the Code on Wages. Recall 20+ employees Wages ≤ ₹21,000/month; 30 days worked Min 8.33%, max 20% Calculated on ₹7,000 or minimum wage, whichever higher Paid within 8 months of year-end What it is. The statutory bonus, previously under the Payment of Bonus Act, 1965, is now part of the Code on Wages . It is distinct from the performance bonuses employers pay voluntarily (Part 3). Element Rule Establishments 20 or more employees Eligible employees Wages up to ₹21,000 a month and at least 30 working days in the year Minimum bonus 8.33% of wages (payable even without profit), subject to rules for new establishments Maximum bonus 20% of wages, depending on the “allocable surplus” from profits Calculation base Wages of ₹7,000 a month or the applicable minimum wage, whichever is higher Set-on and set-off Excess or shortfall of allocable surplus is carried forward to later years Timing Within 8 months of the close of the accounting year Contract labour Under the central rules (May 2026), if a contractor fails to pay the minimum bonus, the principal employer must pay it Example. An eligible employee with a minimum wage of ₹12,000 a month: bonus base is ₹12,000 (higher than ₹7,000). Minimum annual bonus = 12,000 × 12 × 8.33% = ₹11,995 ; maximum = 12,000 × 12 × 20% = ₹28,800 ."}, {"t": "2.17 Other statutory items in payroll", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-17", "x": "In short Payroll must also handle professional tax, labour welfare fund, TDS, minimum and floor wages, overtime and statutory payment timelines. Recall Professional tax ≤ ₹2,500/year LWF: state-specific Wages paid by the 7th; F&F within 2 working days Deductions ≤ 50% of wages Overtime at 2× Item Rule HR relevance Professional tax State levy on employment, capped at ₹2,500 a year by the Constitution; not levied in every state (Delhi and Haryana do not levy it) Deducted by employer, rates vary by state Labour welfare fund (LWF) Small employer and employee contributions under state laws State-wise compliance TDS on salary Employer deducts income tax monthly based on the employee’s chosen regime and declarations Year-end Form 16 equivalent issued to employees Minimum wages Universal under the Code on Wages; fixed by skill level and area; cannot be below the national floor wage once notified Applies to all employees, including contract workers Payment timelines Monthly wages by the 7th of the following month; on resignation, termination or removal within 2 working days Full-and-final settlement processes had to be shortened Deductions Only authorised deductions; total not above 50% of wages in a wage period Recovery of advances and fines limited Overtime At least twice the ordinary rate; overtime limited to 144 hours a quarter under the central OSH rules Shift planning in plants Equal remuneration No discrimination on grounds of gender, including transgender identity, in wages or recruitment for the same or similar work Pay-equity reviews (Part 3)"}, {"t": "2.18 Interview questions on this part", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-18", "x": "In short Calculation questions are common. State the formula, apply it to a number, then mention one practical implication. What is the difference between CTC and take-home pay? CTC is the employer’s total annual cost, including employer PF, gratuity provision, target variable pay and insured benefits. Take-home pay is gross salary minus the employee’s PF, professional tax, income tax and other deductions. For a ₹18 lakh CTC with ₹15 lakh gross, take-home under the new tax regime is roughly ₹1.1 lakh a month. How does the Code on Wages affect salary structures? It defines wages as basic, DA and retaining allowance, and adds back any excluded allowances above 50% of total remuneration. Because PF, gratuity and bonus are calculated on wages, organisations with low basic pay face higher gratuity and possibly higher PF costs, and employees may see slightly lower take-home pay. Most organisations responded by restructuring so that basic and DA are around half of total remuneration, and by communicating the change before it reached payslips. Calculate gratuity for an employee with 9 years and 7 months of service and last drawn basic + DA of ₹45,000. Service rounds up to 10 years because the final part-year exceeds six months. Gratuity = 15 × 45,000 × 10 ÷ 26 = ₹2,59,615. It is payable within 30 days of leaving. What changed with the EPF wage ceiling in 2026? The ceiling rose from ₹15,000 to ₹25,000 a month from 17 September 2026. Coverage is now mandatory for PF wages up to ₹25,000, the employer’s EPS contribution is calculated on wages up to the new ceiling (a maximum of ₹2,083 a month), and employees in the ₹15,000–25,000 band contribute more, which lowers take-home pay but increases retirement savings. Sources: Code on Wages, 2019 (definition of wages) and Ministry of Labo"}, {"t": "2.19 Compensation philosophy and pay strategy", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-19", "x": "In short A compensation philosophy states how an organisation intends to use pay to support its strategy. It answers a fixed set of design questions, which the compendium groups as the “6Ps”. Recall Positioning (lead, match, lag) Internal equity Pay mix (fixed, STI, LTI, benefits) Pay for performance (differentiation) Ranges and structure Transparency and communication Definition. A compensation philosophy is a short statement, approved by leadership, that sets the principles for pay decisions: what the organisation rewards, how it compares with the market, how much pay varies with performance, and how openly pay is communicated. It guides structure design, offers, increments and incentive plans, so that individual decisions are consistent. The design questions a philosophy answers Decision Question Options and examples Market positioning Where do we pay relative to the market? Lead (P65–P75), match (P50), lag (below P50); often segmented by skill (Section 2.5) Internal equity How do we compare jobs internally? Job evaluation and grades (Sections 2.3–2.4) Pay mix What share is fixed, short-term variable, long-term variable and benefits? Fixed-heavy (stability), variable-heavy (results focus), balanced Pay for performance How much more do top performers receive? Size of increment and bonus differentials between ratings Structure How wide are ranges, how many grades? Narrow grades, standard ranges, broadbands Transparency What do employees know about pay? Nothing beyond own pay; ranges by grade; full transparency Pay mix varies with level. The share of pay at risk usually rises with seniority, because senior roles have more influence over results. Level Typical mix in Indian corporates (fixed : short-term variable : long-term) Operator / workman (unionised) Almost entirel"}, {"t": "2.20 Short-term incentives and variable pay", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-20", "x": "In short Short-term incentives reward results over a year or less. A well-designed plan has clear eligibility, a target payout, a few measurable metrics, a payout curve with threshold and cap, and a funding mechanism. Recall Bonus, commission, gainsharing, profit sharing Target %, metrics, weights Threshold, target, maximum Pool funding × individual modifier Line of sight (Vroom) Definition. Short-term incentives (STIs) are variable payments linked to performance over a period of one year or less. They are re-earned each period and do not add to fixed salary. Types Type How it works Best suited to Annual performance bonus Target percentage of salary, adjusted for company, unit and individual performance Most managerial and professional roles Sales incentive / commission Payment per unit of sales, revenue or margin, often with accelerators above target Sales roles with measurable individual output Productivity-linked incentive / gainsharing Team or plant receives a share of measured gains in output, quality or cost Manufacturing plants; often negotiated in wage settlements Profit sharing A share of company profit distributed to employees Broad alignment with company results; weak individual link Spot and project awards One-time recognition payments Rewarding specific contributions quickly Design elements of an annual bonus plan Element Description Typical choices Eligibility Who participates Grades above a level; minimum service in the year Target incentive Payout at 100% achievement, as % of salary 8–15% for managers, 20–40% for senior leaders Metrics and weights What performance is measured Company financial metrics (revenue, EBITDA) plus unit and individual goals; usually 3–5 metrics Payout curve How payout varies with achievement Threshold (e.g., 80% of target pays 5"}, {"t": "2.21 Long-term incentives (LTIs)", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-21", "x": "In short LTIs reward performance and retention over several years, usually through equity. ESOPs give the right to buy shares at a fixed price; RSUs give shares outright on vesting; performance shares vest only if targets are met. Recall ESOP, RSU, PSU, SAR, phantom stock Grant, vesting, cliff, exercise ESOP taxed at exercise, then capital gains SEBI (SBEB & SE) Regulations, 2021 for listed companies Purpose. LTIs align employees with long-term value and encourage retention, because unvested awards are forfeited on leaving. They are standard for senior roles in listed companies and are widely used at all levels in start-ups. Instruments Instrument What the employee receives Value to employee Typical users ESOP (employee stock option) Right to buy shares at a fixed exercise price after vesting Market price − exercise price; zero if the price falls below exercise price Start-ups; Indian listed companies RSU (restricted stock unit) Shares delivered free (or at face value) when they vest Full share value; always some value Listed MNCs, large tech companies Performance share unit (PSU) RSUs that vest only if performance conditions are met (e.g., relative shareholder return, EPS growth) As RSU, if conditions met Senior executives Stock appreciation right (SAR) Cash or shares equal to the increase in share price Price increase only Companies wanting to limit dilution Phantom stock Cash equal to the value or growth of notional shares As SAR or RSU, in cash Unlisted or family-owned companies Key terms. The grant is the award made today. Vesting is when the employee gains the right to the award, typically over 3–4 years. A cliff is a minimum period before any vesting, often one year. Exercise is buying the shares at the exercise price (for options). Worked example. An employee re"}, {"t": "2.22 Benefits", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-22", "x": "In short Benefits provide security and well-being rather than cash. In India they combine statutory benefits with voluntary insurance, retirement and leave benefits. Their value depends on employees’ perception of them as much as on their cost. Recall Statutory: PF, ESI, gratuity, maternity, employee compensation, bonus Voluntary: GMC, GTL, GPA, NPS, leave, parental benefits Flexible benefits Shop-floor benefits: canteen, transport, housing Definition. Benefits are non-cash or deferred forms of reward that protect employees against risks (illness, death, old age) and support their well-being and life outside work. Category Benefits commonly offered in India Notes Statutory PF, EPS and EDLI; ESI; gratuity; maternity benefit (26 weeks); crèche for 50+ employees; employee compensation for workplace injury; statutory bonus; leave under state Shops & Establishments Acts Compliance requirement (Part 2; Chapter 11) Health and protection Group medical cover (GMC, typically ₹3–10 lakh family floater), parental cover, top-ups, group term life (GTL, often 3× annual pay), group personal accident (GPA) GMC is one of the most valued benefits; mental-health cover is required on par with physical illness for insurers since the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 Retirement Employer NPS contribution, superannuation NPS is tax-efficient under the new regime Leave and family Paid leave beyond statute, paternity and adoption leave, sabbaticals, menstrual leave (e.g., Karnataka’s 2025 policy of one paid day a month) Paternity leave is not statutory for the private sector Work and life Flexible work, hybrid arrangements, childcare support, employee assistance programmes (counselling) Increasingly central to EVP Site and shop-floor Subsidised canteen, transport, housing or township, schools and hospi"}, {"t": "2.23 Total rewards", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-23", "x": "In short Total rewards is everything employees value in the employment deal, cash and non-cash. WorldatWork’s model has five elements. Recall Compensation Benefits Well-being Recognition Development Definition. A total rewards approach treats pay, benefits, well-being, recognition and development as one integrated offer, designed to attract, motivate and retain the workforce the strategy needs. Element (WorldatWork) Includes Example Compensation Fixed pay, variable pay, long-term incentives Market-positioned salary, annual bonus, ESOPs Benefits Health, protection, retirement, leave Group medical, NPS, parental leave Well-being Physical, mental, financial and social health; flexibility EAP counselling, hybrid work, financial-literacy programmes Recognition Formal and informal acknowledgement of contribution Spot awards, long-service awards, peer recognition platforms Development Learning, careers, mobility Leadership programmes, cross-business moves, sponsored education Use cases. Total rewards thinking is most useful when cash is constrained or not the deciding factor. ITC, for example, attracts campus talent with cross-business exposure (FMCG, hotels, agri-business, paperboards), structured careers and stability alongside competitive pay. A remote cement plant competes for engineers through housing, schools and safety culture as much as salary. In interviews, use the framework to show that reward is broader than pay."}, {"t": "2.24 Pay equity, transparency and governance", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-24", "x": "In short Pay equity means similar work receives similar pay regardless of gender or other protected characteristics. It is tested by separating the raw pay gap from the adjusted gap. Transparency and governance make pay decisions accountable. Recall Raw vs adjusted gender pay gap Regression for adjusted gap Code on Wages: equal remuneration SEBI BRSR disclosures EU Pay Transparency Directive (2026) NRC; Companies Act limits Measuring a gender pay gap Measure Calculation What it shows Raw (unadjusted) gap (Median male pay − median female pay) ÷ median male pay Overall difference, driven mostly by representation: fewer women in senior grades Adjusted gap Pay difference between men and women after controlling for grade, role, experience, performance and location, usually through regression analysis Unexplained difference that may reflect bias in pay decisions Method. Regress the logarithm of salary on grade, job family, experience, performance rating and location, plus a gender variable. The coefficient on gender estimates the adjusted gap. A significant adjusted gap needs correction through a dedicated budget; a large raw gap with a small adjusted gap points to problems in hiring and promotion rather than pay setting. Where unexplained gaps usually come from. Starting salaries anchored on previous pay, which carries forward past inequities; differences in negotiation; slower increases after career breaks and maternity leave; and job evaluation schemes that undervalue some types of work. Law, disclosure and transparency Code on Wages: prohibits discrimination on grounds of gender, including transgender identity, in wages and recruitment for the same or similar work. SEBI’s Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR): the largest listed companies disclose workf"}, {"t": "2.25 Interview questions on this part", "c": "Chapter 2 · Compensation and Benefits", "u": "book/02-compensation-and-benefits.html#s-2-25", "x": "In short Opinion questions need a position, the reasoning, the strongest counter-argument and a practical design answer. Is variable pay a good motivator? It is when three conditions hold: employees can influence the metric, payout reliably follows achievement, and the amount is meaningful, roughly 10–15% of pay or more. These are the conditions of expectancy theory. It fails when it is linked to results employees cannot influence, when targets change mid-year, or when payouts become routine. For junior roles in large organisations, company-linked variable pay behaves more like profit sharing; for sales and senior roles, well-designed variable pay is effective. ESOPs or RSUs: which would you recommend? It depends on the company’s stage. A start-up conserving cash and offering high growth potential will usually use ESOPs, which cost nothing at grant and reward upside. A listed or mature company will usually prefer RSUs, which retain value even if the share price falls and are therefore a stronger retention tool. Both should vest over three to four years, often with a one-year cliff. Should salaries be transparent? Full individual transparency is rarely practical in India because of privacy expectations and the risk of conflict over individual cases. Publishing pay ranges by grade, explaining how increments and bonuses are decided, and auditing pay equity regularly captures most of the benefit: it builds trust, narrows negotiation-driven gaps, and prepares organisations for disclosure requirements such as the EU directive. A pay-equity review shows women earn 18% less than men overall but only 3% less in the same grades and roles. What does that tell you, and what would you do? Most of the gap comes from representation: fewer women in senior grades. The 3% adjusted gap sh"}, {"t": "3.1 What performance management is for", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-1", "x": "In short Performance management is the continuous process of setting expectations, developing and evaluating performance, and using the results for decisions. The annual appraisal is only one event within it. Recall Performance management vs performance appraisal Purposes: strategic, administrative, developmental, documentation Goal-setting theory Feedback evidence (Kluger & DeNisi) Definition. Performance management (PM) is the continuous process of aligning individual work with organisational goals, setting expectations, observing and supporting performance, evaluating it, and using the evaluation for decisions about pay, development and careers. Performance management versus performance appraisal. A performance appraisal is a periodic evaluation event, usually annual, that produces a rating or assessment. Performance management is the whole system around it: goal setting, ongoing feedback, coaching, review, and the decisions that follow. Many failures attributed to “performance management” are really failures of an appraisal-only approach, in which nothing happens between one annual form and the next. Purposes Purpose What PM provides Example decision Strategic Translation of company strategy into team and individual goals Ensuring a plant’s safety and cost goals appear in every supervisor’s objectives Administrative Evidence for pay, promotion, retention and exit decisions Increment through the merit matrix; promotion to the next grade Developmental Feedback, identification of strengths and gaps, development plans Coaching, training, stretch assignments Documentation Records of expectations and performance Evidence in a dispute over termination or promotion The purposes conflict. An employee who knows that a conversation will determine their bonus is less likely to "}, {"t": "3.2 The performance management cycle", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-2", "x": "In short PM runs as a yearly cycle of four stages: plan, monitor and coach, review and calibrate, reward and develop. In most Indian companies it follows the April–March financial year. Recall Plan → monitor → review → reward/develop Mid-year review Roles: employee, manager, HR Three pillars: goals, actions, consequences Stage Timing (typical Indian cycle) Activities HR’s role 1. Plan April–May Cascade company goals to functions, teams and individuals; agree KRAs, KPIs or OKRs with weights; agree competency expectations and development goals Templates, goal-quality checks, alignment reviews 2. Monitor and coach Throughout the year; formal mid-year review around October Regular check-ins, feedback, coaching, adjusting goals when business conditions change Manager training; nudges and reminders; tracking check-in completion 3. Review and calibrate March–April Self-assessment, manager assessment, calibration across teams, final ratings Facilitating calibration, checking distributions and bias 4. Reward and develop May–July Increments and bonuses, promotions, development plans, performance improvement plans Applying the merit matrix and bonus rules; linking to L&D and talent reviews Roles. The employee owns their goals and development and seeks feedback. The manager sets expectations, coaches, evaluates and explains decisions; the quality of PM depends more on managers than on any form or software. HR designs the system, trains managers, runs calibration, checks fairness and connects outcomes to reward and talent processes. The three pillars named in the compendium remain a useful test of any system: clear and measurable goals and competencies, concrete actions to achieve them, and consequences that follow performance. When consequences are missing, PM becomes paperwork."}, {"t": "3.3 Goal frameworks: KRA, KPI, OKR and SMART", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-3", "x": "In short KRAs define areas of responsibility, KPIs measure them, and OKRs set a few ambitious objectives with measurable key results. SMART is a quality test for any goal. Recall KRA = area; KPI = measure OKR: objective + 3–5 key results OKRs: stretch, often not tied to pay Origin: Andy Grove (Intel), John Doerr (Google) SMART Cascading and weights Definitions Key result area (KRA). A broad area of responsibility in which a role is expected to deliver results, such as “workforce stability” or “statutory compliance”. The term is widely used in Indian organisations. Key performance indicator (KPI). A quantifiable measure of performance in a KRA, with a target. For the KRA “workforce stability”, a KPI could be “monthly voluntary attrition of operators below 1.5%”. Objectives and key results (OKR). A goal framework consisting of an objective , a qualitative statement of what is to be achieved, and three to five key results , measurable outcomes that show whether the objective has been met. OKRs were developed by Andy Grove at Intel in the 1970s and introduced to Google by John Doerr in 1999. Comparison Aspect KRA and KPI OKR Purpose Monitor ongoing performance of the role Drive a few priority changes or improvements Ambition Targets expected to be met Stretch goals; achieving about 70% is often considered success Scope Covers the whole role Covers selected priorities only Time frame Usually annual Often quarterly, with annual objectives Link to pay Usually direct Often deliberately separated, so people set ambitious goals Transparency Usually between employee and manager Often public across the organisation Example for a plant HR manager. Framework Example KRA Workforce stability KPI Monthly operator attrition below 1.5%; new-joiner retention at 90 days above 85% OKR Object"}, {"t": "3.4 Competencies and the “how” of performance", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-4", "x": "In short Results show what was achieved; competencies and values show how. Modern systems assess both, often on two separate axes. Recall What (results) vs how (behaviours) Competency framework with proficiency levels Two-axis rating “Brilliant jerk” problem A competency is an observable combination of knowledge, skills, abilities and behaviours needed for effective performance in a role, defined at different proficiency levels (Chapter 4). In PM, competencies describe the expected behaviours: for example, “Stakeholder management: builds agreement between plant and corporate functions on conflicting priorities”. Assessing only results rewards people who achieve targets in damaging ways: by cutting safety corners, mistreating teams or hoarding information. Assessing only behaviours rewards people who are pleasant but do not deliver. Many organisations therefore rate the what (results against goals) and the how (competencies and values) separately, and combine them through a matrix. How: below expectations How: meets How: role model What: exceeds Not eligible for top rating or promotion until behaviour changes Strong performer Top performer What: meets Development need on behaviour Solid performer Strong performer What: below Performance improvement plan Development need on results Development need on results Why it matters. What leaders reward defines the real culture (Schein, Chapter 7). If a high-revenue salesperson who bullies colleagues receives the top rating, every employee learns that results excuse behaviour."}, {"t": "3.5 Appraisal methods", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-5", "x": "In short Each appraisal method was designed to solve a specific problem. Choose by purpose: measurable results (MBO), strategy translation (BSC), behaviour accuracy (BARS), development (360), talent decisions (9-box). Recall MBO (Drucker, 1954) BSC (Kaplan & Norton, 1992): 4 perspectives BARS 360-degree feedback Forced distribution Graphic rating, critical incident, ranking Overview Method How it works Best for Main weakness Graphic rating scale Rate traits or competencies on a numeric scale (e.g., 1–5) Simple, widely used Vague anchors; open to bias Ranking and paired comparison Order employees from best to worst, or compare every pair Small groups Shows order only; unworkable for large teams Critical incident Record specific examples of effective and ineffective behaviour during the year Evidence for feedback Time-consuming; selective recording Management by objectives (MBO) Agree objectives jointly; evaluate achievement Roles with measurable outcomes Ignores how results were achieved; can encourage easy targets Balanced scorecard (BSC) Goals across financial, customer, internal process and learning perspectives Leadership and unit goals linked to strategy Complex; poorly chosen measures distort behaviour BARS Rating scale anchored to specific observed behaviours Frontline, service and supervisory roles Costly to build; role-specific 360-degree feedback Ratings from manager, peers, direct reports, sometimes customers Development of managers and leaders Popularity effects; confidentiality concerns; risky for pay decisions Forced distribution Fixed percentages of employees in each rating Preventing rating inflation in large populations Assumes all teams are normally distributed; damages collaboration Management by objectives (MBO) Introduced by Peter Drucker in The Prac"}, {"t": "3.6 Rating scales, distributions and calibration", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-6", "x": "In short Ratings must be consistent across managers to be fair. Forced distribution imposes consistency crudely; calibration achieves it through discussion and evidence. Recall 3-, 4- and 5-point scales Forced vs guided distribution Normalisation Calibration steps GE vitality curve (20-70-10) Rating scales Scale Example labels Advantage Disadvantage 5-point Outstanding, exceeds, meets, partly meets, does not meet Differentiates well Ratings cluster at 3 and 4 4-point No middle point Forces a judgement above or below “meets” Can feel harsh 3-point Exceeds, meets, below Simple; fewer arguments Little differentiation for pay Forced distribution Under forced distribution (the “bell curve”), a fixed percentage of employees must receive each rating, such as 10% top, 70% middle and 20% bottom. Jack Welch popularised it at General Electric as the vitality curve (top 20%, vital 70%, bottom 10%), with the bottom 10% often exited, a practice known as “rank and yank”. Arguments for Arguments against Prevents rating inflation, where nearly everyone is rated above average Performance in real teams is not normally distributed; strong teams are penalised Forces managers to differentiate, which pay-for-performance requires Encourages competition with colleagues instead of collaboration Identifies persistent low performers Managers game it, for example by rotating low ratings Demotivates solid performers labelled as “average” Microsoft dropped stack ranking in 2013, and GE, Accenture and Infosys moved away from forced curves around 2015–16. Many organisations now use guided distribution : a recommended distribution applied at the level of a large population (a function or business), not imposed on each small team, and adjusted through calibration. Normalisation is the Indian term for adj"}, {"t": "3.7 Rating errors and bias", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-7", "x": "In short Ratings reflect the rater as much as the ratee. Structured evidence, calibration and data checks reduce the main errors. Recall Halo, horn, recency, leniency, strictness, central tendency Similar-to-me, contrast, proximity bias Idiosyncratic rater effect (~62%) Research on ratings has found that a large share of the variation in performance ratings reflects the individual rater’s tendencies rather than the performance of the person rated. A study by Scullen, Mount and Goff (2000), cited in Deloitte’s redesign, attributed about 62% of rating variance to such “idiosyncratic rater effects”. Error Description Example Halo One positive trait influences the whole rating Fluent presenter rated high on all competencies Horn One negative trait influences the whole rating Quiet employee assumed to lack initiative Recency Recent events outweigh the full period Rating based on the last two months Leniency / strictness Rater consistently rates high or low One manager gives everyone “exceeds” Central tendency Rater avoids extremes Everyone rated “meets” to avoid difficult conversations Similar-to-me (affinity) Higher ratings for people like the rater Same college, background or working style Contrast Rating depends on comparison with others rated just before Average performer looks weak next to a star Proximity bias Employees seen more often in person are rated higher Hybrid workers rated below office-based colleagues Controls. Specific goals and behaviourally anchored criteria; evidence gathered through the year (check-in notes, critical incidents); calibration; rater training; and analysis of rating outcomes by gender, location, work arrangement and manager to detect patterns."}, {"t": "3.8 Feedback, check-ins and coaching", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-8", "x": "In short Frequent, specific, task-focused feedback improves performance; vague or personal feedback can reduce it. Regular check-ins are the core of modern PM. Recall SBI model (CCL) Feedforward (Goldsmith) Check-ins Coaching questions (GROW) Manager capability Principles of effective feedback , drawn from the research in Section 3.1: it is specific (refers to particular actions and results), timely (close to the event), task-focused (about the work, not the person’s character), balanced (recognises what works as well as what does not) and forward-looking (what to do next). The SBI model (Center for Creative Leadership) structures feedback in three parts: Situation (when and where), Behaviour (what the person did, observably), Impact (the effect on results or others). Example: “In Tuesday’s union meeting (situation), you summarised management’s proposal before responding to the demands (behaviour). That lowered the tension and got us an agreement on the shift roster (impact).” Feedforward (Marshall Goldsmith) focuses on suggestions for the future rather than criticism of the past, which reduces defensiveness. Check-ins are short, frequent conversations (monthly or quarterly) about priorities, progress, obstacles and development. They do not replace evaluation but make it more accurate and less surprising. Coaching conversations often follow the GROW model: Goal (what the person wants to achieve), Reality (the current situation), Options (possible actions), Will (what they commit to doing). Manager capability is the constraint. Every redesign that replaced annual reviews with check-ins depended on managers being willing and able to hold good conversations. Organisations that changed the process without training managers found that check-ins simply did not happen."}, {"t": "3.9 Managing under-performance", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-9", "x": "In short Diagnose the cause first, then use a structured improvement plan with support and documentation. In India, the process must respect standing orders, contracts and natural justice. Recall Ability, motivation, environment IDP vs PIP PIP: 60–90 days, measurable, supported Standing orders; natural justice Non-disciplinary termination of a workman may count as retrenchment Diagnosis Under-performance has three broad causes, and each needs a different response. Cause Indicators Response Ability (knowledge, skills) New to the role; tasks beyond current skill Training, coaching, job aids, closer supervision Motivation Capable but disengaged; recent change in attitude Conversation about causes; goals and consequences; personal or career issues Environment Unclear expectations, missing tools, poor processes, conflicting priorities Fix the work system (see Mager and Pipe, Chapter 4) IDP and PIP Individual development plan (IDP) Performance improvement plan (PIP) For Any employee, especially high-potentials Employees below the expected standard Goal Growth towards future roles Reaching the required standard in the current role Content Development goals; experiences, coaching and training (70-20-10); timeline Specific performance gaps; measurable targets; support provided; review dates; consequences Duration Ongoing Usually 60–90 days A fair PIP states the gap with evidence, sets measurable targets that are achievable within the period, provides real support (coaching, training, resources), holds regular reviews with written notes, and states the consequences clearly. A PIP used only as a formality before a decided exit damages trust and the employer brand: Indian employees discuss “PIP culture” openly on professional networks and review sites. India context The legal posit"}, {"t": "3.10 Linking performance to pay, development and talent", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-10", "x": "In short Performance outcomes feed three separate decisions: rewards (increments, bonuses), development (IDPs, training) and talent (promotions, succession). Each needs different information. Recall Merit matrix Bonus multiplier Promotion criteria: performance + potential + readiness Talent review and 9-box Decision Inputs from PM Process Increments Year-end rating Merit matrix of rating and compa-ratio (Chapter 2, Section 2.8) Bonuses Rating and goal achievement Bonus pool funded by company results; individual multiplier by rating (Chapter 2, Section 2.20) Development Strengths and gaps, competency assessment, career aspirations IDP; training needs analysis (Chapter 4) Promotion Sustained performance, potential, readiness for the larger role Promotion panel or assessment; job availability in the structure Succession and talent Performance and potential over time Talent review using the 9-box; succession slates for critical roles (Chapter 6) A common error is to treat promotion as a reward for performance in the current role. Performance shows that someone is effective in their present job, but promotion should depend on evidence that they can succeed in the next one. Promoting the best salesperson to sales manager without assessing leadership potential is a classic example."}, {"t": "3.11 How organisations have redesigned PM", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-11", "x": "In short From about 2012, many large companies replaced annual ratings and forced curves with frequent check-ins. Several later reintroduced simpler ratings for pay decisions. The lasting change is more frequent conversation, not the absence of ratings. Recall Adobe Check-in (2012) Microsoft (2013) Deloitte snapshot (2015) Accenture, GE (2015–16) Infosys (2016) Google GRAD (2022) Organisation Previous system Change Lesson Adobe (2012) Annual reviews and ratings consuming about 80,000 manager hours a year Check-ins between managers and employees at least quarterly; no ratings; managers allocate increases within a budget Frequent conversations improved clarity; voluntary attrition fell Microsoft (2013) Stack ranking against colleagues Dropped stack ranking; evaluation includes impact on others’ success Ranking had discouraged collaboration Deloitte (2015) Annual objectives, single rating, lengthy consensus meetings, about 2 million hours a year Quarterly or per-project performance snapshots : team leaders answer four questions about their own intended actions toward the person (for example, whether they would always want the person on their team, and whether the person is ready for promotion) Asking raters about their own intentions reduces idiosyncratic rater effects Accenture (2015–16) Annual review with rankings Dropped rankings and annual reviews in favour of ongoing feedback Removing rankings supported collaboration GE (2015–16) Vitality curve Continuous feedback through the PD@GE app Even the originator of forced ranking abandoned it Infosys (2016) Bell curve Removed forced distribution; more frequent feedback Indian IT followed global practice Google (2022) Twice-yearly ratings GRAD (Googler Reviews and Development): one annual rating with regular development check"}, {"t": "3.12 Performance management for different workforces", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-12", "x": "In short One design does not fit every group. Plants, sales, project-based knowledge work, hybrid teams and gig workers need different measures and processes. Recall Unionised workmen: settlements, team incentives Sales: objective metrics Project work: per-project feedback Hybrid: outcomes, proximity bias Gig: algorithmic ratings Workforce Typical approach Design considerations Unionised shop-floor workers Pay set by long-term wage settlements; team or line-level productivity incentives (output, quality, safety, attendance); skill-based progression through multi-skilling Individual ratings are rarely used and can be resisted by unions; incentive schemes are negotiated Sales Objective metrics (revenue, margin, collections, new accounts) with commission or incentive plans Balance volume with margin and customer quality; avoid targets that encourage mis-selling Project-based knowledge work (consulting, IT services) Feedback at the end of each project; aggregated evaluation Employees work for several managers; the people manager collects inputs Hybrid and remote teams Outcome-based goals, scheduled check-ins Guard against proximity bias; define availability norms Gig and platform workers Customer ratings and algorithmic metrics (acceptance rate, delivery time) Transparency of metrics, appeal against deactivation; state laws such as Karnataka’s 2025 gig-worker Act address these issues"}, {"t": "3.13 Organisation-level performance measures", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-13", "x": "In short Beyond individuals, HR tracks workforce productivity and the quality of people decisions using a small set of ratios. Recall Revenue and profit per employee Human capital ROI Early attrition Quality of hire Rating distribution by group Measure Formula Use Revenue per employee Revenue ÷ full-time equivalent employees Productivity comparison with peers Profit per employee Profit ÷ full-time equivalent employees Efficiency Human capital ROI (Revenue − (operating expenses − employee costs)) ÷ employee costs Return on spending on people Revenue per rupee of employee cost Revenue ÷ total employee cost How many times revenue covers people costs Early attrition Hires who leave within 90 or 365 days ÷ hires in that cohort Quality of hiring and onboarding Quality of hire Share of new hires rated “meets” or above at first review, combined with retention and ramp-up time Effectiveness of selection Rating distribution by group Share of each rating by gender, location, work arrangement, manager Bias and calibration check Correction The compendium’s 90-day quit-rate formula (p. 40) divides leavers by employees at the start of the period. Early attrition should use the number of hires in the cohort as the denominator."}, {"t": "3.14 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 3 · Performance Management", "u": "book/03-performance-management.html#s-3-14", "x": "In short For opinion questions, state a position, give reasons, acknowledge the strongest counter-argument and finish with a practical design. Should companies use a bell curve? Not as a quota imposed on every team. Real teams are not normally distributed, forced quotas encourage gaming and competition with colleagues, and companies including Microsoft, GE, Accenture and Infosys moved away from them. The legitimate concern behind the bell curve is rating inflation, which makes differentiation impossible. A guided distribution applied across a large population, combined with evidence-based calibration, addresses that without forcing every small team into the same shape. What is the difference between a KPI and an OKR? Which would you use for an HR business partner? KPIs measure the ongoing performance of a role against expected targets; OKRs set a small number of ambitious objectives with measurable key results to drive change, and are often kept separate from pay. An HR business partner would have KPIs for continuing responsibilities such as attrition, time-to-fill and engagement, and one or two OKRs for change priorities, such as building first-line manager capability in a plant. Your top performer achieves every target but demoralises the team. How would you rate them? Rate results and behaviour separately. On results the person exceeds expectations; on behaviour they are below. They should not receive the top overall rating or a promotion until the behaviour changes, and they should receive specific feedback, coaching and possibly a 360 to provide evidence. Rewarding results regardless of behaviour signals to everyone what the organisation really values. What is calibration, and what can go wrong in it? Calibration is a meeting in which managers review proposed ratin"}, {"t": "4.1 Purpose and key terms", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-1", "x": "In short L&D builds the capabilities an organisation needs to execute its strategy. It is one lever for performance, alongside clear goals, good systems and aligned incentives. Recall Strategy → capability → performance Knowledge, skill, ability, competency Training vs development vs education Capability (organisational) Purpose. Strategy is executed through organisational capabilities : the combination of people, processes, systems and tools that deliver results consistently. People capability depends on employees having the knowledge, skills and behaviours their roles require. L&D exists to build those capabilities. It is not the only lever: performance also depends on clear expectations, feedback, resources, processes and incentives, which is why diagnosis comes before training (Section 4.2). Key terms Term Meaning Example Knowledge Understanding of facts, concepts and principles (“what” and “why”) Knowing POSH timelines and principles Skill Learned ability to apply knowledge to perform a task, developed through practice Conducting a structured interview Ability Broader capacity to perform across situations, often influenced by aptitude and experience Handling a hostile negotiation calmly Competency Observable combination of knowledge, skills, abilities and behaviours needed for effective performance, defined at proficiency levels (Chapter 6) “Stakeholder management” at level 3 Training Structured, short-term intervention to build skills for the current role Training on a new HRIS Development Longer-term growth for future roles, often voluntary Leadership programme, cross-functional assignment Education Broad, formal learning not tied to a specific role An MBA Learning The process of acquiring knowledge, skills and behaviours, through formal and informal means Learni"}, {"t": "4.2 Diagnosing performance problems", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-2", "x": "In short Most performance problems are not skill problems. Diagnose the cause before choosing training, using Mager and Pipe’s questions or Gilbert’s Behaviour Engineering Model. Recall “Is it a skill deficiency?” Mager & Pipe questions Gilbert’s BEM: environment vs individual Training vs non-training solutions Principle. Training is the right solution only when people cannot perform because they lack knowledge or skill. If they could perform but do not, because expectations are unclear, tools are missing, processes are broken or incentives reward the wrong behaviour, training will not help and wastes time and money. Mager and Pipe’s analysis Robert Mager and Peter Pipe’s Analyzing Performance Problems (first published 1970) offers a sequence of questions: Is there a real performance gap, and does it matter? Compare actual and expected performance and estimate the cost of the gap. Are expectations clear? Do people know what good performance looks like? Do people have the resources? Tools, time, information, access. Is performance rewarded and poor performance addressed? Or is desired behaviour actually punished (extra work for the best performer) and poor performance ignored? Do people receive feedback on how they are doing? Is it a skill deficiency? Could they perform if their lives depended on it? If yes, it is not a skill problem. If it is a skill deficiency: did they once know how (practice or job aids may be enough) or do they need training? Gilbert’s Behaviour Engineering Model Thomas Gilbert ( Human Competence , 1978) classified the factors behind performance into environmental factors (provided by the organisation) and individual factors (brought by the person), each with three types: Information Instrumentation Motivation Environment Clear expectations, relevan"}, {"t": "4.3 How adults learn", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-3", "x": "In short Adults learn best when learning is relevant, builds on experience, involves practice and reflection, and is reinforced over time. Recall Knowles’ andragogy (6 assumptions) Kolb’s cycle (4 stages) Learning styles: not supported Forgetting curve; spacing; retrieval practice Social learning (Bandura) 70-20-10 (heuristic) Andragogy. Malcolm Knowles described six assumptions about adult learners: they need to know why they are learning something; they are self-directed ; they bring experience that is a resource for learning; they are ready to learn what helps with real situations; they are problem-centred rather than subject-centred; and they are mainly internally motivated . Practical implications: explain relevance, use participants’ experience, centre learning on real work problems and give learners some control. Kolb’s experiential learning cycle (1984): concrete experience → reflective observation → abstract conceptualisation (forming principles) → active experimentation (trying them out). Good programmes take learners round the whole cycle, for example through a role-play, a debrief, a framework and a real application at work. Evidence check The popular idea that people learn better when teaching matches their “learning style” (visual, auditory, kinaesthetic) is not supported by research; reviews such as Pashler and colleagues (2008) found no good evidence for it. Match methods to the content and the skill being learned, not to supposed styles. Memory and retention. Hermann Ebbinghaus’s forgetting curve showed that much newly learned material is forgotten within days without reinforcement. Two well-established remedies are spaced practice (revisiting material over time rather than in one session) and retrieval practice (recalling information, for example throu"}, {"t": "4.4 Training needs analysis", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-4", "x": "In short Training needs analysis (TNA) identifies where capability gaps exist at organisational, role and individual levels, and which gaps are worth addressing through learning. Recall McGehee & Thayer (1961) Organisation, task, person levels Data sources at each level Competency-based TNA Prioritisation William McGehee and Paul Thayer’s three-level model (1961): Level Question Data sources Organisational Where does the business need new or stronger capability, and where will training be supported? Strategy and business plans, KPIs, audit and quality findings, new technology or regulation, customer feedback Task (role) What does each job require, and to what standard? Job analysis, competency frameworks, standard operating procedures, observation Person (individual) Who has a gap, and how large is it? Performance reviews, skill assessments, 360-degree feedback, self-assessment, manager input Competency-based TNA. Where a competency framework specifies the required proficiency level for each role, assessing employees against those levels shows gaps directly and allows learning paths to be designed by level. Prioritising needs. Not every gap justifies investment. Prioritise by business impact, the number of people affected, the risk (safety and compliance needs are mandatory) and whether a non-training solution would work better."}, {"t": "4.5 Learning objectives", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-5", "x": "In short Learning objectives state what learners will be able to do after the programme. They guide design and make evaluation possible. Recall Revised Bloom’s taxonomy (2001) Six levels ABCD objectives Weak vs strong objective Revised Bloom’s taxonomy (Anderson and Krathwohl, 2001) orders cognitive objectives from lower to higher levels: Level Learners can… Example verbs Remember Recall facts List, define Understand Explain ideas Explain, summarise Apply Use knowledge in a situation Calculate, apply, demonstrate Analyse Break information into parts and see relationships Compare, diagnose Evaluate Judge using criteria Assess, recommend Create Produce something new Design, develop Writing objectives (ABCD format): Audience (who), Behaviour (observable action, using a verb from the taxonomy), Condition (circumstances or resources) and Degree (the standard). Weak Strong “Participants will understand POSH.” “HR executives (A) will apply the POSH Act’s timelines (B) to three written case scenarios (C) with no errors (D).” “Managers will learn feedback.” “First-line managers will deliver feedback using the SBI model in a role-play, rated at least 3 of 4 by an observer.”"}, {"t": "4.6 Instructional design", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-6", "x": "In short ADDIE is the standard process for designing learning; SAM is a faster iterative alternative. Gagné’s nine events structure an individual session. Recall ADDIE SAM Gagné’s nine events Blended learning Microlearning ADDIE Phase Activities Output Analyse Confirm the need (Section 4.2), TNA, audience analysis, constraints Learning need and audience profile Design Objectives, assessment methods, content outline, methods, sequence Design document Develop Create materials, activities, e-learning, facilitator guides; pilot Tested materials Implement Schedule, logistics, facilitator preparation, delivery Delivered programme Evaluate Measure reaction, learning, behaviour and results (Section 4.9); improve Evaluation report ADDIE is criticised as linear and slow. SAM (Successive Approximation Model) , developed by Michael Allen, uses rapid prototypes and repeated cycles of design, review and revision, which suits fast-changing content and digital learning. Gagné’s nine events of instruction Robert Gagné (1965) proposed a sequence that supports how people process information: (1) gain attention ; (2) inform learners of the objectives ; (3) stimulate recall of prior knowledge ; (4) present the content ; (5) provide learning guidance (examples, demonstrations); (6) elicit performance (practice); (7) provide feedback ; (8) assess performance ; (9) enhance retention and transfer (job aids, follow-up). The sequence is useful for structuring a workshop or an e-learning module. Blended learning combines methods (e-learning for knowledge, workshops for practice, on-the-job assignments and coaching for application). Microlearning delivers short units (a few minutes) that fit into work and support spaced practice."}, {"t": "4.7 Training methods, coaching and mentoring", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-7", "x": "In short Choose methods by the type of objective: knowledge, skill or behaviour. On-the-job methods carry most of the development load. Recall On-the-job vs off-the-job Method by objective Coaching vs mentoring vs training GROW Reverse mentoring Action learning Methods Method Description Best for On-the-job training Learning while doing the job under guidance Operational and technical skills Job rotation Planned moves across roles Breadth, management trainees Apprenticeship Structured training combined with work over a long period Trades and technician roles Job shadowing Observing an experienced employee Orientation, understanding roles Stretch assignments Roles or projects beyond current capability Development of high potentials Lecture and classroom Instructor-led presentation Knowledge, large groups Case studies Analysis of real or realistic situations Judgement and problem solving Role-play Practising interactions Interpersonal skills (feedback, negotiation, selling) Simulations and games Realistic models of systems or situations, including virtual reality Complex or hazardous tasks, business acumen E-learning Self-paced digital modules Knowledge at scale, compliance Action learning Small groups work on real business problems with reflection and coaching (Reg Revans) Leadership development Coaching One-to-one, goal-focused support Specific performance or leadership challenges Mentoring Relationship with a more experienced person Career and professional growth Choosing by objective. Knowledge can be delivered through e-learning, reading and lectures. Skills require demonstration, practice and feedback (role-plays, simulations, on-the-job practice). Behaviour change requires practice in real situations, feedback over time and support from managers (coaching, 360-degr"}, {"t": "4.8 Transfer of training", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-8", "x": "In short Much training is not applied on the job. Transfer depends on the learner, the design and, above all, the work environment, especially the manager. Recall Baldwin & Ford (1988) Learner characteristics Training design Work environment Before, during, after actions The transfer problem. Learning that is not applied at work produces no return. Timothy Baldwin and J. Kevin Ford’s model (1988) identifies three groups of factors that affect transfer: Factor Examples Learner characteristics Ability, motivation, belief that they can apply the learning Training design Relevance to real work, practice, realistic examples, feedback Work environment Manager support, opportunity to use the skill, peer support, consequences for applying or not applying it Practical actions. Before the programme: managers discuss with participants why they are attending and what they should apply. During : practise on real work tasks and plan specific applications. After : managers follow up, give opportunities to use the skills, provide job aids and recognise application; use spaced refreshers."}, {"t": "4.9 Evaluating learning", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-9", "x": "In short Kirkpatrick’s four levels measure reaction, learning, behaviour and results; Phillips adds ROI. Most organisations measure only reaction, which is weakly related to learning and behaviour change. Recall Kirkpatrick (1959): four levels New World Kirkpatrick: start at Level 4 Phillips ROI formula Isolating effects Brinkerhoff’s Success Case Method Reaction ≠ learning Kirkpatrick’s four levels Donald Kirkpatrick’s model (first published 1959) evaluates training at four levels: Level Question How to measure Timing 1. Reaction Did participants find it useful, relevant and engaging? Feedback forms, Net Promoter Score End of programme 2. Learning Did knowledge, skills or attitudes improve? Pre- and post-tests, skill demonstrations End of programme 3. Behaviour Are participants applying what they learned at work? Manager observation, 360-degree feedback, work samples, system data Three to six months later 4. Results Did business outcomes improve? Productivity, quality, safety, sales, attrition, customer measures Six to twelve months later Limits of Level 1. A meta-analysis by Alliger and colleagues (1997) found that participants’ reactions were only weakly related to learning and to behaviour change. A programme rated highly by participants may change nothing at work. New World Kirkpatrick. Later versions of the model recommend designing backwards : start with the Level 4 result needed, define the Level 3 critical behaviours that drive it, then design the learning, and put “required drivers” (manager follow-up, reinforcement, accountability) in place to sustain behaviour change. Phillips ROI methodology Jack Phillips added a fifth level, return on investment , and emphasised isolating the effects of training. ROI (%) = (Programme benefits − Programme costs) ÷ Programme"}, {"t": "4.10 Leadership development", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-10", "x": "In short Leadership development prepares people for larger roles through experience, relationships and formal programmes, with particular attention to transitions between levels. Recall Leadership Pipeline (Charan et al.): six passages First-time managers Assessment and development centres Methods Indian programmes Transitions between levels. Ram Charan, Stephen Drotter and James Noel’s The Leadership Pipeline (2001) describes six “passages”, each requiring new skills, time allocation and work values: managing self to managing others; managing others to managing managers; managing managers to functional manager; functional manager to business manager; business manager to group manager; and group manager to enterprise manager. The first passage is the most common failure point: high-performing individual contributors promoted to manage others often continue doing the work themselves rather than achieving results through the team. First-time manager programmes address this transition. Methods. Stretch assignments and rotations, action learning on real business problems, executive coaching, mentoring, 360-degree feedback, formal programmes (often with business schools), and development centres , which use assessment-centre exercises to diagnose development needs rather than to select. Indian examples. The Tata Administrative Service and Tata Management Training Centre in Pune, HUL’s management trainee and leadership programmes, and the leadership academies of groups such as Mahindra and Aditya Birla are often cited as leadership pipelines."}, {"t": "4.11 Learning technology", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-11", "x": "In short An LMS administers formal learning; an LXP recommends content and supports self-directed learning. AI is adding personalisation and tutoring. Recall LMS LXP Skills taxonomy AI tutors, content generation VR for hazardous training Technology Function Examples Learning management system (LMS) Administers courses: enrolment, delivery, tracking, compliance reporting SAP SuccessFactors Learning, Cornerstone, Darwinbox Learning experience platform (LXP) Curates and recommends content from many sources, supports self-directed learning, often linked to skills Degreed, EdCast Skills taxonomies Common list of skills used to tag roles, people and content Used to recommend learning and match people to roles AI tools Personalised recommendations, AI tutors and practice partners, rapid content creation Embedded in LXPs and productivity tools Virtual and augmented reality Immersive practice of hazardous, rare or costly scenarios Safety training, equipment handling, customer conversations (for example, Bank of America’s VR training)"}, {"t": "4.12 L&D in India and key metrics", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-12", "x": "In short Large Indian employers run corporate academies and large-scale reskilling; listed companies disclose training data in BRSR. Metrics should go beyond hours and spend. Recall Infosys Mysuru; TMTC Apprenticeships, Skill India IT reskilling for AI BRSR disclosure Metrics beyond activity Indian context. Corporate academies: Infosys’s Global Education Centre in Mysuru, one of the largest corporate training facilities in the world; the Tata Management Training Centre; group academies at Mahindra, Aditya Birla and others. Apprenticeships and skilling: the Apprentices Act, 1961, the National Apprenticeship Promotion and Training Schemes, sector skill councils and the Skill India programme. Reskilling for AI: Indian IT services firms report training very large numbers of employees in AI and generative AI, and fresher programmes now include AI tools. In 2025 Infosys released several hundred Mysuru trainees who did not clear internal assessments, raising questions about assessment design and fair treatment of trainees. Disclosure: listed companies report training coverage and hours in the Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR) . Metrics. Metric What it shows Training hours and spend per employee Activity and investment (input) Completion and assessment pass rates Participation and learning Application rate (share applying skills after 90 days) Transfer (Level 3) Time to proficiency for new hires or new roles Speed of capability building Internal fill rate for key roles Effectiveness of development and succession Change in business KPIs for trained versus untrained groups Results (Level 4)"}, {"t": "4.13 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 4 · Learning and Development", "u": "book/04-learning-and-development.html#s-4-13", "x": "In short Show that you diagnose before training, design for application, and measure beyond reaction. How would you measure the effectiveness of a training programme? Use Kirkpatrick’s four levels with concrete measures: reaction at the end, learning through pre- and post-tests, behaviour through manager observation or 360 feedback after three to six months, and results through business KPIs. Design backwards from the business result, use a comparison group where possible, and apply Phillips ROI to large programmes. Reaction scores alone prove little because they are only weakly related to learning. A manager asks for communication training for the team. What do you do? Treat the request as a symptom to diagnose. Ask what is going wrong, with examples, and check whether the issue is unclear expectations, a process problem, a conflict or a genuine skill gap, using Mager and Pipe’s questions. Design a solution for the actual cause, which may be a process change or facilitation rather than training. Coaching or mentoring for a newly promoted plant manager? Both, for different purposes. Coaching for the immediate performance challenges, such as running production reviews, managing the union relationship and delegating, with goals over three to six months. A mentor, such as an experienced plant head, for perspective and career guidance over a longer period. Do you agree with 70-20-10? As a design principle, yes: most development happens through challenging work, supported by feedback and coaching, with formal learning providing frameworks. As a precise ratio, no: it comes from executives’ recollections, not measurement. Experiences develop people only when they are planned, reflected on and supported. Safety training was completed, but accidents have not fallen. Why, and wha"}, {"t": "5.1 Core concepts", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-1", "x": "In short Engagement is active commitment that shows up as discretionary effort. Satisfaction is contentment. Employee experience is the whole journey that produces both. Well-being is the health that sustains them. Recall Engagement (Kahn; Schaufeli) Satisfaction Organisational commitment Employee experience (EX) Well-being Why engagement matters Definitions Employee engagement is a positive, active state of commitment to one’s work and organisation that shows in discretionary effort: doing more than the role strictly requires. Two academic definitions are widely cited. William Kahn (1990) described personal engagement as people bringing themselves physically, cognitively and emotionally into their work roles. Wilmar Schaufeli and colleagues defined work engagement as a state characterised by vigour (energy and persistence), dedication (enthusiasm and pride) and absorption (being engrossed in the work), measured by the Utrecht Work Engagement Scale. Job satisfaction is how content an employee is with their job and its conditions. It is passive: a satisfied employee is not necessarily willing to give extra effort. Organisational commitment is the employee’s attachment to the organisation. Meyer and Allen distinguished affective commitment (wanting to stay), continuance commitment (needing to stay because leaving is costly) and normative commitment (feeling obliged to stay). Employee experience (EX) is the sum of an employee’s interactions with the organisation across the employee lifecycle, from recruitment to exit, and how the employee perceives them. Employee well-being is the physical, mental, financial and social health of employees, in so far as work affects it and the employer can support it. How the concepts relate Engagement Satisfaction Employee experience Well-"}, {"t": "5.2 Engagement models", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-2", "x": "In short The main models describe engagement as an outcome (Aon), a set of workplace conditions (Gallup Q12), a psychological state with preconditions (Kahn) or a balance of demands and resources (JD-R). Recall Aon: Say, Stay, Strive Gallup Q12: hierarchy of needs at work Kahn: meaningfulness, safety, availability JD-R: demands → burnout; resources → engagement Aon: Say, Stay, Strive Aon (formerly Aon Hewitt) defines engagement through three observable outcomes. Engaged employees Say : speak positively about the organisation to colleagues, potential employees and customers. They Stay : have an intense desire to remain with the organisation. They Strive : are motivated to exert extra effort for the organisation’s success. Aon’s survey measures these with six items (two per outcome) and links them to engagement drivers , grouped into areas such as the work itself, people, opportunities, total rewards, company practices and quality of life. The analytical value of the model lies in identifying which drivers most influence engagement in a particular organisation. Gallup Q12 Gallup measures engagement through twelve survey items that describe the workplace conditions engaged employees report. The items are arranged as a hierarchy of needs at work : Level Theme of the items Basic needs Knowing what is expected; having the materials and equipment to do the work Individual contribution Opportunity to do what one does best; recent recognition; someone at work who cares; encouragement of development Teamwork Opinions count; connection to the organisation’s mission; co-workers committed to quality; a close friend at work Growth Recent conversation about progress; opportunities to learn and grow The first item, about knowing what is expected at work, sits at the base: if expectatio"}, {"t": "5.3 What drives engagement", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-3", "x": "In short The strongest drivers are the immediate manager, meaningful work, growth, recognition, fairness and confidence in leadership. Perks rarely appear among the top drivers. Recall Manager (≈70% of variance) Job characteristics model Growth and career Recognition Fairness (equity theory) Leadership and purpose Driver Why it matters Practical levers Immediate manager Sets expectations, gives feedback, recognises, supports growth Manager selection, training and accountability for team engagement Meaningful work Hackman and Oldham’s job characteristics (skill variety, task identity, task significance, autonomy, feedback) increase motivation Job enrichment, autonomy, line of sight to customers and results Growth and career Learning and visible progression keep people engaged and reduce exits Career paths, internal mobility, development plans Recognition Timely, specific recognition reinforces behaviour and signals value Peer and manager recognition programmes; spot awards Fairness Perceived unfairness in pay, promotion or treatment reduces effort (equity theory) Transparent pay ranges, calibrated ratings, consistent policies Leadership and purpose Confidence in direction and in leaders’ integrity Leader communication, consistency between stated values and decisions Well-being and flexibility Availability of energy (Kahn; JD-R) Workload management, flexible work, support services Perks are not drivers. Free food, offsites and recreation rooms may raise satisfaction briefly but rarely appear among the top drivers in driver analyses. In an interview, starting an engagement answer with perks signals a shallow understanding."}, {"t": "5.4 Measuring engagement and experience", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-4", "x": "In short Combine a periodic survey with pulses, lifecycle surveys, interviews and passive data. Report at team level only above an anonymity threshold, and always pair measurement with action. Recall Census survey; driver analysis Pulse surveys eNPS = %promoters − %detractors Stay and exit interviews Passive data Anonymity threshold Methods Method Description Strengths Watch-outs Annual census survey All employees; 30–60 items on engagement outcomes and drivers; benchmarked Complete picture; driver analysis Long; results arrive late; fatigue if no action follows Pulse surveys 5–15 items, monthly or quarterly Trend tracking; tests whether actions work Over-surveying reduces response rates Lifecycle (moment) surveys At set points: 30 and 90 days after joining, after promotion, after return from leave, at exit Diagnoses specific stages of EX Requires HRIS triggers eNPS “How likely are you to recommend this organisation as a place to work?” on 0–10 Simple, comparable over time One number with no explanation; pair with a comment question Stay interviews Structured conversations with valued employees about why they stay and what might make them leave Prevents regretted exits Must lead to visible action Exit interviews and surveys Reasons for leaving Identifies avoidable exits Leavers soften reasons; a survey a few months after exit is more candid Passive data Attrition, absenteeism, overtime, leave not taken, internal mobility Objective, continuous Privacy and data-protection obligations (Chapter 9) AI-based listening Conversational bots (e.g., Amber by inFeedo, an Indian platform), text analytics of comments Scale; early warning Trust and surveillance concerns eNPS Respondents scoring 9–10 are promoters, 7–8 passives and 0–6 detractors. eNPS = % promoters − % detractors , gi"}, {"t": "5.5 From results to action", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-5", "x": "In short Surveys build engagement only when results are shared quickly and acted on. Managers own a few team-level actions; HR provides data and support. Recall Share within 4–6 weeks 2–3 priorities per team Manager-owned action plans “You said, we did” Re-measure with pulses Share results quickly , within four to six weeks, at company, function and team level. Choose two or three priorities per team, based on driver analysis and team discussion, rather than trying to fix everything. Managers own team action plans , developed with their teams; HR supplies data, toolkits and coaching. Company-wide issues (pay, policy, leadership) are owned centrally. Communicate actions visibly, linking them to what employees said (“you said, we did”). Re-measure with pulse surveys to check whether actions are working. Why surveys fail. The most common reason is no visible action: employees conclude that answering is pointless, response rates fall and cynicism rises. Other causes are action plans owned only by HR, too many priorities, and results that are used to blame managers rather than support them."}, {"t": "5.6 Attrition and retention", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-6", "x": "In short Attrition must be segmented before it is interpreted: voluntary or involuntary, regretted or not, by tenure, role and manager. Retention strategies target the specific drivers found. Recall Attrition formula Voluntary vs involuntary Regretted vs non-regretted Early attrition India: 17.7% (2024) Cost of turnover Retention levers Measurement Measure Formula or definition Annual attrition rate Separations during the year ÷ average headcount during the year × 100 Annualised monthly rate Monthly separations ÷ average monthly headcount × 12 (approximation) Voluntary attrition Resignations initiated by employees Involuntary attrition Exits initiated by the employer (terminations, retrenchment) Regretted attrition Voluntary exits of employees the organisation wanted to keep (good performers, critical skills) Early attrition Hires leaving within 90 or 365 days ÷ hires in that cohort Retention rate Employees present at both the start and end of a period ÷ employees at the start India context Aon’s India survey reported overall attrition of 17.7% in 2024 , down from 18.7% in 2023, as attrition returned towards pre-pandemic levels. Rates vary widely: frontline roles in retail, quick commerce, BPO and delivery often exceed 50% a year, while public-sector companies and core manufacturing officer cadres are much lower. Always segment before comparing. Cost of turnover Turnover costs include separation costs (exit processing, notice-period disruption), vacancy costs (lost output, overtime), replacement costs (recruitment, agency fees, interview time) and ramp-up costs (training and the months before the new hire reaches full productivity). Estimates commonly range from several months’ salary for junior roles to more than a year’s salary for senior or specialised roles. Diagnos"}, {"t": "5.7 Engagement across different workforces", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-7", "x": "In short Drivers and listening methods differ between plant, corporate, frontline and hybrid employees. Designs that work for one group often fail for another. Recall Shop floor: safety, fairness, supervisor, facilities Union as part of the system Frontline and gig Hybrid: inclusion, proximity bias Workforce Typical top drivers Listening methods Design considerations Plant and shop floor (often unionised) Safety; fair treatment by supervisors; fairness of wage settlements; shift and overtime predictability; canteen, transport and housing; family welfare (schools, medical care); skill growth Local-language surveys through kiosks or paper; gate meetings; works committee; union channels Unions may see surveys as bypassing them, so involve them; line supervisors are the key engagement lever; literacy and fear of reprisal affect responses Corporate and professional Growth, manager quality, meaningful work, flexibility, pay competitiveness Online surveys, pulses, skip-level meetings Survey fatigue; cynicism if results are not acted on Frontline service and quick commerce Pay reliability, shift safety, respectful supervisors, quick onboarding, visible progression Mobile-first, vernacular, short pulses; supervisor huddles Very high attrition makes onboarding and the first 30 days critical Gig and platform workers Earnings stability, transparency of incentives and ratings, fair deactivation, insurance In-app feedback, worker councils Not employees; social security under the Code on Social Security (Chapter 11) Hybrid and remote Inclusion, clear expectations, fair evaluation Online pulses, team retrospectives Proximity bias; deliberate in-person time for learning and connection"}, {"t": "5.8 Well-being and burnout", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-8", "x": "In short Well-being covers physical, mental, financial and social health. Burnout is an occupational phenomenon caused by chronic, unmanaged workplace stress, so the most effective interventions change work, not only individuals. Recall Four dimensions Burnout (WHO ICD-11, 2019) Interventions: organisation, team, individual Mental Healthcare Act 2017 Right to disconnect (not law in India) Menstrual leave (Karnataka 2025) Dimensions Dimension Examples of employer support Physical Health insurance, health check-ups, ergonomic workplaces, safety programmes Mental and emotional Employee assistance programmes (counselling), mental-health training for managers, manageable workloads Financial Fair and timely pay, financial-literacy programmes, salary advances, retirement benefits Social Inclusion, team connection, family-friendly policies Burnout The World Health Organization’s ICD-11 (in force since 2022, adopted in 2019) describes burnout as an occupational phenomenon , not a medical condition, resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. It has three dimensions: exhaustion , mental distance from or cynicism about the job , and reduced professional efficacy . Because its cause lies in work, the most effective responses change work. Level Interventions Effectiveness Organisation Staffing and workload levels, realistic deadlines, manager training, flexible work, leave that is actually taken, protected time without meetings Highest: addresses causes Team Norms on after-hours communication, psychological safety, rotation of on-call duties, recognition Moderate to high Individual Counselling (EAP), resilience and stress-management training, mental-health first aiders Supportive, but cannot offset excessive demands India context Working hours deb"}, {"t": "5.9 Employee experience (EX)", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-9", "x": "In short EX design applies customer-experience methods to the employee lifecycle: personas, journey maps and moments that matter, then fixes the friction found. Recall Lifecycle stages Personas Journey mapping Moments that matter Digital EX and AI Lifecycle stages: attract, recruit, onboard, develop, perform, retain, separate, and alumni. EX design methods , borrowed from customer experience: Personas: representative employee profiles with distinct needs, for example a first-year engineering trainee at a remote plant, a working parent in a hybrid sales role, or a dark-store shift lead. Journey maps: the steps a persona goes through in a process (such as onboarding), with what they do, think and feel at each step, and where friction occurs. Moments that matter: points with outsized influence on how employees feel about the organisation, such as the first day, the first one-to-one with a manager, a missed promotion, return from maternity leave, relocation and resignation. Typical findings and fixes. Common friction points include delayed laptops and system access on day one, reimbursements that take weeks, HR queries passed between teams, and managers who are unprepared for new joiners. Fixes combine process redesign, digital tools (employee self-service, chatbots, HRMS workflows) and manager preparation. AI in EX. AI assistants answer HR queries, recommend learning, summarise feedback and predict attrition risk. IBM, for example, has described using AI to predict employees likely to leave. The risks are algorithmic bias, loss of human contact in sensitive matters and perceptions of surveillance; HR should keep humans responsible for sensitive conversations and decisions."}, {"t": "5.10 Employee value proposition (EVP)", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-10", "x": "In short The EVP is what an organisation offers employees in return for their contribution. The employer brand is how the market perceives the organisation as an employer. A credible brand depends on a true EVP. Recall EVP definition Gartner: Opportunity, People, Organisation, Work, Rewards EVP vs employer brand True, distinctive, relevant, segmented Build in five steps Measures Definition. The employee value proposition is the set of rewards, opportunities and experiences an employer offers in return for employees’ skills, effort and commitment. It is the internal promise that underpins attraction and retention. Gartner’s EVP attributes group what employees value into five categories: Attribute Includes Opportunity Career growth, development, internal mobility People Manager quality, colleagues, culture Organisation Reputation, purpose, stability, social responsibility Work Interesting work, autonomy, flexibility, work-life balance Rewards Pay, benefits, recognition EVP and employer brand EVP Employer brand Nature The actual offer and promise The perception held by candidates and the market Audience Mainly current employees Mainly potential employees and the public Built through Policies, practices, leadership and managers Communication, campus presence, social media, employee advocacy, review sites Test Is it true for employees? Is it consistent with what employees say publicly? Example HCLTech’s “Employees First, Customers Second” philosophy (Vineet Nayar, from 2005): inverting the pyramid, open feedback on managers, service tickets employees can raise against enabling functions HCLTech’s resulting reputation as an employee-centric IT employer Employees publish their experience on Glassdoor, AmbitionBox and LinkedIn, so an employer brand that is not supported by the "}, {"t": "5.11 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 5 · Engagement, Well-being, Employee Experience and EVP", "u": "book/05-engagement-well-being-ex-and-evp.html#s-5-11", "x": "In short Start with diagnosis by segment, name the manager as the main lever, and end with how you would measure the result. What is the difference between engagement and satisfaction? Satisfaction is contentment with the job and its conditions; engagement is active commitment that shows in discretionary effort. A well-paid employee in an easy role can be satisfied but disengaged. Herzberg’s theory explains the difference: pay and conditions prevent dissatisfaction, while achievement, recognition and growth create engagement. Attrition in a business unit is 28%. How would you approach it? First segment it: voluntary or involuntary, regretted or not, by role, tenure, location and manager. Then diagnose using exit and stay data, compa-ratios of leavers, early-attrition patterns and engagement results for the unit. Act on the two or three main causes found, such as a market correction for critical roles, manager coaching, career paths or onboarding changes, and track regretted attrition quarterly. Engagement scores are high, but so is attrition. How can both be true? The people leaving may not have responded to the survey, or may belong to groups the survey misses, such as contract staff or first-year hires. Pay may be uncompetitive, so people like working here but leave for much higher offers. Or the survey may measure satisfaction rather than intention to stay. Compare responses and exits by segment, and examine exit reasons and pay data. How would you build an EVP for an organisation? Research what current employees value and how the organisation compares with competitors; identify attributes that are both true and distinctive, using Gartner’s five categories; segment the EVP for key groups; test it internally before communicating it externally; and measure its effect o"}, {"t": "6.1 Strategy and structure", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-1", "x": "In short Organisation design is the deliberate shaping of structure, processes, rewards and people so that strategy can be executed. Structure follows strategy, and all elements must align. Recall Chandler (1962) Galbraith’s Star Model Mintzberg’s configurations Mechanistic vs organic (Burns & Stalker) Definition. Organisation design is the process of deciding how work is divided, grouped, coordinated and governed, and aligning structure with processes, reward systems and people practices, so that an organisation can execute its strategy. Structure follows strategy. Alfred Chandler’s study of large American companies ( Strategy and Structure , 1962) showed that as companies diversified into new products and markets, their centralised functional structures became overloaded and they moved to divisional structures. The principle is that structure should be chosen to fit the strategy, not inherited. Galbraith’s Star Model. Jay Galbraith described five design policies that must be aligned: strategy (direction), structure (where decision power sits and how units are grouped), processes (how information and decisions flow across units), rewards (what behaviour is measured and paid for) and people (the skills and mindsets the design requires). A common failure is to change structure alone: for example, forming cross-functional teams while still rewarding functional targets. Mintzberg’s configurations. Henry Mintzberg (1979) described five typical structural configurations, each relying on a different coordination mechanism. Configuration Coordination mechanism Typical example Simple structure Direct supervision by the owner or leader Start-up, small family business Machine bureaucracy Standardisation of work processes Cement plant, mass manufacturing, large bank operations Pro"}, {"t": "6.2 Design parameters", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-2", "x": "In short Every structure is a combination of six choices: specialisation, grouping, chain of command, span of control, centralisation and formalisation. Recall Work specialisation Departmentalisation Chain of command; unity of command Span of control Centralisation Formalisation Parameter Definition Trade-off Work specialisation How narrowly tasks are divided into separate jobs Efficiency and expertise versus monotony and coordination costs Departmentalisation How jobs are grouped: by function, product, geography, customer or process Expertise versus market focus (Section 6.3) Chain of command Lines of authority from top to bottom; unity of command means each person reports to one boss Clarity versus flexibility (matrix structures break unity of command) Span of control Number of direct reports per manager Wide spans are cheaper and faster; narrow spans allow closer support (Section 6.5) Centralisation Where decisions are made Control and consistency versus speed and local responsiveness Formalisation Degree to which work is governed by written rules and procedures Predictability and safety versus adaptability"}, {"t": "6.3 Structure types", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-3", "x": "In short The basic choices are functional, divisional and matrix structures. Newer forms (front–back, network, team-based) combine or loosen them. No structure is best in general; fit to strategy decides. Recall Functional Divisional: product, geography, customer Matrix: weak, balanced, strong Front–back hybrid Network Team-based / agile HR implications Functional structure Employees are grouped by specialised function (manufacturing, sales, finance, HR), each led by a functional head reporting to the chief executive. Strengths: depth of expertise, economies of scale, standard policies and clear career paths within functions. Weaknesses: silos, slow cross-functional decisions, and no single owner of a product or customer below the CEO. Fits: single-business organisations with a stable product range competing on efficiency, such as a cement or steel company. HR implication: HR is usually centralised and designs common policies for the whole company. Divisional structure Employees are grouped into semi-autonomous divisions organised by product (consumer and industrial divisions), geography (north and south regions; India and international) or customer (retail and corporate banking). Each division has its own functional resources and usually its own profit and loss responsibility. Strengths: clear accountability for results, faster decisions close to the market, focus on specific customers. Weaknesses: duplication of functions and cost, inconsistent practices, missed synergies between divisions. Fits: multi-product or multi-market organisations. HR implication: HR business partners sit in divisions; a corporate HR centre sets common frameworks. Matrix structure Employees report along two dimensions at once, typically function (for expertise and standards) and business, pro"}, {"t": "6.4 Matching strategy to structure", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-4", "x": "In short The strategy defines the key success factors, and the key success factors indicate the structure. Recall Cost leadership → functional, centralised Differentiation → divisional or matrix Focus Diversification → holding / divisional Strategy (Porter) Key success factors Structure that fits Example Cost leadership Scale, process efficiency, tight cost control, standardisation Functional, centralised, highly formalised Cement, steel, low-cost airlines Differentiation Innovation, brand, customer intimacy, speed to market Divisional or matrix, decentralised, cross-functional teams Premium consumer brands, pharmaceutical R&D Focus Deep knowledge of one segment Simple or functional Speciality chemicals, boutique consultancies Related or unrelated diversification Capital allocation, governance, shared brand and talent Divisional or holding company Diversified groups"}, {"t": "6.5 Spans of control and layers", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-5", "x": "In short Spans and layers determine cost and speed. The right span depends on the nature of the work, the team’s experience and the manager’s other duties. Recall Factors affecting span Typical 6–8; 10–15+ for routine work Layers and delayering Worked example Factors that allow a wider span: similar and routine work, an experienced team, good systems and procedures, co-location, and a manager with few non-managerial duties. Factors that require a narrower span: complex or novel work, inexperienced staff, dispersed teams, high risk (safety-critical work) and a manager who is also a major individual contributor. Typical values. Many corporate averages fall between six and eight direct reports; operational supervisors of routine work often manage 10–15 or more; senior leaders of complex functions may have fewer. Very narrow spans (fewer than four) are usually a sign of excess layers, or of managerial titles given for status. Layers are the number of levels from the chief executive to the front line. More layers slow decisions and communication and add cost. Delayering removes layers and widens spans. Worked example. A business unit has 1,200 employees and 240 managers, an average span of 5. Raising the average span to 7 in the parts of the unit where work is routine and teams are experienced would require about 1,200 ÷ 8 ≈ 150 managers (because each manager also counts in the total) instead of 240, freeing about 90 roles. In practice, redesign starts with the work (decision rights, systems), not with removing managers, and exits must follow the law (Chapter 10)."}, {"t": "6.6 Job design", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-6", "x": "In short Job design decides the content of jobs. The job characteristics model explains which features make jobs motivating. Recall Specialisation vs enlargement, enrichment, rotation Hackman & Oldham (1976) Five core characteristics MPS formula Job crafting Approach Description Purpose Specialisation Narrow, repetitive tasks Efficiency (Taylor’s scientific management) Job rotation Moving people across jobs periodically Skills breadth, reduced monotony, cover for absences Job enlargement Adding more tasks at the same level (horizontal) Variety Job enrichment Adding responsibility, autonomy and planning or control tasks (vertical), drawing on Herzberg’s motivators Motivation and ownership Job crafting Employees reshape their own tasks, relationships and view of the job Engagement and fit Job characteristics model J. Richard Hackman and Greg Oldham (1976) identified five core job characteristics: skill variety (range of skills used), task identity (completing a whole, identifiable piece of work), task significance (impact on others), autonomy (freedom in scheduling and method) and feedback (direct information about results). The first three create a sense of meaningfulness , autonomy creates responsibility , and feedback creates knowledge of results ; together these lead to motivation, performance and satisfaction. Motivating potential score (MPS) = ((skill variety + task identity + task significance) ÷ 3) × autonomy × feedback. Because autonomy and feedback multiply the score, a job low on either has little motivating potential however varied it is. Application. Redesigning a dispatch clerk’s job so that one person handles a customer’s order from booking to delivery confirmation (task identity), with authority to resolve delays (autonomy) and a daily dashboard of on-time"}, {"t": "6.7 Job architecture", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-7", "x": "In short Job architecture is the framework that organises all jobs into families, levels and standard titles mapped to grades. It underpins pay, careers, mobility and workforce data. Recall Job family, sub-family, track, level, title, grade Level guides Dual career ladder Benefits Building steps Governance Definition. Job architecture is the structured framework that classifies all jobs in an organisation by job family (type of work) and level (size and complexity), with standard titles, linked to the grading and pay structure. Element Meaning Example Job family Broad area of work Human Resources Sub-family Specialisation within the family Talent Acquisition; Total Rewards; HR Business Partnering Career track Path type Professional (individual contributor) or management Level Size and complexity of the role, defined by a level guide P1 Associate, P2 Specialist, P3 Senior Specialist, P4 Lead; M1 Manager, M2 Senior Manager, M3 Head Standard title Consistent name for the job “Senior Specialist, Talent Acquisition” Grade Pay grade from job evaluation (Chapter 2) Each level maps to a grade and pay range Level guides describe each level by generic criteria such as scope of impact, complexity of problems, autonomy, knowledge and leadership responsibility, so any job can be placed consistently. Dual career ladder. Organisations with deep technical work (R&D, engineering, technology) create a professional track that rises to senior levels equivalent to management grades, so experts can progress without managing people. Why organisations build job architecture Benefit How Accurate benchmarking Standard jobs can be matched to survey jobs on content (Chapter 2) Internal equity Same level and grade means same pay range across units Career paths and mobility Employees can see routes "}, {"t": "6.8 Competency frameworks", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-8", "x": "In short A competency framework defines the behaviours and capabilities needed for performance, at several proficiency levels. It connects selection, performance management, development and succession. Recall Core, functional, leadership competencies Definition + behavioural indicators + levels Building: critical incidents, BEI, expert panels Uses Pitfalls Definition. A competency is an observable, measurable combination of knowledge, skills, abilities and behaviours that distinguishes effective performance in a role. A competency framework is the organised set of competencies for an organisation or job family. Type Applies to Example Core (organisational) All employees, linked to values Integrity, customer focus, collaboration Functional or technical A job family Compensation analysis; process safety management Leadership People managers, by level Developing others, strategic thinking Structure of a competency. A definition, behavioural indicators and proficiency levels , for example: 1 Basic (applies with guidance), 2 Intermediate (applies independently), 3 Advanced (handles complex situations and guides others), 4 Expert (shapes practice across the organisation). Each role specifies the required level for each competency. How frameworks are built. Through job analysis and expert panels; the critical incident technique (examples of very effective and ineffective behaviour); and behavioural event interviews with high and average performers, an approach developed by David McClelland and later Lyle and Signe Spencer, which identifies the behaviours that distinguish superior performers. Many organisations adapt commercial libraries such as Korn Ferry’s (Lominger) competency library. Uses: competency-based interviews (Chapter 1), the “how” of performance (Chapter 3), train"}, {"t": "6.9 Workforce planning", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-9", "x": "In short Workforce planning compares future demand for people and skills with future supply and plans how to close the gaps. It combines judgement with quantitative methods. Recall Operational vs strategic WFP Demand: judgement, Delphi, scenarios, ratio, trend, regression, work study Supply: skills inventory, replacement charts, Markov Gap analysis 6 Bs Definition. Workforce planning (traditionally human resource planning, HRP) is the process of forecasting the organisation’s future demand for people and skills, assessing future supply, and planning actions to close the gaps, so that the right people with the right skills are available at the right time and cost. Horizons. Operational workforce planning covers the next 12–18 months, linked to the budget and headcount plan. Strategic workforce planning looks three to five years ahead, focusing on capabilities the strategy requires, such as digital or AI skills. Process Scan the environment and strategy: growth plans, new plants or markets, technology, regulation (for example, the Labour Codes), labour-market conditions. Forecast demand: how many people, with what skills, where and when. Forecast supply: the current workforce projected forward after attrition, retirements, promotions and transfers, plus the availability of external talent. Analyse gaps: shortages and surpluses by role, skill, level and location. Plan actions to close gaps (the 6 Bs below) and integrate them with recruitment, development and budgets. Monitor and adjust at least quarterly. Demand forecasting Method Type Description Example Managerial judgement Qualitative Managers estimate needs from business plans Department heads’ headcount requests Delphi technique Qualitative Anonymous experts give estimates over several rounds, seeing a summary each ti"}, {"t": "6.10 Succession planning", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-10", "x": "In short Succession planning prepares internal candidates for critical roles. Modern practice builds talent pools rather than naming one replacement per role. Recall Critical roles Success profile Readiness: now, 1–2 years, 3+ years Bench strength Talent pools Assessment and development Process. Identify critical roles: high impact on results and hard to fill, not only senior roles (for example, a kiln process specialist). Define success profiles: experiences, competencies and attributes needed. Assess potential successors through performance history, 9-box talent reviews (Chapter 3), assessment centres and 360-degree feedback. Classify readiness: ready now, ready in one to two years, ready in three or more years. Develop successors through stretch assignments, rotations, coaching and leadership programmes. Review at least annually in talent councils. Measures. Bench strength is the percentage of critical roles with at least one ready-now successor. Others include the internal fill rate for critical roles, the success rate of internal appointments and the diversity of succession slates. Talent pools. Because roles change and named successors leave, many organisations develop pools of high-potential people for families of roles, rather than one replacement for each position."}, {"t": "6.11 The HR operating model", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-11", "x": "In short Ulrich’s model organises HR into business partners, centres of excellence and shared services. It improved HR’s strategic role but created hand-off problems; newer models add digital self-service and product thinking. Recall Ulrich (1997): four roles Three pillars Criticisms HR as a product; agile HR HR-to-employee ratio Dave Ulrich’s model ( Human Resource Champions , 1997) defined four HR roles: strategic partner (aligning HR with business strategy), change agent (leading transformation), administrative expert (efficient processes) and employee champion (representing employees’ needs). It was later applied as the “three-pillar” or “three-legged stool” operating model: Pillar Role Example HR business partners (HRBPs) Work with business leaders to translate business needs into people priorities HRBP for a business unit or region Centres of excellence (CoEs) Specialists who design policies and programmes Total rewards, talent and leadership, learning, analytics Shared services Efficient, standardised transactions and queries Payroll, onboarding administration, HR helpdesk Criticisms. Hand-offs between the three pillars confuse employees about whom to contact; HRBPs get pulled into transactional work; CoEs design programmes disconnected from business reality; and shared services can feel impersonal. Newer approaches add employee self-service and chatbots, treat HR offerings as products designed around employee experience, and use agile cross-pillar teams for priority projects. HR-to-employee ratio. The number of HR staff per 100 employees is a common efficiency benchmark; it varies widely with size, industry and the level of outsourcing and automation."}, {"t": "6.12 Future of work", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-12", "x": "In short Workforce planning now has to account for gig work, hybrid work, AI and a shift from jobs to skills. Recall Gig and platform work Hybrid and remote AI and automation Skills-based organisation Internal talent marketplace Trend Implications for organisation design and planning Indian context Gig and platform work Part of the workforce is outside employment; planning includes contingent workers NITI Aayog estimated 77 lakh gig workers in 2020–21, projected to reach 2.35 crore by 2029–30; Social Security Code coverage (Chapter 11) Hybrid and remote work Roles designed for distributed teams; location strategy; policies for collaboration and fairness Return-to-office moves in IT services AI and automation Tasks within jobs automated; jobs redesigned; reskilling at scale Restructuring in IT services, including TCS’s reduction of about 12,000 roles in 2025 Skills-based organisation Skills, not only jobs, become the unit for hiring, development and deployment; skills taxonomies Internal mobility platforms in large Indian firms Internal talent marketplaces Employees matched to projects and roles across the organisation Used by multinationals and some Indian groups"}, {"t": "6.13 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 6 · Organisation Design, Job Architecture and Workforce Planning", "u": "book/06-org-design-job-architecture-and-workforce-planning.html#s-6-13", "x": "In short Structure questions test trade-offs. Workforce questions test whether you can turn a business plan into numbers. Functional or divisional structure for a company entering three new product categories? It depends on how different the categories are. If they have different customers, channels and competitors, a divisional structure gives accountability and speed, with shared services for HR, finance and IT to avoid duplication. If they are similar, a functional structure with category managers, effectively a light matrix, keeps efficiency. The decision is a trade-off between responsiveness and economies of scale. What is job architecture, and why does it matter? It is the framework that organises all jobs into families, levels and standard titles linked to grades. It makes salary benchmarking accurate, supports internal equity, shows career paths and mobility, gives clean workforce data and provides a common framework when integrating acquisitions. How would you forecast manpower for a new cement grinding unit? Use ratios from existing units, such as people per tonne of capacity by department, adjusted for the new unit’s automation level. Add shift patterns and a leave reserve, then split the requirement into permanent, fixed-term and contract workers, checking contract-labour and standing-order thresholds. Plan supply through internal transfers for supervisors and local ITIs and apprenticeships for technicians. What problems does a matrix structure create, and how can HR reduce them? Conflicting priorities from two managers, slower decisions and unclear accountability. HR can reduce them by defining decision rights (for example with a RACI matrix), having both managers set goals jointly and contribute to performance reviews, providing forums to resolve conflicts"}, {"t": "7.1 What organisational behaviour studies", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-1", "x": "In short OB studies how individuals, groups and structures affect behaviour in organisations, so that behaviour can be understood, predicted and influenced. Recall Individual, group, organisation levels Evidence-based, contingency approach Why HR uses OB Organisational behaviour (OB) is the study of how people think, feel and act in organisations, at three levels: the individual (personality, perception, motivation), the group (teams, conflict, power, leadership) and the organisation (structure, culture, change). OB draws on psychology, sociology and anthropology and emphasises contingency : what works depends on the situation. HR practices such as selection, performance management, rewards and change management are applications of OB findings."}, {"t": "7.2 Personality and individual differences", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-2", "x": "In short The Big Five is the best-supported personality model; conscientiousness predicts job performance across most jobs. MBTI is popular but unsuitable for selection. Recall Big Five (OCEAN) Barrick & Mount (1991) Dark Triad MBTI limits Locus of control; self-efficacy Emotional intelligence Johari Window Personality is the relatively stable pattern of thoughts, feelings and behaviours that characterises a person. The Big Five (five-factor model) Trait High scorers are… Work relevance Openness to experience Curious, imaginative, open to new ideas Adaptability to change, creativity, learning Conscientiousness Organised, dependable, disciplined, achievement-oriented The most consistent predictor of job performance across occupations (Barrick and Mount, 1991) Extraversion Sociable, assertive, energetic Performance in sales and management; leadership emergence Agreeableness Cooperative, trusting, compassionate Teamwork and customer service; very high agreeableness can make tough decisions harder Neuroticism (low = emotional stability) Anxious, easily stressed, moody Low neuroticism supports performance under pressure and job satisfaction Each trait is a continuum, not a category. Validated Big Five-based instruments are used in selection and development alongside other methods. A related six-factor model, HEXACO , adds honesty–humility, which is relevant to integrity. The Dark Triad : narcissism (grandiosity, entitlement), Machiavellianism (manipulation, cynicism about others) and psychopathy (lack of empathy, impulsivity). These traits are associated with counterproductive work behaviour and toxic leadership. Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) , based on Carl Jung’s theory, classifies people into 16 types using four dichotomies: extraversion–introversion, sensing–intuiti"}, {"t": "7.3 Values and attitudes", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-3", "x": "In short Values are enduring beliefs about what is important; attitudes are evaluations of specific objects such as one’s job. Both shape behaviour at work. Recall Terminal vs instrumental values (Rokeach) Job satisfaction, commitment, engagement Organisational citizenship behaviour Cognitive dissonance (Festinger) Values are enduring beliefs about what is desirable. Milton Rokeach distinguished terminal values (desired end states, such as a comfortable life or family security) from instrumental values (preferred ways of behaving, such as honesty or ambition). Value fit between employees and organisations affects attraction, satisfaction and retention (person–organisation fit, Chapter 1). Attitudes are evaluations of people, objects or events. Key work attitudes are job satisfaction , organisational commitment and engagement (Chapter 5). Organisational citizenship behaviour (OCB) is discretionary behaviour that is not part of formal job requirements but helps the organisation, such as helping colleagues, speaking well of the organisation and volunteering for extra tasks. Engaged employees show more OCB. Cognitive dissonance (Leon Festinger, 1957) is the discomfort people feel when their attitudes and behaviour conflict; they reduce it by changing one or the other. Involving employees in designing a change, and therefore committing them publicly to it, can shift their attitudes towards the change."}, {"t": "7.4 Perception, attribution and decision-making", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-4", "x": "In short People interpret others’ behaviour through attributions and biases. Structured processes and evidence reduce bias in HR decisions. Recall Kelley: consistency, distinctiveness, consensus Fundamental attribution error Self-serving bias Perceptual biases Bounded rationality; heuristics Escalation of commitment; groupthink Perception is the process of organising and interpreting sensory information. Because people perceive selectively, the same event can be seen differently. Attribution theory Harold Kelley proposed that people decide whether behaviour is caused internally (by the person’s ability or effort) or externally (by the situation) using three cues: consistency (does the person behave this way regularly?), distinctiveness (do they behave this way only in this situation?) and consensus (do others behave the same way in the same situation?). High consistency, low distinctiveness and low consensus suggest an internal cause. Two systematic errors. The fundamental attribution error is the tendency to over-attribute others’ behaviour to their character and under-attribute it to the situation: a manager calls an operator “careless” when the machine guard is badly designed. The self-serving bias is the tendency to credit one’s successes to oneself and blame failures on circumstances. Perceptual biases in HR decisions Bias Description HR example Halo / horn effect One positive or negative trait colours the overall judgement Fluent English taken as evidence of competence Contrast effect Judging someone in comparison with the previous person Average candidate looks strong after a weak one Similarity (affinity) bias Favouring people like oneself Hiring from one’s own college or region Stereotyping Judging individuals by group membership “Older employees won’t adopt th"}, {"t": "7.5 Motivation", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-5", "x": "In short Content theories explain what motivates people; process theories explain how motivation works. HR practices apply both. Recall Content: Maslow, ERG, Herzberg, McClelland Process: Vroom, Adams, Locke & Latham, SDT Reinforcement (Skinner) Pink: autonomy, mastery, purpose Theory-to-practice table Motivation is the set of processes that account for the intensity, direction and persistence of effort towards a goal. Content theories: what motivates Theory Key idea Evidence and use Maslow’s hierarchy of needs (1943) Five levels: physiological, safety, social, esteem, self-actualisation; later versions add cognitive, aesthetic and transcendence needs Intuitive, but research gives little support for a strict order in which each level must be satisfied before the next. Useful to explain why job security matters most to low-paid or insecure workers Alderfer’s ERG theory (1969) Three needs: E xistence, R elatedness, G rowth; several can operate at once; frustration of a higher need leads to regression to a lower one A more flexible version of Maslow Herzberg’s two-factor theory (1959) Hygiene factors (pay, policies, supervision, conditions, security) prevent dissatisfaction; motivators (achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, growth) create satisfaction Explains why fixing pay stops complaints but does not create engagement; basis for job enrichment McClelland’s acquired needs Needs for achievement , power and affiliation , shaped by experience High achievement need suits entrepreneurial and sales roles; a socialised need for power suits leadership Correction The compendium (p. 70) states that each of Maslow’s levels must be satisfied before the next. Research does not support a strict sequence; people pursue several needs at the same time. Present the h"}, {"t": "7.6 Groups and teams", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-6", "x": "In short Teams develop through stages, need complementary roles, and perform best when members feel safe to speak up and contributions are visible. Recall Group vs team Tuckman’s five stages Belbin’s nine roles Social loafing Psychological safety Virtual teams A group is two or more people who interact to achieve shared objectives; a team is a group whose members’ efforts produce collective results greater than the sum of individual inputs, with shared accountability. Tuckman’s stages (1965; the fifth added 1977): forming (orientation, politeness), storming (conflict over roles and approaches), norming (agreed norms and cohesion), performing (effective work) and adjourning (completion and disbanding). New project teams struggle in the early stages; facilitation and clear roles shorten them. Belbin’s team roles. Meredith Belbin identified nine roles people tend to play: Plant (creative), Resource Investigator (explores opportunities and contacts), Co-ordinator (clarifies goals, delegates), Shaper (drives action), Monitor Evaluator (critical analysis), Teamworker (supports cohesion), Implementer (turns ideas into plans), Completer Finisher (attention to detail) and Specialist (expert knowledge). Balanced teams cover the necessary roles. Social loafing is the tendency to exert less effort when working in a group than alone, first observed by Max Ringelmann in rope-pulling experiments. It is reduced by making individual contributions identifiable, keeping teams small and setting shared goals. Team effectiveness depends on clear goals, complementary skills, appropriate size, mutual accountability and psychological safety (Section 7.11). Virtual and hybrid teams need deliberate communication norms, trust-building and attention to fairness between remote and co-located members"}, {"t": "7.7 Conflict and negotiation", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-7", "x": "In short Conflict can be functional or dysfunctional. The Thomas–Kilmann model describes five conflict-handling modes; principled negotiation focuses on interests and alternatives. Recall Functional vs dysfunctional; task vs relationship conflict Thomas–Kilmann five modes Distributive vs integrative Fisher & Ury: four principles BATNA Conflict arises when one party perceives that another has negatively affected, or is about to affect, something it cares about. Task conflict (about the work) at moderate levels can improve decisions; relationship conflict (personal) is usually harmful. Thomas–Kilmann conflict modes Kenneth Thomas and Ralph Kilmann classified conflict-handling styles on two dimensions, assertiveness (satisfying one’s own concerns) and cooperativeness (satisfying the other party’s concerns): Mode Assertive Cooperative Appropriate when Competing High Low Quick action is vital; matters of safety or ethics Collaborating High High Both sides’ concerns are too important to compromise; long-term relationships Compromising Moderate Moderate Goals are moderately important; time pressure; equal power Avoiding Low Low The issue is trivial; a cooling-off period is needed Accommodating Low High The issue matters more to the other party; preserving the relationship No mode is best in all situations; skilled managers choose the mode to suit the issue. Negotiation Distributive negotiation divides a fixed amount (one side’s gain is the other’s loss); integrative negotiation seeks solutions that create value for both (Chapter 10 applies this to collective bargaining). Roger Fisher and William Ury’s Getting to Yes (1981) set out principled negotiation : separate the people from the problem; focus on interests , not positions; invent options for mutual gain; and insist on obj"}, {"t": "7.8 Power and influence", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-8", "x": "In short Power is the capacity to influence others. Its sources range from formal position to expertise and personal regard; influence tactics vary in effectiveness. Recall French & Raven: legitimate, reward, coercive, expert, referent (+ information) Influence tactics Organisational politics John French and Bertram Raven (1959) identified five bases of power , to which Raven later added information: Base Source Example Legitimate Formal position A plant head’s authority to approve shifts Reward Ability to give rewards A manager deciding increments Coercive Ability to punish Disciplinary authority Expert Knowledge and skills A compensation specialist’s influence over pay design Referent Admiration and identification A respected senior colleague Information Access to and control of information An HR business partner who understands business priorities Personal power (expert and referent) tends to produce commitment; position power used coercively tends to produce compliance or resistance. Effective influence tactics include rational persuasion, inspirational appeals and consultation; pressure and legitimating tactics are less effective. Organisational politics , the use of influence to pursue self-interest, increases when resources are scarce, criteria are unclear and trust is low; transparent processes reduce it."}, {"t": "7.9 Leadership", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-9", "x": "In short Leadership theories moved from traits to behaviours, then to situational fit, then to transformational and relational approaches. Effective leaders adapt their style and attend to both tasks and people. Recall Trait theory Ohio State, Michigan, Blake–Mouton Fiedler; Hersey–Blanchard; path–goal Transformational (4 Is) vs transactional Servant, authentic, LMX, Level 5 Leadership vs management (Kotter) Approach Key idea Main contributors Trait Effective leaders share traits such as drive, integrity, self-confidence, intelligence and extraversion Early research; traits predict who emerges as a leader better than who is effective Behavioural Leadership is defined by behaviour: initiating structure (task focus) and consideration (people focus) Ohio State and Michigan studies; Blake and Mouton’s Managerial Grid (concern for production vs people) Fiedler’s contingency model Effectiveness depends on matching the leader’s fixed style (task or relationship orientation, measured by the least-preferred co-worker scale) to the situation (leader–member relations, task structure, position power) Fred Fiedler Situational leadership Leaders adapt style to followers’ readiness: directing , coaching , supporting , delegating Paul Hersey and Ken Blanchard Path–goal theory Leaders clarify paths to goals and remove obstacles, choosing directive, supportive, participative or achievement-oriented behaviour Robert House Transactional Exchange of rewards for performance; correction of deviations Suits routine, measurable work Transformational Inspires followers to exceed self-interest through the “4 Is”: idealised influence , inspirational motivation , intellectual stimulation , individualised consideration James MacGregor Burns; Bernard Bass Servant leadership Leader’s priority is servi"}, {"t": "7.10 Organisational culture", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-10", "x": "In short Culture is the shared assumptions, values and norms that shape how work is done. Schein’s levels explain its depth, the Competing Values Framework classifies it, and Hofstede explains national influences. Recall Schein: artifacts, espoused values, basic assumptions Embedding mechanisms CVF: clan, adhocracy, market, hierarchy; OCAI Hofstede’s six dimensions; India’s profile Strong vs weak culture Culture change Definition. Organisational culture is the pattern of shared basic assumptions, values and norms that a group has learned and that guides how members perceive, think and behave. It shows in what is rewarded, tolerated and talked about. Schein’s three levels Edgar Schein described culture at three levels of depth: Level Description Example Artifacts Visible structures, processes, language, dress, office layout, rituals and stories; easy to see, hard to interpret Open-plan offices; informal dress; first-name terms with leaders Espoused values Stated strategies, goals, values and philosophies “We value innovation” in the values statement Basic underlying assumptions Unconscious, taken-for-granted beliefs that actually drive behaviour “Mistakes are punished”, “the boss is always right” When espoused values conflict with basic assumptions, people follow the assumptions. A company that espouses innovation but punishes failed experiments will not innovate. How leaders embed culture. Schein identified primary mechanisms: what leaders pay attention to, measure and control ; how they react to critical incidents and crises; how they allocate resources ; what they role-model and teach ; how they allocate rewards and status ; and how they recruit, select, promote and remove people. These matter more than statements or posters. Competing Values Framework Kim Cameron and"}, {"t": "7.11 Psychological safety and DEIB", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-11", "x": "In short Psychological safety lets people speak up and learn from mistakes; it is the strongest predictor of team effectiveness found in Google’s research. Diversity, equity, inclusion and belonging require both legal compliance and everyday practice. Recall Edmondson (1999) Project Aristotle (2012–15) How leaders build safety DEIB definitions Indian law and data Inclusion practices Psychological safety (Amy Edmondson, 1999) is a shared belief that a team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking: people can ask questions, admit mistakes, raise concerns and challenge ideas without fear of embarrassment or punishment. Google’s Project Aristotle (2012–15) studied around 180 teams and found psychological safety to be the most important of five factors in team effectiveness, followed by dependability, structure and clarity, meaning and impact. How leaders build it: framing work as learning, admitting their own mistakes, asking for input before giving their view, responding to bad news with curiosity rather than blame, and holding blameless reviews of incidents (including near-miss reporting in plants). DEIB Concept Meaning Diversity The presence of differences in the workforce: gender, age, disability, caste, region, language, religion, sexual orientation, background, thinking style Equity Fair access to opportunities, achieved by removing barriers that affect some groups more than others Inclusion An environment in which everyone can participate and contribute fully Belonging Individuals’ feeling that they are accepted and valued as part of the organisation India context Women: female labour-force participation has risen to about 42% (Periodic Labour Force Survey 2023–24) from about 23% in 2017–18, but representation in corporate middle and senior management remains low. The C"}, {"t": "7.12 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 7 · Organisational Behaviour and Culture", "u": "book/07-ob-and-organisational-culture.html#s-7-12", "x": "In short Name the theory briefly, then apply it to the situation in the question. Herzberg says pay is a hygiene factor. Should organisations stop worrying about pay? No. Hygiene factors must be adequate, or people become dissatisfied and leave. But raising pay above what is seen as fair rarely creates engagement; motivators such as achievement, recognition, responsibility and growth do. Equity theory adds that perceived fairness relative to others matters more than the absolute amount. How would you change an organisation’s culture? Diagnose it first, using Schein’s levels and the Competing Values Framework to compare current and preferred cultures. Then change the experiences that form basic assumptions: what leaders pay attention to, measure, reward and model; hiring and promotion criteria; how mistakes are handled; and how decisions are made. Align systems such as performance management and recognition, tell stories of the new behaviour, and expect the change to take years. Which leadership style would you use with a team of new management trainees? Situational leadership suggests starting with directing and coaching, because trainees are enthusiastic but inexperienced, and moving to supporting and delegating as their competence grows. Self-determination theory adds that giving autonomy within clear goals, opportunities to build competence and team connection sustains motivation. What is psychological safety, and how would you know if a team lacks it? It is the shared belief that the team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking. Signs that it is lacking: people do not raise problems in meetings, bad news reaches leaders late, near-misses are not reported and only the most senior person speaks. It can be measured with survey items such as “If you make a mistake on this"}, {"t": "8.1 Concepts", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-1", "x": "In short Organisational change moves an organisation from a current state to a desired future state. Change management is the structured approach to the people side of that movement. Recall Current state → future state Change vs transition (Bridges) Types: scale, planning, object Implementation vs adoption Performance dip Change fatigue Organisational change is a shift in how an organisation works: its strategy, structure, processes, technology, culture or people. Change management is the structured approach to preparing, equipping and supporting individuals so that the change is adopted and its intended results achieved. Change and transition. William Bridges ( Managing Transitions , 1991) distinguished change , the external, situational shift (a new system, structure or policy), from transition , the internal, psychological process people go through to come to terms with it. Many initiatives fail because they manage the change (plans, systems, go-live dates) but neglect the transition (loss, uncertainty, learning). Types of change Dimension Types Example Scale Incremental : improvements within the existing model. Transformational : fundamental change to strategy, structure or culture Adding quarterly check-ins versus moving from a functional to a divisional structure Planning Planned : deliberate, with a roadmap and governance. Emergent : evolves through local adaptation A phased HRIS rollout versus hybrid working norms developing team by team Object Structural , process , technological , cultural Reorganisation; new hiring process; cloud HRIS; from command-and-control to collaborative culture Trigger Reactive (responding to a crisis) or proactive (anticipating) Cost cuts after losses versus early investment in digital skills Implementation versus adoption. Implementa"}, {"t": "8.2 Why change succeeds or fails", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-2", "x": "In short Change fails most often for people-related reasons: weak sponsorship, poor communication, unprepared managers and little attention to adoption. The popular “70% fail” figure has no solid basis. Recall Common failure causes Sponsorship as top success factor (Prosci) “70% fail” unsupported (Hughes 2011) Readiness assessment Common causes of failure: an unclear case for change; weak or invisible sponsorship; little involvement of those affected; insufficient communication, especially two-way; middle managers who are not prepared or not convinced; inadequate training and support; misaligned rewards and systems that reinforce the old way; declaring victory too early; and too many simultaneous changes. What predicts success. Prosci’s long-running benchmarking research among change practitioners consistently identifies active and visible executive sponsorship as the strongest contributor to success, followed by a structured approach, employee engagement, frequent and open communication, and well-equipped middle managers. Caution The frequently quoted claim that “70% of change programmes fail” has no reliable empirical basis; a review by Mark Hughes ( Journal of Change Management , 2011) traced the claim and found no valid supporting evidence. If you use it in an interview, describe it as a common claim rather than a fact. Readiness assessment. Before launching, assess readiness: how clearly people understand the reason for change, leaders’ commitment, the organisation’s history with change, current workload and the capacity to absorb more change, and the strength of managers and change agents."}, {"t": "8.3 How people respond to change", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-3", "x": "In short People move through emotional stages during change and resist for understandable reasons. Treating resistance as information leads to better design and faster adoption. Recall Change curve (Kübler-Ross, adapted) Bridges: ending, neutral zone, new beginning Individual and organisational sources of resistance Kotter & Schlesinger: six approaches The change curve Adapted from Elisabeth Kübler-Ross’s model of grief (1969), the change curve describes common emotional stages: shock and denial , then frustration and anger , a low point of depression or disengagement, followed by experimentation , decision and integration . The practical use is timing: communicate early and repeatedly during shock and denial, provide support and listening during frustration and the low point, and provide training and quick wins during experimentation. Bridges’ three phases of transition Phase What people experience What leaders should do Ending, losing, letting go Loss of familiar roles, relationships, status or competence Acknowledge losses openly; explain what is ending and what is not Neutral zone Confusion, anxiety, lower productivity, but also creativity Provide temporary structures, frequent communication, short-term goals New beginning New identity, energy and commitment Celebrate early successes; reinforce the new ways Why people resist Individual sources Organisational sources Habit : comfort with existing routines Structural inertia : systems, rules and processes built for the old way Security : fear of job loss Group norms : peer pressure to keep things as they are Economic factors : fear of reduced pay or overtime Threats to expertise : new methods make existing skills less valuable Fear of the unknown Threats to power and resources : units or managers losing authority or b"}, {"t": "8.4 Change models", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-4", "x": "In short Lewin explains the logic of change, Kotter guides leaders through large-scale change, ADKAR diagnoses individual adoption, 7S checks alignment, and Appreciative Inquiry builds change on strengths. Recall Lewin (1947): unfreeze, change, refreeze; force field Kotter (1995/1996): 8 steps; Accelerate (2014) ADKAR (Prosci): barrier point McKinsey 7S (1980) Appreciative Inquiry 4-D (1987) Which model when Lewin’s three-step model and force-field analysis Kurt Lewin (1947) described change as unfreezing (creating readiness by challenging the status quo), changing (moving to new behaviours with support) and refreezing (stabilising the new state through systems, rewards and norms). Force-field analysis lists the driving forces for a change and the restraining forces against it. Lewin argued that reducing restraining forces (fear, lack of skills, conflicting incentives) is usually more effective than increasing driving forces, which tends to raise resistance. Example: introducing digital invoicing. Driving forces: cost savings, audit requirements, customer demand. Restraining forces: staff unfamiliar with the software, fear of errors, an approval process designed for paper. Actions target the restraining forces: training, a parallel run, a redesigned approval flow. Criticism: “refreeze” implies stability that rarely exists today; later writers speak of stabilising or embedding. Kotter’s eight steps John Kotter’s article “Leading Change: Why Transformation Efforts Fail” ( Harvard Business Review , 1995) and book Leading Change (1996) set out eight steps, each addressing a common failure. Step Purpose Example: rolling out a new HRIS 1. Establish a sense of urgency Show why the status quo is untenable Present payroll errors, audit findings and hours spent on manual work 2. "}, {"t": "8.5 Roles and stakeholders", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-5", "x": "In short Change is carried through a chain of sponsors, managers and change agents. Stakeholder analysis shows whom to involve and how. Recall Sponsor: ABCs Sponsorship spine Middle managers Change agents and champions HR’s role Power–interest grid Commitment curve Role Responsibilities Sponsor (senior leader who owns the change) Prosci’s “ABCs”: be A ctive and visible throughout; B uild a coalition of support among peers and managers; C ommunicate directly with employees about why the change matters. Provide resources and remove barriers Guiding coalition Leaders with authority, expertise and credibility who drive the change together Middle managers Translate the change into daily priorities, explain what it means for their teams, handle concerns and resistance, model the new behaviours. Often also affected by the change themselves Change agents and champions People (HR, project teams, respected employees) who plan and support communication, training and local problem-solving HR Stakeholder and impact analysis, communication and training plans, manager toolkits, aligning performance goals and rewards, union consultation, measuring adoption The sponsorship spine. Change is reinforced through a chain: sponsor → senior leaders → middle managers → employees. A weak link, such as an unconvinced middle manager, stalls the change for everyone below. Stakeholder analysis. A power–interest grid classifies stakeholders: high power, high interest (manage closely and involve), high power, low interest (keep satisfied), low power, high interest (keep informed and involved), low power, low interest (monitor). A commitment curve tracks movement from awareness to understanding, acceptance and commitment."}, {"t": "8.6 Communicating change", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-6", "x": "In short Effective change communication is early, repeated, two-way, delivered largely by managers, and answers “what does this mean for me?” Recall Why, what, how, when, what it means for me Repetition Two-way channels Manager cascade Segment by audience Communication plan Principles. Explain why the change is necessary before what will change; answer the personal question of what it means for me (job, pay, location, skills); communicate early, even when not all details are known, to prevent rumours; repeat key messages through several channels, since people absorb change messages slowly; make communication two-way (questions, feedback, forums); and use managers as the main channel for personal implications, since employees prefer to hear these from their own manager. A communication plan lists each audience (by function, location, level and union status), the key messages for each, the channels (town halls, manager briefings, gate meetings, intranet, WhatsApp groups for shop-floor staff), the messenger, the timing and how feedback will be collected. In plants, use local languages and face-to-face sessions on the shop floor."}, {"t": "8.7 Change in unionised Indian workplaces", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-7", "x": "In short Changes to conditions of service for workers require 21 days’ notice under the IR Code, and major changes are usually negotiated through settlements. Consultation is both a legal requirement and the practical route to adoption. Recall IR Code s.40 (formerly ID Act s.9A) 21 days’ notice Matters in the Third Schedule Settlements as change vehicles Tata Steel restructuring Notice of change. Under section 40 of the Industrial Relations Code (formerly section 9A of the Industrial Disputes Act), an employer that proposes to change workers’ conditions of service on matters listed in the Code’s Third Schedule must give notice to affected workers and may not effect the change within 21 days of the notice. The listed matters include wages and allowances, contributions to provident fund or pension, hours of work and rest intervals, leave and holidays, starting or changing shift working other than as provided in standing orders, classification by grades, withdrawal of customary concessions or privileges, new rules of discipline, rationalisation, standardisation or improvement of plant or technique likely to lead to retrenchment , and increases or reductions in the number of workers. Changes are also restricted while conciliation or adjudication proceedings are pending. Settlements as change vehicles. Major changes on the shop floor, such as new shift patterns, multi-skilling, revised manpower norms or automation, are typically negotiated in long-term wage settlements, in which the union agrees to productivity changes in exchange for wage increases and sometimes job-security commitments (Chapter 10). Example: Tata Steel. Between the early 1990s and the mid-2000s, Tata Steel reduced its workforce from roughly 78,000 to about 44,000 while increasing output, largely through vo"}, {"t": "8.8 Mergers, acquisitions and integration", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-8", "x": "In short People and culture issues are central to whether mergers deliver value. Pay, titles, seniority and leadership roles are the flashpoints. Recall Cultural due diligence Day-one readiness Harmonisation: pay, grades, titles, policies, seniority Retention of key talent Indian cases Phase HR priorities Due diligence Workforce data, liabilities (gratuity, PF, pending disputes), union agreements, key talent, culture assessment Pre-close and day one Leadership appointments, communication to all employees, retention arrangements for critical people, continuity of payroll and benefits Integration Common organisation structure; mapping jobs into one job architecture (Chapter 6); harmonising pay, grades, titles, benefits and policies; integrating systems; merging seniority lists where relevant Culture integration Assessing differences (for example with the Competing Values Framework, Chapter 7); defining the target culture; mixed teams; leaders modelling shared values Indian examples. The consolidation of ten public-sector banks into four (2019–20) required integrating different pay settlements, seniority lists and systems. The merger of HDFC Ltd into HDFC Bank (2023) combined a mortgage company’s and a bank’s cultures and policies. The Air India–Vistara merger (completed November 2024) involved integrating a full-service carrier jointly owned with Singapore Airlines into Air India, with pilots’ seniority and pay harmonisation among the most contested issues."}, {"t": "8.9 Measuring change", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-9", "x": "In short Measure adoption and outcomes, not only project milestones. Recall Readiness surveys ADKAR assessments Adoption metrics Performance outcomes Leading and lagging indicators Measure type Examples Readiness Surveys of understanding, commitment and capacity before launch Individual progress ADKAR assessments by group Adoption (leading indicators) System log-ins, transactions completed in the new process, use of new practices observed by managers, helpdesk ticket trends Outcomes (lagging indicators) The business results the change was meant to deliver: cost, cycle time, quality, engagement, attrition People impact Engagement and pulse scores, attrition in affected groups, grievances"}, {"t": "8.10 Case studies", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-10", "x": "In short Cases illustrate sponsorship, involvement and fit with capability. Recall Microsoft under Nadella United Airlines bonus lottery (2018) McDonald’s Made For You (1998) Case What happened Lesson Microsoft (from 2014) Under Satya Nadella, Microsoft shifted from internal competition (stack ranking was dropped in 2013) to a “growth mindset” culture and a cloud-first strategy, with leaders modelling learning and collaboration Culture change led from the top and reinforced through performance management and hiring United Airlines (2018) The airline announced it would replace quarterly performance bonuses for frontline staff with a lottery offering a small number of large prizes; strong employee backlash led it to pause the plan within days Changing pay without involving employees provokes resistance; an example of equity theory in practice McDonald’s “Made For You” (1998) A new made-to-order kitchen system was installed across US restaurants at large cost; it slowed service, strained crews and franchisees and did not deliver the expected sales gains Change must fit operational capability and what customers value Correction The compendium (p. 91) attributes a 60% crew turnover rate to the “Made For You” programme; no reliable source supports this, and restaurant crew turnover was already high. It is omitted here."}, {"t": "8.11 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 8 · Change Management", "u": "book/08-change-management.html#s-8-11", "x": "In short Use one model to structure your answer, then show practical steps, roles and measures. Explain Kotter’s model with an example. Choose a concrete change, such as an HRIS rollout or a reorganisation, and walk through the eight steps: urgency from data, a guiding coalition, a clear vision, repeated communication, removing obstacles, short-term wins, consolidating and not declaring victory early, and anchoring the change in systems and culture. Note that most failures occur at the first step (weak urgency) and the seventh (stopping too soon). What is the difference between ADKAR and Kotter? Kotter is an organisation-level guide for leaders running a large change. ADKAR is an individual-level model for diagnosing and supporting adoption: it shows whether people lack awareness, desire, knowledge, ability or reinforcement. They are used together: Kotter for the programme, ADKAR for each affected group. How would you introduce a new shift system in a unionised plant? Build the business case with data, then hold early informal discussions with union leaders before any formal step. Give the formal 21-day notice of change required under the IR Code and form a joint committee to design details such as transport, canteen timings and safeguards for women on night shifts. Pilot on one line, review, and roll out, possibly as part of the wage settlement. Communicate on the shop floor in the local language throughout. Managers resist a job-title rationalisation. How do you handle it? Diagnose with ADKAR. Build awareness of the costs of inconsistent titles (pay inconsistencies, inaccurate benchmarking, blocked mobility). Address desire by recognising the real loss, status, and keeping grade and level visible while guaranteeing no pay impact. Provide knowledge through FAQs and man"}, {"t": "9.1 What HR analytics is", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-1", "x": "In short HR analytics uses workforce data and statistical methods to improve people decisions. It progresses from describing what happened to recommending what to do. Recall Metrics vs analytics vs people analytics Descriptive, diagnostic, predictive, prescriptive Bersin’s four levels Why it matters Definitions. HR metrics are measures of HR activity and outcomes, such as attrition rate or time-to-fill. HR analytics (or people analytics ) is the systematic use of workforce data, statistical analysis and increasingly machine learning to understand people-related outcomes and improve decisions. The term “people analytics” is often used for the broader, business-focused practice that combines HR data with operational and financial data. Types of analytics Type Question HR example Typical methods Descriptive What happened? Attrition by department last quarter Reports, dashboards Diagnostic Why did it happen? Attrition is concentrated in three managers’ teams and among employees paid below 85% of their range midpoint Segmentation, correlation, driver analysis, text analysis of exit comments Predictive What is likely to happen? Probability that each employee will leave within six months Logistic regression, survival analysis, machine learning Prescriptive What should we do? Which retention action gives the best return for which group Optimisation, simulation, experiments Maturity. Josh Bersin’s widely used model describes four levels: (1) operational reporting (accurate basic reports), (2) advanced reporting (benchmarks, dashboards, trends), (3) strategic analytics (segmentation, causal analysis, linking people data to business outcomes) and (4) predictive analytics (forecasting and scenario modelling). Most organisations operate at levels 1 and 2; few sustain level 4. Why it"}, {"t": "9.2 The analytics process", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-2", "x": "In short Start with a business question and a decision, not with data. Follow a disciplined process from question to measured action. Recall Seven steps Business question first Hypotheses LAMP (Boudreau & Ramstad) Define the business question and the decision it will inform. “Which plant operators are likely to leave in the next six months, and what would keep them?” is better than “build an attrition dashboard”. Form hypotheses from managers, research and experience: pay below market, shift patterns, commute distance, supervisor behaviour, lack of progression. Gather and prepare data from the HRIS, payroll, attendance, performance, engagement surveys, exit data and external sources; clean, define and join it. Analyse , starting with simple segmentation and visualisation before modelling. Interpret with business context: is the effect large enough to matter, and is it causal? Act : a small number of actions with owners and timelines. Measure the effect , ideally by comparing a pilot group with a similar group that did not receive the intervention. The LAMP framework (John Boudreau and Peter Ramstad) holds that measurement improves decisions only when four elements are present: Logic (a clear model of how people factors drive business outcomes), Analytics (sound methods), Measures (reliable data) and Process (communication and decision processes that turn insight into action)."}, {"t": "9.3 Data foundations", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-3", "x": "In short Analytics depends on accurate, consistently defined and lawfully used data. Most of the effort in real projects goes into data preparation. Recall Data sources Common definitions Quality problems Joining data Privacy by design Source Typical data HRIS / HRMS Employee master data, job, grade, location, manager, tenure, movements Payroll Pay, allowances, increments, bonuses Time and attendance Attendance, leave, overtime, shifts Performance and talent Ratings, goals, potential, succession status Recruitment (ATS) Source, time-to-hire, assessment scores Learning (LMS) Courses, completions, assessments Engagement and surveys Survey scores, comments, eNPS Exit data Exit reasons, interviews Business systems Productivity, sales, quality, safety, customer data Common data problems: inconsistent definitions (does “attrition” include transfers and contract staff?), missing or outdated records, inconsistent job titles (one reason job architecture matters, Chapter 6), different employee identifiers across systems, and small groups that make results unstable or identify individuals. Privacy by design: use only the data needed for the purpose, aggregate where possible, restrict access, and follow the DPDP Act (Section 9.10)."}, {"t": "9.4 Statistical foundations", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-4", "x": "In short A few statistical ideas prevent most analytical mistakes: distributions, correlation versus causation, regression, significance and sample size, and controlled comparison. Recall Mean vs median Correlation ≠ causation Linear vs logistic regression Multicollinearity (VIF) Statistical vs practical significance Confounding; Simpson’s paradox Experiments and control groups Concept Meaning HR relevance Mean and median Average; middle value Pay data is skewed by high earners, so medians are more representative Distribution and variance Spread of values Rating distributions; pay dispersion within grades Correlation Strength of linear association between two variables, from −1 to +1 Engagement and productivity move together, but correlation alone does not show which causes which Causation One variable changes another Established through experiments or careful designs that control other factors Linear regression Models a continuous outcome (such as pay) as a function of several variables Pay-equity analysis Logistic regression Models the probability of a yes/no outcome (such as leaving) Attrition risk models Statistical significance Whether an observed effect is unlikely to be due to chance (often p < 0.05) Small HR samples often produce unstable results Practical significance Whether the effect is large enough to matter A statistically significant 0.5% pay difference may not justify action, while a 10% difference would Confounding A third variable affects both the factor and the outcome Remote workers may appear to leave more because they are newer, not because they are remote Simpson’s paradox A trend in combined data reverses within subgroups Women may appear to be promoted less overall but at equal or higher rates within every function, because they are concentrated"}, {"t": "9.5 Key HR analyses", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-5", "x": "In short The most common analyses are attrition drivers and risk, pay equity, selection validity, absenteeism, engagement–performance links and organisational networks. Recall Attrition: logistic regression, survival analysis Pay equity: regression on log pay Selection validity Bradford factor Engagement–performance ONA Ethical limits Attrition analysis Descriptive and diagnostic: segment attrition by role, tenure, manager, location, pay position, performance and engagement; compare leavers with stayers. Predictive: a logistic regression estimates each employee’s probability of leaving from variables such as tenure, compa-ratio, time since last promotion, rating, manager change, commute distance, overtime and engagement score. The coefficients show which factors increase risk. Survival analysis models when people leave, which is useful for early attrition. Machine-learning models can improve prediction but are harder to explain. Interpretation example. A model shows that operators whose pay is below 90% of the range midpoint and who have had no skill progression in two years have roughly twice the attrition risk of others, holding other factors constant. The actions follow directly: a targeted pay correction and a skills-progression scheme, tested first in one plant. Ethical limit: risk scores should guide support (career conversations, pay review), never exclusion from opportunities, and individuals’ scores should not be shown to managers without safeguards. Pay-equity analysis Regress the logarithm of pay on legitimate factors (grade, job family, experience, performance rating, location) plus a gender indicator. The gender coefficient estimates the adjusted pay gap : the difference not explained by the legitimate factors. A large raw gap with a small adjusted gap poin"}, {"t": "9.6 Metrics and dashboards", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-6", "x": "In short A good dashboard shows a few decision-relevant metrics, with trends, targets and segments, for a specific audience. Recall Core HR metrics and formulas Dashboard design principles Audiences: board, business, HR Limitations Core metrics Area Metric Formula or definition Workforce Headcount and FTE Count at a date; FTE adjusts for part-time staff Retention Attrition rate Separations ÷ average headcount × 100 Retention Regretted attrition Voluntary exits of employees the organisation wanted to keep ÷ average headcount Hiring Time-to-fill; cost-per-hire; quality of hire Chapter 1, Section 1.10 Pay Compa-ratio; personnel cost ratio Salary ÷ midpoint; employee cost ÷ revenue Productivity Revenue per employee; human capital ROI Revenue ÷ FTE; (revenue − (operating expenses − employee costs)) ÷ employee costs Engagement Engagement index; eNPS Chapter 5 Learning Training hours; application rate Chapter 4 Diversity Representation by level; pay gap Share of women and other groups at each level Absence Absenteeism rate Days absent ÷ scheduled working days × 100 Dashboard design Start from decisions. Each metric should prompt a question or action for its audience. Few metrics: 6–10 for a leadership dashboard, with drill-downs for detail. Context: show trends over time, targets or benchmarks, and segments. Consistency: shared definitions across the organisation. Readable design: clear labels, minimal decoration, colour used only to flag exceptions. Audience Typical content Board and CEO Headcount and cost, revenue per employee, regretted attrition, bench strength for critical roles, engagement, safety (for plants), diversity Business leaders Open positions and time-to-fill, attrition by team, performance distribution, overtime, absenteeism HR centres of excellence Recruitmen"}, {"t": "9.7 Well-known cases", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-7", "x": "In short Documented cases show analytics changing beliefs, cutting costs and improving fairness, and also the need to treat vendor claims carefully. Recall Google Project Oxygen (2008–09) Google: four interviews IBM attrition prediction (2019) Salesforce pay audits (from 2015) Case What was done Point it illustrates Google Project Oxygen (2008–09) Google’s people analytics team tested whether managers matter, using performance reviews, feedback surveys and award nominations. They found that managers did affect team performance and retention, and identified eight (later ten) behaviours of effective managers, such as being a good coach, empowering the team without micromanaging and expressing interest in team members’ success. The findings were built into manager feedback and training Analytics can test assumptions and change practice Google’s interview research Analysis of hiring data found that four interviews predicted hiring decisions about as accurately as more, so Google reduced the number of interviews and dropped brain-teaser questions that did not predict performance Analytics reduces cost and improves candidate experience IBM IBM’s then chief executive said in 2019 that the company’s AI could predict which employees were about to leave with about 95% accuracy and had saved about US$300 million in retention costs Prediction has value only when paired with action; the figures are IBM’s own Salesforce Annual pay audits since 2015, adjusting pay for employees where unexplained gaps by gender or ethnicity were found Analytics as a tool for fairness Correction The compendium (pp. 100–101) lists applications attributed to Under Armour, AON, Netflix, LinkedIn and Amazon without sources, most of which could not be verified. The cases above are well documented."}, {"t": "9.8 HR technology", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-8", "x": "In short HR technology runs from core records (HRIS) to process suites (HRMS) and talent platforms (HCM). Implementations succeed when processes are redesigned and adoption is managed. Recall HRIS, HRMS, HCM ATS, LMS/LXP, payroll, engagement tools ESS and MSS Indian vendors Implementation steps Failure reasons Systems System Scope Examples HRIS (human resource information system) Core employee data: records, organisation structure, job and pay history, attendance and leave Core modules of most suites HRMS (human resource management system) HRIS plus processes: recruitment, performance, learning, payroll integration Darwinbox, Keka, Zoho People HCM (human capital management) HRMS plus strategic talent management: succession, workforce planning, analytics Workday HCM, SAP SuccessFactors, Oracle HCM ATS Recruitment workflow Greenhouse, Lever, Darwinbox Recruit LMS and LXP Learning administration and experience (Chapter 4) Cornerstone, Degreed Engagement and listening platforms Surveys, pulses, recognition Workday Peakon, inFeedo Amber, Vantage Circle ESS and MSS Employee and manager self-service Leave, payslips, approvals, profile updates on mobile HR chatbots and assistants Answer queries, guide transactions McDonald’s “Olivia” for hourly hiring; IBM’s AI assistant for HR queries The labels HRIS, HRMS and HCM overlap in vendors’ marketing; the useful distinction is between core records, process automation and strategic talent management. India context Indian HR technology has grown rapidly. Darwinbox (Hyderabad), a unicorn since 2022, serves large Indian groups and has expanded across Asia; Keka, greytHR, Zoho People, PeopleStrong and ZingHR serve many mid-sized firms. Indian payroll is complex (state professional tax and labour welfare funds, PF and ESI, two tax regimes,"}, {"t": "9.9 Artificial intelligence in HR", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-9", "x": "In short AI can make HR faster and more personalised, but it can also scale bias, invade privacy and make decisions people cannot explain. Governance keeps humans accountable. Recall Predictive ML vs generative AI vs agents Uses across the lifecycle Risks: bias, opacity, hallucination, privacy, surveillance Governance principles EU AI Act; NYC LL144; India AI Governance Guidelines Types. Predictive machine learning estimates outcomes from data (attrition risk, candidate match). Generative AI produces text and other content (job descriptions, policies, summaries, learning content, answers to employee questions). AI agents carry out multi-step tasks such as scheduling interviews or processing routine requests. Lifecycle stage Uses Recruitment CV screening and matching, chatbots, interview scheduling, JD drafting (Chapter 1) Onboarding and service HR assistants answering policy questions, guided transactions Learning Personalised recommendations, AI tutors, content creation Performance and engagement Feedback summaries, sentiment analysis of comments, rating-pattern checks Workforce planning Skills inference, demand forecasting, attrition prediction Risks. Risk Example Mitigation Bias Amazon’s experimental CV-screening tool learned to penalise CVs mentioning “women’s” (abandoned in 2018) Test outcomes by group before and after deployment; remove proxies for protected characteristics Opacity Candidates or employees cannot understand why a decision was made Prefer explainable models for decisions about people; document logic Inaccuracy (hallucination) A generative AI assistant gives a wrong answer on leave policy Ground answers in approved policy documents; human review for sensitive queries Privacy and confidentiality Employee data entered into public AI tools Approved ente"}, {"t": "9.10 The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-10", "x": "In short The DPDP Act governs digital personal data in India. Employers may process employee data for employment purposes without consent as a legitimate use, but must keep it secure, limited to purpose, and report breaches. Recall Data principal, fiduciary, processor Consent vs legitimate use (s.7) Notice Duties and rights Breach: 72-hour report (Rules) SDF and DPO Penalties up to ₹250 crore Rules: 13 Nov 2025; phased to ~May 2027 Key concepts Concept Meaning for HR Data principal The person the data is about: employee, candidate, former employee Data fiduciary The organisation deciding why and how data is processed: the employer Data processor An entity processing data on the fiduciary’s behalf: payroll provider, HRMS vendor, background-verification agency Scope Digital personal data, and personal data collected in non-digital form and later digitised Lawful grounds Personal data may be processed either with consent (free, specific, informed, unconditional and unambiguous, given after a notice) or for a legitimate use listed in section 7. One legitimate use is processing for the purposes of employment , or to safeguard the employer from loss or liability, for example to prevent corporate espionage, maintain confidentiality of trade secrets or provide benefits to employees. Processing payroll, attendance and performance data therefore does not require separate consent. Uses outside employment purposes, such as using employees’ photographs in marketing, require consent. Obligations of the employer as data fiduciary Give a clear notice of what data is collected and why, where consent is the basis. Keep data accurate and complete where it is used for decisions. Implement reasonable security safeguards . Erase data when the purpose is served and retention is no longer requ"}, {"t": "9.11 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 9 · HR Analytics, HR Technology and AI", "u": "book/09-hr-analytics-hr-tech-and-ai.html#s-9-11", "x": "In short Show that you start from a business question, understand the limits of data and models, and know the legal position. How would you use analytics to reduce attrition? Define the question and decision, segment attrition by role, tenure, manager and pay position, and test hypotheses with data from the HRIS, payroll, ratings, surveys and exit interviews. Build a logistic regression to identify drivers and risk, then design targeted actions such as pay corrections, manager coaching or career moves, pilot them, and measure regretted attrition against a comparison group. Use risk scores to offer support, never to exclude people. What is the difference between HRIS, HRMS and HCM? An HRIS manages core employee records, an HRMS adds HR processes such as recruitment, performance and learning, and an HCM suite adds strategic talent management such as succession, workforce planning and analytics. The labels overlap in vendor marketing. Do we need employees’ consent to process payroll data under the DPDP Act? No. Processing for employment purposes is a legitimate use under section 7. The employer must still keep the data secure and accurate, use it only for that purpose, delete it when no longer needed, bind vendors by contract and report breaches. Consent is needed for uses outside employment purposes. What could go wrong with an AI model that predicts attrition? It may learn bias from historical data or use proxies for protected characteristics, such as leave patterns that correlate with gender. Managers may stop investing in people flagged as likely to leave, making the prediction self-fulfilling. Sensitive data may be exposed, and trust may suffer if employees discover it. Governance, bias testing, transparency and human decisions reduce these risks. Will AI replace HR p"}, {"t": "10.1 What industrial relations is", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-1", "x": "In short Industrial relations is the relationship between employers, workers and their unions, and the State, covering terms of employment, collective representation and the resolution of conflict. Recall IR vs employee relations Actors: employers, workers/unions, State Unitary, pluralist, radical approaches Dunlop’s systems theory Where IR is strong in India Definition. Industrial relations (IR) is the system of relationships between employers, workers and their organisations (trade unions), and the State, through which terms and conditions of employment are set, collective interests are represented, and conflicts are resolved. Industrial relations and employee relations. IR traditionally refers to the collective relationship: unions, collective bargaining, disputes, strikes and settlements. Employee relations (ER) is broader and includes individual and non-union relationships: communication, grievances, discipline, fairness and well-being. In Indian IT and services, ER dominates; in manufacturing, mining, ports, banking, transport and the public sector, collective IR remains central. Theoretical approaches Approach View of the organisation and conflict Implication for practice Unitary The organisation is a single team with common goals; conflict is abnormal, caused by poor communication or troublemakers Unions are seen as unnecessary; emphasis on direct communication and loyalty. Common in new-economy firms Pluralist The organisation contains groups with different, legitimate interests; conflict is normal and should be managed through institutions Recognised unions, collective bargaining, agreed procedures. The basis of Indian labour law Radical (Marxist) Conflict reflects a fundamental divide between capital and labour in society Explains politically affiliated union"}, {"t": "10.2 Constitutional and legal foundations", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-2", "x": "In short Indian labour law rests on Fundamental Rights, the Directive Principles of State Policy and the Concurrent List. India has not ratified the ILO conventions on freedom of association and collective bargaining. Recall Art. 14, 16, 19(1)(c), 21, 23, 24 DPSPs: Art. 38, 39, 41, 42, 43, 43A Concurrent List ILO Conventions 87 and 98 not ratified Fundamental Rights (enforceable in courts) Article Content Relevance to labour 14 Equality before law Basis for challenging arbitrary action by State employers 16 Equality of opportunity in public employment Public-sector recruitment and promotion 19(1)(c) Freedom to form associations and unions Right to form unions; the Supreme Court has held that this does not include a fundamental right to strike, which is regulated by statute 21 Right to life and personal liberty Interpreted to include livelihood and dignity 23 Prohibition of forced labour Bonded labour, unpaid work 24 No employment of children below 14 in factories, mines or hazardous work Child labour laws Directive Principles of State Policy (guide legislation; not enforceable in courts) Article Content 38 Social order based on justice 39 Adequate livelihood; equal pay for equal work for men and women; protection of workers’ health 41 Right to work, education and public assistance in certain cases 42 Just and humane conditions of work and maternity relief 43 Living wage and decent standard of life 43A Participation of workers in the management of industries Labour is in the Concurrent List (entries on trade unions, industrial and labour disputes, social security, and welfare of labour), so both Parliament and state legislatures can make laws, and states frame rules under the central Codes. This is why compliance can differ between states. International standards. India "}, {"t": "10.3 The Industrial Relations Code: scope and key definitions", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-3", "x": "In short The IR Code merged three laws on unions, standing orders and industrial disputes. Its definitions of “industry” and “worker” decide who is covered. Recall Merged: TU Act 1926, Standing Orders Act 1946, ID Act 1947 Industry: Rajappa triple test + exclusions Worker: excludes managers and supervisors above ₹18,000 Industrial dispute includes individual termination The Industrial Relations Code, 2020 consolidated the Trade Unions Act, 1926 , the Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Act, 1946 and the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947 . It covers four areas: organisation of workers, conditions of employment, settlement of disputes, and workforce restructuring (lay-off, retrenchment and closure). “Industry” Most IR provisions apply only to an industry . In Bangalore Water Supply and Sewerage Board v. A. Rajappa (1978) , a seven-judge bench of the Supreme Court gave a wide definition through the triple test : an activity is an industry if it is (1) a systematic activity, (2) organised by cooperation between employer and employees, (3) for the production or distribution of goods or services to satisfy human wants. Profit motive and capital investment are irrelevant. Where an organisation carries on several activities, the dominant nature of the activity decides. The IR Code codifies this broad approach but excludes : institutions owned or managed by organisations wholly or substantially engaged in charitable, social or philanthropic service; sovereign functions of the government (including defence, atomic energy and space); domestic service; and any other activity notified by the government. Correction The compendium (p. 118) states that charitable organisations may qualify as industries. That was true under Rajappa , but the IR Code now excludes institutions wholly or s"}, {"t": "10.4 Trade unions", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-4", "x": "In short Registered unions have legal status and immunities. The IR Code introduced union recognition: a sole negotiating union at 51% membership, otherwise a negotiating council of unions with at least 20%. Recall Madras Labour Union 1918; AITUC 1920 BMS, INTUC, CITU, AITUC, HMS Registration: 10% or 100 (min. 7) Immunities Recognition: 51% / 20% Unfair labour practices Definition. A trade union is an association of workers (or employers) formed primarily to regulate relations between workers and employers and to protect and advance members’ interests. History. The Madras Labour Union , founded in 1918 with B. P. Wadia’s leadership, is regarded as India’s first organised trade union, and the All India Trade Union Congress (AITUC) was founded in 1920. Most large union federations are affiliated with political parties: Bharatiya Mazdoor Sangh (BMS) , the largest, linked to the RSS; INTUC (Congress); CITU (CPI-M); AITUC (CPI); and Hind Mazdoor Sabha (HMS) . Plants often have an internal union, a union affiliated with a federation, or several competing unions. Registration and status Element IR Code position Minimum membership for registration At least 10% of workers or 100 workers , whichever is less, with a minimum of 7 members Office-bearers At least half must be workers actually engaged in the establishment or industry, with limited exceptions Legal status A registered union is a body corporate that can hold property and sue or be sued Immunities Protection from criminal conspiracy charges and civil suits for acts done in furtherance of a trade dispute, provided they are lawful Recognition of negotiating unions Before the Code, there was no central law requiring an employer to recognise a union for bargaining (some states, such as Maharashtra, had their own provisions)."}, {"t": "10.5 Collective bargaining and settlements", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-5", "x": "In short Collective bargaining is negotiation between employers and unions over terms of employment. It produces settlements, usually for three to four years, increasingly linked to productivity. Recall Charter of demands Distributive vs integrative bargaining Bipartite settlement vs settlement in conciliation Long-term settlements Preparation data Definition. Collective bargaining is the process by which employers and workers’ representatives negotiate wages, working conditions and other terms of employment, and the rules that govern their relationship. Process Stage What happens Charter of demands The union submits written demands, typically wages, allowances, incentives, leave, welfare, manpower and job security, before the current settlement expires Preparation Management analyses each demand: cost, regional and industry wage comparisons, productivity trends, ability to pay; prepares its own proposals Negotiation Meetings between the management team (plant head, HR, finance) and union representatives; positions narrow through trade-offs Agreement A written settlement signed by both parties, specifying terms and duration Implementation and administration Applying the settlement, resolving interpretation disputes Types of bargaining (Walton and McKersie). Distributive bargaining divides a fixed amount: what one side gains, the other loses, typically on wage increases. Integrative bargaining seeks solutions that benefit both: for example, higher wages in exchange for multi-skilling, revised shift patterns or productivity improvements that reduce cost per unit. Settlements Type How it is made Who it binds Bipartite settlement Agreed directly between employer and union The parties to the agreement Settlement in conciliation Agreed during conciliation proceedings, signed "}, {"t": "10.6 Standing orders", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-6", "x": "In short Standing orders are the formal conditions of employment for workers: classification, hours, leave, misconduct, discipline and termination. Under the IR Code they apply to establishments with 300 or more workers. Recall Threshold 300 (was 100) Contents Model Standing Orders, 2026 Schedules A, B, C Definition. Standing orders are written rules defining the conditions of employment of workers in an industrial establishment, so that both employer and workers know their rights and obligations. Contents typically include: classification of workers (permanent, probationer, badli or substitute, temporary, casual, apprentice, fixed-term); working hours, shifts and holidays; attendance and late arrival; leave; acts of misconduct ; the disciplinary procedure , including suspension and punishments; termination and notice; and the grievance procedure. Aspect Standing Orders Act, 1946 IR Code, 2020 Applicability Establishments with 100 or more workers Establishments with 300 or more workers Model standing orders Central and state models Model Standing Orders, 2026 : Schedule A (mines), B (manufacturing), C (services); employers may adopt them Fixed-term employment Recognised only through amendments Statutory category Many establishments below 300 workers continue to maintain standing orders or equivalent service rules, because clear rules support fair discipline and reduce disputes."}, {"t": "10.7 Discipline and domestic enquiry", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-7", "x": "In short Disciplinary action against workers must follow the standing orders and the principles of natural justice. A defective enquiry is the most common reason dismissals are overturned. Recall Misconduct: minor and major Charge-sheet, enquiry, findings, second show-cause, order Natural justice Subsistence allowance 50% → 75% Proportionate punishment Hot-stove rule Misconduct is conduct that breaches the standing orders, service rules or the employment relationship. Standing orders list acts of misconduct, typically including wilful insubordination, theft or fraud, wilful damage to property, habitual absence or lateness, assault or threatening behaviour, drunkenness on duty, sexual harassment, participating in an illegal strike, and habitual negligence. Steps in a domestic enquiry Step Purpose and key requirements 1. Complaint or report Written record of the incident 2. Preliminary inquiry Fact-finding to decide whether there is a case 3. Suspension pending enquiry (if needed) Used for serious misconduct. Subsistence allowance : 50% of wages for the first 90 days and 75% thereafter, if the delay is not caused by the worker 4. Charge-sheet / show-cause notice Specific allegations with dates and details; reasonable time to reply 5. Domestic enquiry Held if the reply is unsatisfactory. An independent enquiry officer conducts it; a presenting officer presents management’s case; the worker may have a defence representative (often a co-worker or union representative); witnesses are examined and cross-examined; documents are shown to the worker; proceedings are recorded 6. Findings Enquiry officer’s report on whether charges are proved, with reasons 7. Second show-cause Worker is given the findings and the proposed punishment and invited to respond (required by many standing"}, {"t": "10.8 Grievance handling and bipartite forums", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-8", "x": "In short Grievances are handled first inside the organisation. The IR Code requires a Grievance Redressal Committee at 20 or more workers and a Works Committee at 100 or more. Recall Grievance vs dispute GRC: 20+, ≤10 members, 30 days Works Committee: 100+, ≤20 members Escalation to conciliation A grievance is an individual’s complaint about employment (pay, leave, transfer, treatment, working conditions). If unresolved it can become a dispute . Effective grievance handling resolves issues early and close to the workplace, which prevents escalation. Forum Applicability Composition and process Grievance Redressal Committee (GRC) Industrial establishments with 20 or more workers Equal representation of employer and workers, not more than 10 members , with women represented in proportion to their share of the workforce (central rules). Resolves individual grievances, generally within 30 days ; an unresolved grievance can go to conciliation Works Committee Establishments with 100 or more workers Up to 20 members , with worker representatives not fewer than employer representatives. Promotes cooperation and discusses common day-to-day issues; it does not replace collective bargaining Good practice in grievance handling: a written procedure with time limits at each level (supervisor, department head, GRC), listening and investigating before deciding, giving reasons, keeping records, and analysing grievances for patterns that indicate wider problems."}, {"t": "10.9 Dispute-resolution machinery", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-9", "x": "In short Disputes move from internal forums to conciliation, then to voluntary arbitration or adjudication by Industrial Tribunals. The IR Code abolished Labour Courts and Boards of Conciliation. Recall Conciliation officer Voluntary arbitration Industrial Tribunal (two members) National Industrial Tribunal Labour Courts abolished Individual direct access Level Nature Role GRC and Works Committee Internal, bipartite Resolve individual grievances and day-to-day issues Conciliation officer Government officer, mediation Helps the parties reach a settlement; if conciliation fails, submits a failure report Voluntary arbitration Arbitrator chosen by both parties by written agreement Binding award Industrial Tribunal Adjudication; two members , one judicial and one administrative, with provision for single-member benches Decides disputes referred to it or brought by workers; its award is binding National Industrial Tribunal Constituted by the Central Government Disputes of national importance or affecting establishments in more than one state Changes under the IR Code. Labour Courts, Boards of Conciliation and Courts of Inquiry were abolished ; adjudication is now by Industrial Tribunals. An individual worker with a dispute over discharge, dismissal, retrenchment or termination can apply directly to the Tribunal after the conciliation period has passed without settlement, rather than waiting for the government to refer the dispute, subject to a limitation period. Who approaches whom. An individual grievance goes first to the GRC. Day-to-day cooperation issues go to the Works Committee. A collective dispute goes to the conciliation officer. A failed conciliation leads to arbitration (if both agree) or the Industrial Tribunal. Disputes of national scope go to the National Indust"}, {"t": "10.10 Strikes and lockouts", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-10", "x": "In short Strikes and lockouts are lawful only if notice and timing conditions are met. The IR Code extended notice requirements to all industrial establishments and treats mass casual leave as a strike. Recall Definitions; forms of strike Notice within 60 days; not within 14 days Not during conciliation + 7 days; tribunal/arbitration + 60 days Mass casual leave (50%+) = strike Illegal strike: no wages Managing a strike Definitions. A strike is a cessation of work by a body of workers acting in combination, or a concerted refusal to work. Under the IR Code it includes concerted casual leave by 50% or more of the workers on a given day . A lockout is the temporary closure of a place of employment, suspension of work or refusal by an employer to continue employing workers, used as pressure in a dispute. Forms of strike. General strike; sit-down or stay-in strike (workers occupy the premises without working); tool-down or pen-down strike; go-slow (deliberately reducing output while remaining at work, generally treated as misconduct); work-to-rule (following every rule exactly to slow work); token strike (short, symbolic); sympathetic strike (supporting other workers); hunger strike. A gherao (physically confining managers) is unlawful. Conditions for a lawful strike or lockout (all industrial establishments) Condition Rule Notice Must be given within 60 days before the strike or lockout Waiting period No strike or lockout within 14 days of giving notice, or before the date specified in the notice During conciliation Prohibited while conciliation is pending and for 7 days after it ends During adjudication or arbitration Prohibited while proceedings are pending before a Tribunal or arbitrator and for 60 days after they conclude During a settlement or award Prohibited on matte"}, {"t": "10.11 Lay-off, retrenchment and closure", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-11", "x": "In short Lay-off is temporary inability to provide work; retrenchment is termination for reasons other than punishment; closure is permanent shutdown. Government permission is needed at 300 or more workers. A reskilling-fund payment is added to retrenchment compensation. Recall Definitions and exclusions Lay-off: 50% of basic + DA Retrenchment: 1 month notice, 15 days per year, LIFO 300+: permission, 3 months’ notice Reskilling fund: 15 days’ wages Re-employment preference (1 year) Definitions Lay-off is the failure, refusal or inability of an employer to give employment to a worker on the muster roll because of shortage of coal, power or raw materials, accumulation of stocks, breakdown of machinery, natural calamity or similar reasons. The employment relationship continues. Retrenchment is termination of a worker’s service by the employer for any reason other than punishment through disciplinary action. It does not include : voluntary retirement; superannuation; non-renewal of a fixed-term contract on expiry; and termination on grounds of continued ill-health. Closure is the permanent closing down of a place of employment or part of it. Comparison Lay-off Retrenchment Closure Nature Temporary Permanent, for individuals Permanent, for the establishment or part Employment relationship Continues Ends for retrenched workers Ends for all affected workers Compensation 50% of basic + DA for days laid off (generally limited to 45 days in 12 months unless otherwise agreed) 15 days’ average pay for each completed year of continuous service (or part over six months) Same as retrenchment Conditions (establishments below 300 workers) One month’s notice or pay in lieu; notice to the appropriate government; last in, first out within a category unless reasons are recorded 60 days’ not"}, {"t": "10.12 Fixed-term employment", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-12", "x": "In short Fixed-term employment is direct employment for a defined period, with the same pay and benefits as permanent workers and gratuity after one year. Recall Written contract, direct employment Parity in hours, wages, benefits Gratuity after 1 year, pro rata Non-renewal ≠ retrenchment Definition. Fixed-term employment (FTE) is the engagement of a worker directly by the employer, under a written contract, for a fixed period. Conditions under the IR Code: hours of work, wages, allowances and other benefits must be no less favourable than those of permanent workers doing the same or similar work; fixed-term workers are eligible for statutory benefits in proportion to their service; and they are eligible for gratuity after one year of service. Non-renewal on expiry is not retrenchment. Why it matters. FTE gives employers flexibility for seasonal demand, projects and ramp-ups without using contractors, while giving workers parity of pay and benefits. Unions are concerned that it can replace permanent jobs with successive short contracts; repeated renewals for permanent, perennial work carry legal and IR risk."}, {"t": "10.13 Workers’ participation in management", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-13", "x": "In short Article 43A directs the State to secure workers’ participation in management. In practice it takes the form of works committees, joint councils, worker directors and consultation. Recall Art. 43A Levels: information, consultation, joint decision Works committees, joint management councils Worker directors in some PSUs Workers’ participation in management (WPM) ranges from information sharing, through consultation, to joint decision-making. Indian forms include works committees (statutory), joint management councils and shop councils (introduced through government schemes from the 1950s and 1970s), worker representatives on boards in some public-sector undertakings, and joint committees on safety, productivity and canteens. WPM has had limited success where unions are politically divided or management treats forums as formalities; it works where forums have real decisions to influence, such as safety or shift design."}, {"t": "10.14 Lessons from Indian IR cases", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-14", "x": "In short Major Indian disputes show recurring causes: blocked union recognition, pay gaps between permanent and contract workers, wage non-payment and weak grievance channels. Recall Maruti Manesar 2012 Honda Manesar 2005 Wistron Kolar 2020 Samsung Sriperumbudur 2024 Tata Steel: industrial harmony Case What happened Lessons for HR Maruti Suzuki, Manesar (2012) After months of tension over registration of a new union and the large share of contract workers on lower pay, violence broke out in July 2012; HR manager Awanish Kumar Dev was killed and part of the plant burned. In 2017 a court convicted 31 workers, 13 of them to life imprisonment Pay disparity between contract and permanent workers doing similar work, obstruction of union recognition and weak grievance channels create explosive conditions; early warning signs must be acted on Honda Motorcycle and Scooter, Manesar (2005) Disputes over union formation and dismissals led to violent clashes between workers and police Respecting the right to organise prevents escalation Wistron, Kolar (December 2020) Workers at an iPhone assembly plant, many engaged through contractors, rioted over unpaid and short-paid wages and overtime; Apple placed the supplier on probation Principal employers are accountable for contractors’ wage compliance; payroll accuracy is an IR issue Samsung, Sriperumbudur (September–October 2024) A strike lasting over a month, led by a union affiliated with CITU, over wages and union recognition; ended after mediation by the Tamil Nadu government; the registration question went to the Madras High Court Union recognition is the central IR issue in many newer multinational plants Tata Steel Long record of industrial harmony built on joint consultation with the Tata Workers’ Union and extensive welfare; lar"}, {"t": "10.15 Old law and the IR Code: summary", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-15", "x": "In short The Code increased flexibility for employers on thresholds, fixed-term employment and strike notice, and added recognition, a reskilling fund and faster adjudication for workers. Aspect Earlier law IR Code, 2020 Laws TU Act 1926, Standing Orders Act 1946, ID Act 1947 One Code Union recognition No central provision Sole negotiating union at 51%; negotiating council of unions with 20%+ Standing orders 100+ workers 300+ workers; Model Standing Orders, 2026 Permission for lay-off, retrenchment, closure 100+ workers 300+ workers (states may raise) Strike and lockout notice Mainly public utility services All industrial establishments; mass casual leave counts as a strike Supervisor wage limit for “worker” ₹10,000 ₹18,000 Fixed-term employment By notification Statutory, with parity and gratuity after one year Reskilling fund None 15 days’ wages per retrenched worker Adjudication Labour Courts, Tribunals, Boards Two-member Industrial Tribunals; Labour Courts abolished Grievance Redressal Committee 20+ workers (from 2010 amendment) 20+ workers; up to 10 members Penalties Imprisonment for many offences Mainly fines; compounding of minor first offences Notice of change in service conditions 21 days (section 9A) 21 days (section 40)"}, {"t": "10.16 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 10 · Industrial Relations and the Industrial Relations Code", "u": "book/10-industrial-relations-and-the-ir-code.html#s-10-16", "x": "In short IR questions test legal accuracy and practical judgement. Give the rule, then show how you would handle the situation. What is the difference between lay-off, retrenchment and closure? Lay-off is a temporary inability to provide work, for reasons such as power or raw-material shortage, and the employment relationship continues; compensation is 50% of basic and DA. Retrenchment is permanent termination for reasons other than disciplinary punishment, requiring a month’s notice, 15 days’ pay per completed year, last-in-first-out and a reskilling-fund payment. Closure is the permanent shutdown of the establishment. Establishments with 300 or more workers need prior government permission for all three. How is a union recognised under the IR Code? A union with 51% or more of the workers on the muster roll as members becomes the sole negotiating union. If none reaches 51%, a negotiating council is formed of unions that each have at least 20%. Membership is verified under the rules. It gives the employer one bargaining counterpart and reduces inter-union rivalry. Walk me through a domestic enquiry. After a preliminary inquiry, the worker receives a charge-sheet with specific allegations and time to reply, and may be suspended on subsistence allowance. An impartial enquiry officer holds the enquiry, with a presenting officer for management and a defence representative for the worker; witnesses are examined and cross-examined and documents disclosed. The officer records findings with reasons, the worker gets a second show-cause notice with the proposed punishment, and the final order must be proportionate. Natural justice throughout is essential, because defective enquiries are the main reason dismissals are set aside. Is the IR Code pro-employer or pro-worker? It increa"}, {"t": "11.1 Why the Labour Codes, and where implementation stands", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-1", "x": "In short Four Codes replaced 29 central laws to simplify definitions, registrations and compliance, extend coverage and modernise enforcement. They are in force; rules are still being completed at state level. Recall Second National Commission on Labour (2002) 29 laws → 4 Codes Wages 4, IR 3, SS 9, OSH 13 21 Nov 2025; central rules 8 May 2026 Concurrent subject: state rules The problem the Codes address. Before the Codes, India had over 40 central labour laws and many more state laws, enacted at different times since the 1920s. They used different definitions of “wages”, “employee” and “establishment”, different thresholds and separate registrations, returns and inspections. Coverage was patchy: minimum wages applied only to scheduled employments, and most social security applied only to the organised sector. Employers faced complex compliance, and many workers had little protection. The reform. The Second National Commission on Labour (2002) recommended grouping labour laws into a few functional codes. After consultations, Parliament passed the Code on Wages in 2019 and the Industrial Relations, Social Security, and Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Codes in September 2020 . Code Laws merged Main subject Code on Wages, 2019 4 Minimum wages, payment of wages, bonus, equal remuneration Industrial Relations Code, 2020 3 Unions, standing orders, disputes, strikes, retrenchment (Chapter 10) Code on Social Security, 2020 9 PF, ESI, gratuity, maternity, employee compensation, unorganised, gig and platform workers OSH Code, 2020 13 Safety, health, working hours, leave, welfare, contract labour, migrant workers Timeline Date Event 2019 Code on Wages passed September 2020 IR, SS and OSH Codes passed 21 November 2025 All four Codes brought into force; 29 central "}, {"t": "11.2 Code on Wages, 2019", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-2", "x": "In short The Code on Wages gives every employee a minimum wage, introduces a national floor wage, applies one definition of wages, regulates payment and deductions, and bars gender discrimination in pay. Recall Merged 4 laws Universal minimum wage Floor wage Wages definition and 50% rule Payment by the 7th; F&F in 2 days Deductions ≤ 50%; OT 2× Inspector-cum-facilitator Laws merged: Payment of Wages Act, 1936; Minimum Wages Act, 1948; Payment of Bonus Act, 1965; Equal Remuneration Act, 1976. Coverage The Code applies to all employees in all establishments . Earlier, minimum wages applied only to “scheduled employments” listed by governments, leaving a large share of workers outside statutory protection, and the Payment of Wages Act applied only to employees below a wage ceiling (₹24,000 a month in its final form). Removing these limits is the Code’s main change. Definition of wages Wages include basic pay, dearness allowance and retaining allowance, and exclude items such as HRA, conveyance, statutory bonus, overtime, commission, employer PF and gratuity. If the excluded items exceed 50% of total remuneration , the excess is treated as wages. The worked example and its effects on salary structures are in Chapter 2, Section 2.12. Minimum wages and the floor wage Element Rule Who fixes minimum wages The appropriate government (mostly state governments) Basis Skill category (unskilled, semi-skilled, skilled, highly skilled), geographical area, and working conditions such as hazardous or underground work Unit Under the central rules, a daily rate; the hourly rate is the daily rate divided by 8, and the monthly rate is the daily rate multiplied by 26 Revision At intervals not exceeding five years, with periodic adjustment for cost of living (variable dearness allowance) Floo"}, {"t": "11.3 Code on Social Security, 2020", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-3", "x": "In short The Social Security Code consolidates nine laws, keeps the contributory schemes (PF, ESI), employer liabilities (gratuity, maternity, employee compensation), and extends social security to unorganised, gig and platform workers. Recall 9 laws merged EPF 20+, ₹25,000 ceiling ESI 10+, ₹21,000 Gratuity: FTE after 1 year Maternity 26 weeks; crèche 50+ Employee compensation Gig: 1–2% turnover, cap 5% e-Shram; 90/120 days Laws merged: Employees’ Compensation Act, 1923; Employees’ State Insurance Act, 1948; Employees’ Provident Funds and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1952; Employment Exchanges (Compulsory Notification of Vacancies) Act, 1959; Maternity Benefit Act, 1961; Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972; Cine-Workers Welfare Fund Act, 1981; Building and Other Construction Workers Welfare Cess Act, 1996; Unorganised Workers’ Social Security Act, 2008. Structure of social security under the Code Type Schemes Funded by Contributory social insurance EPF, EPS and EDLI (EPFO); ESI (ESIC) Employer and employee contributions Employer liabilities Gratuity; maternity benefit (where ESI does not apply); employee compensation for injury (where ESI does not apply) Employer Welfare funds and schemes Building and construction workers (cess of 1% of construction cost), cine workers, unorganised workers, gig and platform workers Cess, aggregator contributions, government Provident fund and ESI EPF / EPS / EDLI ESI Applies to Establishments with 20+ employees Establishments with 10+ employees Covered employees Mandatory for PF wages up to ₹25,000/month from 17 September 2026 (previously ₹15,000) Gross wages up to ₹21,000/month (₹25,000 for persons with disabilities) Contributions Employee 12%; employer 12% (8.33% to EPS on wages up to the ceiling, the rest to EPF), plus EDLI 0.5% and admin"}, {"t": "11.4 Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-4", "x": "In short The OSH Code sets common rules for safety, health, welfare, working hours and leave across establishments, with a single registration. It introduced mandatory appointment letters and allowed women to work at night with safeguards. Recall 13 laws merged 10+ workers; factory 20/40 Single registration Appointment letters 8-hour day, 48-hour week OT 2×, consent, 144 hrs/quarter Annual leave: 180 days → 1 per 20 Women at night: consent + safeguards Laws merged include the Factories Act, 1948; Mines Act, 1952; Plantations Labour Act, 1951; Contract Labour (Regulation and Abolition) Act, 1970; Inter-State Migrant Workmen Act, 1979; Building and Other Construction Workers Act, 1996; Dock Workers Act, 1986; Working Journalists Act, 1955; Motor Transport Workers Act, 1961; Sales Promotion Employees Act, 1976; and Beedi and Cigar Workers Act, 1966, among others (13 in all). Coverage and registration Element Rule Establishments covered Those employing 10 or more workers , and all mines and docks regardless of size Factory Premises where a manufacturing process is carried on with 20 or more workers with power, or 40 or more without power (previously 10 and 20 under the Factories Act) Registration One registration for an establishment, replacing multiple registrations under separate laws Licence for contractors A single licence, which can be valid in more than one state or across India Employer duties Provide a workplace free from hazards that cause or are likely to cause injury or occupational disease. Issue an appointment letter to every employee, in the prescribed format, stating details such as designation, category, wages and social-security entitlements. This applies to contract and fixed-term workers as well. Provide free annual health check-ups for specified workers;"}, {"t": "11.5 Contract labour and inter-state migrant workers", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-5", "x": "In short Contract labour is regulated when 50 or more contract workers are engaged. The principal employer remains liable for wages and amenities if the contractor defaults, and contract labour is barred in core activities with exceptions. Recall Principal employer vs contractor Threshold 50 (was 20) Single pan-India licence Core activities bar + exceptions Vicarious liability for wages 2% annual increment SAIL (2001): no automatic absorption Migrant workers: ≤ ₹18,000, journey allowance Definitions. Contract labour are workers employed in an establishment through a contractor , who has the direct employment relationship with them. The principal employer is the owner or occupier of the establishment where they work. Changes from the CLRA Act Aspect Contract Labour (R&A) Act, 1970 OSH Code, 2020 Applicability Establishments or contractors with 20+ contract workers 50+ contract workers Licence Separate licences per establishment and state Option of a single licence valid across states or India Core activities Government could prohibit contract labour in particular processes after examining criteria Contract labour prohibited in core activities , with exceptions: where such work is normally done through contractors in that industry, where the activity does not require full-time workers for most of the working hours, or where there is a sudden increase in work that must be completed in a specified time Contract workers regularly employed by contractors No specific provision Workers employed regularly by a contractor under agreed terms are excluded from the definition of contract labour; under the central rules they must receive an annual increment of at least 2% Principal employer’s obligations Wage liability: the contractor pays wages, but if the contractor fails to pay in"}, {"t": "11.6 Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-6", "x": "In short The POSH Act requires every employer with 10 or more employees to have an Internal Committee and a time-bound process for complaints of sexual harassment against women. It remains a separate law outside the Codes. Recall Vishaka (1997) Aggrieved woman; wide workplace Definition; impact not intent IC at 10+; composition Local Committee 3 months; 90/10/60/90 days Interim relief; confidentiality ₹50,000 penalty Aureliano Fernandes (2023) Origin. In Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997) , after the gang-rape of Bhanwari Devi, a social worker, while she was trying to stop a child marriage, the Supreme Court laid down guidelines on preventing sexual harassment at work, treating it as a violation of fundamental rights. The POSH Act, 2013 gave these guidelines statutory form. Scope and definitions Element Meaning Aggrieved woman A woman of any age, whether employed or not, who alleges sexual harassment at a workplace. The Act protects women; men and other genders are protected only by company policies that go further Employee Includes regular, temporary, ad hoc, daily-wage, contract and fixed-term workers, trainees, apprentices and interns, paid or unpaid Workplace Broad: includes any place visited by the employee in the course of employment, including transport provided by the employer, client sites, off-sites and, in practice, virtual workspaces Sexual harassment Unwelcome acts or behaviour, directly or by implication: physical contact and advances; a demand or request for sexual favours; sexually coloured remarks; showing pornography; any other unwelcome physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature. It also covers implied or explicit promises of preferential treatment, threats about employment, interference with work or creation of a hostile environment,"}, {"t": "11.7 Other laws HR must know", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-7", "x": "In short Several laws remain outside the Codes and regularly appear in HR work. Recall Apprentices Act, 1961 Shops & Establishments Acts Child and Adolescent Labour Act RPwD Act, 2016 Transgender Persons Act, 2019 DPDP Act, 2023 Law Key provisions for HR Apprentices Act, 1961 Apprentices are trainees under a registered contract of apprenticeship, paid a stipend, not wages; they are not workers under labour laws during training. Government support through the National Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme (trades) and National Apprenticeship Training Scheme (graduates and diploma holders) State Shops and Establishments Acts Registration, working hours, holidays, leave and conditions for shops, offices, IT companies and other commercial establishments; vary by state Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986 (amended 2016) No employment of children below 14 in any occupation (except helping a family enterprise outside school hours, without affecting education); adolescents aged 14–18 may not work in hazardous occupations and processes Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 Non-discrimination; private establishments must publish an equal-opportunity policy and provide reasonable accommodation and accessibility; 4% reservation applies to government establishments Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act, 2019 No discrimination in employment, recruitment or promotion; establishments must designate a complaint officer Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 Governs processing of employee and candidate personal data (Chapter 9)"}, {"t": "11.8 What HR must do under the Codes", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-8", "x": "In short Compliance under the Codes is a programme, not a single change: pay structure, contracts, records, committees, contractor management and state-wise tracking. Area Actions Pay structures Apply the uniform wage definition and the 50% rule; recompute PF, gratuity, bonus, overtime and leave encashment; communicate changes in take-home pay in advance EPF Implement the ₹25,000 ceiling from 17 September 2026 for newly covered employees and EPS contributions Contracts Issue appointment letters in the prescribed format to all employees, including contract and fixed-term staff; review fixed-term contracts for parity and gratuity Payroll and exits Pay wages by the 7th; complete full-and-final settlement within two working days; cap deductions at 50% Working hours Revise shift policies within 8-hour and 48-hour limits; overtime consent, 2× pay and the quarterly cap Women at night Written consent, transport, CCTV and facilities Contract labour Identify core activities; licences; monthly audit of contractors’ wages, PF and ESI; amenities; grievance committee; 2% increment for regular contract workers Gig workers (aggregators) Register workers on e-Shram via API; provision for the Social Security Fund contribution; comply with state laws Committees Grievance Redressal Committee (20+), Works Committee (100+), Internal Committee under POSH (10+), safety committees where required Records and returns Registers, wage slips and returns in the new formats, electronically where permitted State tracking Monitor state rules for every state where the organisation operates"}, {"t": "11.9 Interview questions", "c": "Chapter 11 · Labour Codes II: Wages, Social Security, OSH and POSH", "u": "book/11-labour-codes-ii-wages-social-security-osh-and-posh.html#s-11-9", "x": "In short Expect factual questions on thresholds and timelines and judgement questions on gig work, contract labour and POSH cases. What are the four Labour Codes, and what is their current status? The Code on Wages, 2019 and the Industrial Relations, Social Security, and Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Codes, 2020. They replaced 29 central laws and came into force on 21 November 2025. The central rules were notified on 8 May 2026, and states are notifying their own rules, so detailed requirements vary by state. What is the floor wage, and how does it differ from the minimum wage? The floor wage is a national base fixed by the Central Government on the basis of minimum living standards, possibly varying by region. Minimum wages are fixed by the appropriate government, mostly states, by skill level and area, and cannot be set below the floor wage. The statutory floor rate was still to be notified in 2026. How does the Social Security Code treat gig workers? Is it enough? It recognises gig and platform workers for social security for the first time, funds schemes through aggregator contributions of 1–2% of turnover capped at 5% of payments to workers, requires registration on e-Shram, and sets eligibility at 90 days with one aggregator or 120 days across several. It does not make them employees, so they have no minimum wage, PF or ESI as of right. Gaps include unsettled contribution rates and benefits and the lack of rules on algorithmic management and deactivation, which states such as Karnataka have begun to address. Can women work night shifts in a factory? Yes. Under the OSH Code women can work in all establishments, including at night and in hazardous work, with their prior written consent and with safeguards: safe transport, adequate facilities, CC"}, {"t": "12.1 How GDs are run and assessed", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-1", "x": "In short Assessors judge the quality and structure of contributions, listening and building on others, and the ability to move the group forward. Airtime alone earns little. Recall Topic, case and abstract GDs Assessment criteria Roles: initiator, contributor, facilitator, summariser Common mistakes Formats. Indian HR selection uses three main formats. Topic GDs discuss a current issue (“Should India legislate a right to disconnect?”). Case GDs give a short business or HR situation and ask the group to recommend a solution. Abstract GDs give a word or phrase (“Bridges”, “Zero”) to interpret. A GD typically has 8–12 participants and lasts 15–20 minutes, sometimes followed by a summary from one or more participants. What assessors look for. Criterion What it looks like Content Relevant points, accurate facts with sources, more than one dimension of the issue Structure Contributions organised (for example by stakeholder or time horizon), clearly linked to the topic Listening and building Referring to and extending others’ points, not repeating them Leadership and facilitation Moving the group forward: defining terms, proposing structure, bringing in quieter members, steering back to the topic Communication Clear, concise, audible, calm under interruption Balance Taking a position while acknowledging trade-offs Roles you can play well. Initiator : opens with a definition, a fact and a proposed structure; only worthwhile if you have them ready. Contributor : adds new dimensions with evidence. Facilitator : summarises progress mid-way, reconciles opposing views, invites others in. Summariser : closes with a fair account of the main arguments and areas of agreement. Common mistakes: speaking often with nothing new; long lists of statistics; unsourced numbers (“a survey says”);"}, {"t": "12.2 Preparing and structuring contributions", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-2", "x": "In short Prepare a few facts per topic and a small set of structures that work for any topic. A good contribution makes one clear point, supports it and connects to the discussion. Recall PREP: point, reason, example, point Structures: stakeholders, time horizons, PESTLE, for–against–middle path Entering and building Handling dominant speakers Summarising Structure for a single contribution (PREP): state your point , give the reason , support it with an example or fact , and restate the point in relation to the group’s discussion. Thirty to forty-five seconds is usually enough. Structures for a whole topic (useful for initiating or for adding a missing dimension): Structure Use Stakeholders Employees, employers, government, customers, society Time horizons Short-term effects versus long-term effects PESTLE Political, economic, social, technological, legal, environmental Levels Individual, organisation, economy For, against, middle path Most opinion topics on workplace policy Entering the discussion. Wait for a natural pause, address the group, and connect to the last point: “Building on Riya’s point about cost, the evidence on productivity per hour suggests…”. Short, specific contributions are easier to make in a crowded discussion. When one or two people dominate. Do not compete on volume. Use a pause to summarise the discussion so far and propose the next aspect: “We’ve covered the employer’s view in detail; can we look at the worker’s side, which we haven’t discussed?” Inviting a quiet participant by name is also noticed by assessors. Summarising. Present the main views fairly, not only your own; state where the group agreed and disagreed; offer a balanced conclusion. Keep it to about a minute."}, {"t": "12.3 Case and abstract GDs", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-3", "x": "In short Case GDs need a shared problem definition before solutions. Abstract GDs reward quick, relevant interpretations. Recall Clarify, diagnose, options, recommend, measure Agree the problem first Abstract: interpret into business and HR themes Case GDs. Use clarify → diagnose → options → recommend → measure . Clarify the facts and constraints given; diagnose root causes (most groups jump to solutions too early); generate options; recommend with a rationale and implementation steps; and say how success will be measured. Proposing this structure early helps the group and is a visible leadership contribution. Abstract GDs. Offer an interpretation quickly and connect it to business or HR. For “Bridges”: bridging generations at work; bridging the gender gap; change management as a bridge between current and future states; bridging skills gaps through reskilling. Interpretations that lead to substantive discussion count for more than clever wordplay."}, {"t": "12.4 “Longer working hours are the path to India’s growth”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-4", "x": "Recall 70-hour (2023) and 90-hour (2025) remarks EY Pune (2024) OSH Code: 48 hours/week Pencavel (2014) South Korea: 68 → 52 hours (2018) Context. In 2023, Infosys co-founder N. R. Narayana Murthy said young Indians should work 70 hours a week to raise national productivity; in January 2025, the chairman of Larsen & Toubro spoke of 90-hour weeks. The death of a 26-year-old EY employee in Pune in 2024, attributed by her family to overwork, made working hours a national issue. ILO data place India among the countries with the longest average working weeks. The OSH Code limits weekly hours to 48, with overtime at double wages and capped at 144 hours a quarter. Arguments for. Countries that caught up economically, such as Japan, South Korea and China, combined long hours with rapid growth; early careers and start-ups reward intensity; some periods (launches, crises) genuinely require extra effort. Arguments against. Output per hour falls sharply beyond about 50 hours a week, and beyond about 55 hours additional hours add little output (John Pencavel, Stanford, 2014). Long hours increase errors, accidents, burnout and health costs. They disadvantage women, who carry most unpaid care work in India. Productivity growth comes mainly from capital, technology, skills and management, not from hours. South Korea itself cut its legal maximum working week from 68 to 52 hours in 2018. Balanced position. Measure and raise output, not hours. Intensity can be voluntary and periodic, but long hours as a norm reduce productivity and exclude many people from the workforce. Useful lines. “Hours are an input; organisations should manage outputs.” “If long hours were the route to productivity, India would already be among the most productive economies.”"}, {"t": "12.5 “AI will replace HR professionals”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-5", "x": "Recall WEF 2025: +170m, −92m, net +78m by 2030 ~39% of core skills to change TCS ~12,000 roles (2025) Tasks vs jobs Context. Generative AI is automating screening, query handling, drafting and reporting. The World Economic Forum’s Future of Jobs Report 2025 estimates that by 2030 about 170 million jobs will be created and 92 million displaced globally, a net gain of 78 million, with roughly 39% of workers’ core skills expected to change. In India, TCS reduced its workforce by about 12,000 roles (around 2%) in 2025 while continuing to hire freshers, and large IT firms are redesigning entry-level work around AI. Arguments for. Much HR work is rules-based and repetitive (payroll queries, screening, documentation, reporting), and AI already does it faster and cheaper. HR headcount ratios are likely to fall. Arguments against. Judgement, trust and accountability remain human: employee relations, negotiation with unions, investigations, change leadership, ethics and coaching managers. AI also creates new HR work: reskilling programmes, AI governance, bias audits, data-protection compliance and redesigning jobs. Employees want people, not systems, in sensitive moments. Balanced position. AI will replace many HR tasks , not the HR function . The profession will shift towards analytics, design and advisory work, and HR will lead the workforce side of AI adoption across the organisation. Useful line. “The HR professional who uses AI well will replace the one who doesn’t.”"}, {"t": "12.6 “Gig workers should be treated as employees”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-6", "x": "Recall NITI Aayog: 77 lakh (2020–21) → 2.35 crore (2029–30) SS Code: 1–2% turnover, cap 5% e-Shram; 90/120 days Karnataka Act 2025; Rajasthan 2023 Uber v Aslam (UK, 2021) Context. NITI Aayog (2022) estimated 77 lakh gig workers in 2020–21, projected to reach 2.35 crore by 2029–30. The Code on Social Security recognises gig and platform workers for social security, funded by aggregator contributions of 1–2% of turnover capped at 5% of payments to workers, with registration on the e-Shram portal and eligibility after 90 days with one aggregator or 120 days across several. Rajasthan (2023) and Karnataka (2025) passed state laws; Karnataka’s includes a welfare fee and transparency provisions. In 2021 the UK Supreme Court held in Uber v Aslam that drivers were “workers” entitled to minimum wage and holiday pay. Arguments for employee status. Many workers depend economically on one platform; platforms control pricing, allocation, ratings and deactivation through algorithms, which resembles employment; without employee status workers lack minimum wages, social security and protection against arbitrary deactivation. Arguments against. Flexibility is the main reason many people choose gig work; many work across several apps part-time; employee costs would raise prices and could reduce the number of opportunities, particularly for those who find formal jobs hardest to obtain. Balanced position. A third category : portable social security funded by platforms, minimum earnings for time logged in, transparency about pay and ratings algorithms, and a fair appeal process before deactivation, without full employee status. This is broadly the direction India has taken. Useful line. “Flexibility for the platform should not mean fragility for the worker.”"}, {"t": "12.7 “India should adopt a four-day working week”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-7", "x": "Recall OSH Code: 48-hour week; states allow longer days UK pilot 2022: 61 companies, most continued Sector fit Context. The OSH Code caps the week at 48 hours, and several states allow longer working days within that limit, so compressed weeks (four days of about 12 hours including breaks) are legally possible with overtime and consent rules. A 2022 UK pilot with 61 companies, using reduced hours rather than compressed ones, found that most continued with a four-day week afterwards, reporting stable revenue and lower burnout. Arguments for. Better retention and well-being, savings on long commutes in Indian cities, and a sharper focus on output. Arguments against. Compressed 12-hour days are exhausting and raise safety risks in plants; continuous manufacturing, retail, healthcare and customer service cannot easily close for a day; IT services firms work to client hours; a reduced-hours model raises costs unless productivity rises. Balanced position. Suitable as a pilot for some knowledge-work teams with clear output measures; not a national mandate."}, {"t": "12.8 “India needs a right-to-disconnect law”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-8", "x": "Recall France 2017; Portugal 2021; Australia 2024 India: private member’s bills only Company norms Context. Laws giving employees a right to ignore work communication outside working hours exist in France (2017), Portugal (2021) and Australia (2024). In India the idea exists only in private member’s bills introduced in Parliament since 2018; it is not law. Arguments for. Hybrid work has blurred the boundary between work and home; constant availability contributes to burnout; junior employees cannot refuse after-hours requests without legal protection. Arguments against. Global teams work across time zones; a uniform law is hard to enforce and could reduce flexibility that employees value; enforcement capacity for labour laws is already stretched. Balanced position. Start with organisational norms (no expectation of replies after hours, scheduled sending, response-time agreements, rotation of on-call duties); legislate only a minimum protection that employees cannot be penalised for not responding outside working hours."}, {"t": "12.9 “Menstrual leave: progressive or regressive?”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-9", "x": "Recall Karnataka 2025: 1 day/month, 18–52 Bihar (1992) Zomato (2020) Supreme Court: policy matter; hiring-bias concern Context. Karnataka’s Menstrual Leave Policy, 2025 gives women aged 18–52 one day of paid menstrual leave a month (12 a year) in government and private establishments, without a medical certificate. Bihar has provided such leave to women government employees since 1992, and some companies, such as Zomato from 2020, introduced it voluntarily. The Supreme Court has treated the issue as a matter of policy and noted the risk that mandatory leave could discourage employers from hiring women. Arguments for. It recognises a genuine health need for many women, reduces the stigma attached to menstruation, and rest is more productive than working while unwell. Arguments against. It may increase hiring bias against women, reinforce stereotypes, and raise privacy concerns. Balanced position. Support it within a broader, gender-neutral health and flexibility policy, without requiring documentation, and monitor hiring and promotion data for bias. Funding through social insurance rather than individual employers would reduce the incentive to discriminate."}, {"t": "12.10 “Return to office: necessity or nostalgia?”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-10", "x": "Recall IT firms tightened attendance; TCS linked variable pay (2024) Learning for juniors Women’s participation Purposeful presence Context. Many Indian IT firms increased office-attendance requirements after the pandemic; TCS linked variable pay to office attendance in 2024. Global companies have moved to three to five office days. Arguments for the office. Juniors learn faster by observing colleagues; collaboration and culture are stronger in person; some security and client requirements favour office work. Arguments for flexibility. It improves retention and women’s workforce participation, widens hiring beyond large cities, and saves long commuting time and cost. Balanced position. “Purposeful presence”: anchor days for collaboration, learning and team events, with flexibility otherwise, differentiated by role; measure outcomes rather than attendance."}, {"t": "12.11 “The Labour Codes favour employers”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-11", "x": "Recall In force 21 Nov 2025; central rules 8 May 2026 Pro-flexibility provisions Pro-worker provisions Union opposition (except BMS) State rules decide outcomes Context. The four Labour Codes came into force on 21 November 2025, with final central rules notified on 8 May 2026. Most central trade unions, other than the BMS, opposed them and called nationwide protests and strikes in 2025. Arguments that they favour employers. Government permission for lay-off, retrenchment and closure now applies only at 300 or more workers (previously 100); standing orders apply at 300; fixed-term employment is statutory; strike notice applies to all establishments and mass casual leave counts as a strike; the contract-labour threshold rose to 50; many offences were decriminalised. Arguments that they protect workers. A universal minimum wage and a national floor wage; social security for gig and platform workers; gratuity for fixed-term employees after one year; a reskilling fund for retrenched workers; mandatory appointment letters; union recognition rules; safeguards for women working at night; and a uniform wage definition that raises PF and gratuity. Balanced position. The Codes trade flexibility for employers in exchange for wider coverage and clearer processes for workers. Their real effect will depend on state rules and enforcement."}, {"t": "12.12 “Moonlighting should be allowed”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-12", "x": "Recall Wipro: ~300 terminated (2022) Infosys: side work with approval Conflict of interest Contract Act s.27 Context. In 2022 Wipro terminated about 300 employees found working for competitors; Infosys allowed outside work with prior approval where it did not conflict with its business. Indian employment contracts usually include exclusivity clauses during employment; courts generally uphold reasonable restrictions during employment, while section 27 of the Indian Contract Act limits restraints after employment ends. Arguments for allowing it. Employees’ time outside work is their own; side projects build skills and income; outright bans are hard to enforce and push the activity underground. Arguments against. Conflicts of interest, misuse of confidential information, fatigue affecting performance, and divided commitment. Balanced position. Allow non-competing outside work with disclosure; prohibit work for competitors, use of company time, equipment or information, and anything that affects performance."}, {"t": "12.13 “There should be quotas for women in senior management”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-13", "x": "Recall Female LFPR ~42% (PLFS 2023–24) Companies Act: one woman director Norway: 40% board quota Targets vs quotas Pipeline fixes Context. Female labour-force participation in India has risen to about 42% (PLFS 2023–24) from about 23% in 2017–18, but women’s share falls sharply at middle and senior management levels. The Companies Act, 2013 requires at least one woman director on listed companies’ boards, and SEBI’s BRSR requires disclosure of gender data. Norway introduced a 40% quota for women on public-company boards in the 2000s. Arguments for quotas. Progress without them is slow; visible women leaders change aspirations and pipelines; quotas force organisations to fix the barriers that held women back. Arguments against. Risk of tokenism and stigma for women appointed; quotas address the outcome without fixing the pipeline; board quotas have not necessarily changed representation in senior management below the board. Balanced position. Use targets with accountability rather than rigid quotas, combined with pipeline fixes: returnship programmes, sponsorship, flexible senior roles, equal parental leave, pay-equity audits, safe travel and night-shift arrangements, and transparent promotion criteria."}, {"t": "12.14 “Trade unions have lost their relevance”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-14", "x": "Recall Strong in PSUs, banks, ports, mining, auto Samsung Sriperumbudur (2024) Gig-worker associations India hasn’t ratified ILO 87/98 Context. Union membership is low in private services but remains strong in public-sector undertakings, banks, ports, mining and the automobile industry. Recognition disputes continue in new manufacturing plants, as in the strike at Samsung’s Sriperumbudur plant in 2024. Gig-worker associations have emerged in several states. India has not ratified the ILO conventions on freedom of association and collective bargaining. Arguments that unions are less relevant. Knowledge work, individual pay negotiation and direct employee-voice channels; political affiliations that can make unions adversarial; flexible and contract work that unions struggle to organise. Arguments that they remain relevant. Power imbalances between employers and workers persist, especially for contract and gig workers; restructuring driven by AI and automation increases the need for negotiated transitions; collective voice can give management early warning of problems. Balanced position. The need for collective voice is growing; its form is changing from party-affiliated unions towards enterprise unions, worker associations and digital organising. Good industrial relations treats worker voice as useful information."}, {"t": "12.15 “Loyalty to employers is dead”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-15", "x": "Recall Attrition 17.7% (Aon, 2024) Early-career switching Reciprocal loyalty “Tours of duty”, alumni networks Context. Aon reported average attrition of 17.7% in India in 2024, and early-career employees in IT and start-ups often change jobs within two years. Arguments for. Employers ended lifetime employment first, through layoffs and restructuring; switching jobs is often the fastest way to raise pay and learn; career security now comes from skills rather than from one employer. Arguments against. Many employees stay long term where they are developed and treated fairly; organisations such as the Tata group and many public-sector companies retain people for decades. Balanced position. Loyalty has become reciprocal and conditional : people stay where they learn, grow and are treated fairly. Organisations should design for this through internal mobility, defined “tours of duty” with development goals, and alumni networks that bring people back."}, {"t": "12.16 “Salaries should be made transparent within companies”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-16", "x": "Recall EU Pay Transparency Directive (June 2026) Ranges by grade Pay-equity audits Context. The EU Pay Transparency Directive, to be implemented by June 2026, requires pay ranges in job advertisements and gender pay-gap reporting, influencing practice in multinationals. Indian law does not require pay transparency beyond listed companies’ disclosures. Arguments for. Transparency reduces unexplained gaps, particularly by gender; it builds trust; and it reduces the advantage of those who negotiate hardest. Arguments against. Privacy expectations; conflict and envy over individual cases; and pressure towards compressed pay that weakens differentiation for performance. Balanced position. Publish pay ranges by grade , explain how increments and bonuses are decided, train managers to discuss pay, and audit pay equity regularly. Full individual transparency is rarely necessary."}, {"t": "12.17 “Skills matter more than degrees”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-17", "x": "Recall Skills-based hiring Apprenticeships; PM Internship Scheme Regulated professions Context. Skills-based hiring is spreading in technology and frontline roles, and apprenticeship and internship schemes provide alternative entry routes. Degrees still act as filters for many roles. Arguments for. Degrees are a weak proxy for job performance; skills assessments widen access to talent from non-traditional backgrounds; skills change faster than degree curricula. Arguments against. Degrees signal persistence and foundational knowledge; some professions are regulated and require qualifications; skills assessments can also be biased or poorly designed. Balanced position. Treat degrees as one signal rather than a filter; use validated skills assessments, apprenticeships and work samples; keep degree requirements where they are legally or genuinely necessary."}, {"t": "12.18 “Layoffs: can they be done ethically?”", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-18", "x": "Recall Retrenchment law (300+; 15 days/year; reskilling fund) VRS Better.com Zoom layoff (2021) Communication, dignity, support Context. Restructuring in technology and start-ups has made layoffs a frequent topic. In Indian law, retrenchment of workers requires notice, compensation of 15 days’ pay per year of service and a reskilling-fund payment, with government permission for establishments of 300 or more workers (Chapter 10). A widely criticised example abroad was Better.com’s dismissal of around 900 employees on a single video call in December 2021. Arguments that layoffs can be ethical. Organisations sometimes must reduce costs to survive and protect the remaining jobs; a fair process can preserve dignity and trust. Arguments that they rarely are. Layoffs often follow over-hiring decisions by leaders who are not affected; they damage engagement among survivors; and they are often announced impersonally. Balanced position. Treat layoffs as a last resort after redeployment, attrition and voluntary schemes; use fair, transparent selection criteria; communicate personally and respectfully; provide severance beyond the legal minimum, extended health cover and outplacement support; and look after the remaining employees."}, {"t": "12.19 Case GD practice prompts", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-19", "x": "In short Practise each in 15 minutes with clarify, diagnose, options, recommend, measure. Case Points to raise A cement plant in a remote location loses 30% of young engineers within 18 months Segment exits; career paths and rotation to other plants; housing and family facilities; pay compa-ratios; mentoring; early-attrition tracking An IT firm must reduce its workforce by 5% Alternatives first; fair criteria; legal requirements; personal communication; severance and outplacement; support for remaining staff A union demands a 30% wage increase over three years; the company can afford 18% Wage benchmarks; ability to pay; productivity-linked trades; settlement duration; communication; contingency planning A social-media post alleges the Internal Committee ignored a sexual-harassment complaint Immediate review of the case; IC composition and independence; confidentiality; communication; protection from retaliation; audit of past cases Delivery riders in Bengaluru protest a cut in per-order pay Platform-worker law (Social Security Code, Karnataka Act); transparency of incentive changes; dialogue with worker associations; earnings floor; communication Two banks merge with different seniority lists and pay scales Job mapping and architecture; harmonisation over several cycles; protecting existing pay; union negotiation; culture integration"}, {"t": "12.20 Facts to quote", "c": "Chapter 12 · Group Discussions: India HR Topics", "u": "book/12-gd-prep-india-hr-topics.html#s-12-20", "x": "In short Quote sources by name. Re-check time-sensitive facts shortly before your GDs. Fact Source Labour Codes in force from 21 November 2025; final central rules 8 May 2026 Press Information Bureau; Ministry of Labour notifications EPF wage ceiling raised from ₹15,000 to ₹25,000 a month from 17 September 2026 EPFO notification S.O. 5109(E) Average salary increase 8.9% in 2025, 9.1% projected for 2026; attrition 17.7% in 2024 Aon, Salary Increase and Turnover Survey, India (February 2026) 21% of employees engaged worldwide Gallup, State of the Global Workplace 2025 Gig workers: 77 lakh (2020–21), projected 2.35 crore (2029–30) NITI Aayog (2022) Jobs by 2030: 170 million created, 92 million displaced, net 78 million World Economic Forum, Future of Jobs Report 2025 Female labour-force participation about 42% (2023–24), up from about 23% (2017–18) Periodic Labour Force Survey, MoSPI TCS reduced about 12,000 roles (about 2%) in 2025 Company statement and press reports DPDP Rules notified 13 November 2025; most duties apply from about May 2027 Ministry of Electronics and IT Output per hour falls sharply beyond about 50 hours a week Pencavel (2014), Stanford University Sources as listed in the table and in each brief, including Pencavel (2014), \"The Productivity of Working Hours\", Economic Journal ; Karnataka Menstrual Leave Policy (2025); PRS Legislative Research on state gig-worker laws; Uber BV v Aslam [2021] UKSC 5; Autonomy and 4 Day Week Global, UK pilot results (2023); Directive (EU) 2023/970. Re-check figures on layoffs, policies and state laws close to your GD dates."}];